32f7a6dbc2","sdu-ecf1fb45-20c2-4d9f-9cf4-ef9f378aef79"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Onsite vasovagal reactions with and without loss of consciousness are distinct outcomes with different risk factors","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-093.33-822351","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"AIMS/HYPOTHESIS: A better understanding of the mechanisms underlying an elevated infection risk in individuals with type 2 diabetes is needed to guide risk stratification and prevention. We investigated the risk of infection in subgroups of individuals with type 2 diabetes according to indices of insulin sensitivity and beta cell function. METHODS: We classified 7265 individuals with recently diagnosed type 2 diabetes (median duration 1.4 years, IQR 0.5-2.9 years) into hyperinsulinaemic (high beta cell function [HOMA 2-beta-cell function, HOMA2-B], low insulin sensitivity [HOMA 2-insulin sensitivity, HOMA2-S]), classical (low HOMA2-B, low HOMA2-S) and insulinopenic (low HOMA2-B, high HOMA2-S) type 2 diabetes. Individuals were followed until first hospital-treated infection or first prescription for an anti-infective agent (community-treated infection). We used Cox regression analysis to estimate HRs adjusted for age, sex, index year, diabetes duration and treatment, lifestyle behaviours and comorbidities. RESULTS: Among study participants, 28% had hyperinsulinaemic, 63% had classical and 9% had insulinopenic type 2 diabetes. The 10 year risks of hospital-treated infections were 42.3%, 36.8% and 31.0% in the three subgroups, respectively. Compared with the insulinopenic subgroup, adjusted HRs for hospital-treated infections were elevated for hyperinsulinaemic (1.38 [95% CI 1.16, 1.65]) and classical type 2 diabetes (1.20 [95% CI 1.02, 1.42]). The 10 year risks of community-treated infections were high in all three subgroups at 91.6%, 90.1% and 88.3%, respectively, corresponding to adjusted HRs of 1.20 (95% CI 1.08, 1.33) for the hyperinsulinaemic and 1.10 (95% CI 1.00, 1.21) for the classical subgroup. Infection risk in the hyperinsulinaemic subgroup decreased substantially when further adjusted for abdominal obesity, metabolic derangements and low-grade inflammation. CONCLUSIONS/INTERPRETATION: The risk of severe infections is clearly elevated in individuals with type 2 diabetes characterised by a higher degree of insulin resistance/hyperinsulinaemia.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Kristensen, Frederik P. B.","Domazet, Sidsel L.","Nielsen, Jens S.","Stidsen, Jacob V.","Højlund, Kurt","Beck-Nielsen, Henning","Vestergaard, Peter","Jessen, Niels","Olsen, Michael H.","Hansen, Torben","Brøns, Charlotte","Vaag, Allan","Sørensen, Henrik T.","Thomsen, Reimar W."],"cuid":["S04-098.29-803709"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Domazet, Sidsel L.","Domazet, Sidsel Louise"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Lund University"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Clinical diabetes","Cohort Studies","Complications all","Denmark epidemiology","Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 epidemiology","Epidemiology","Female","Human","Humans","Hyperinsulinism epidemiology","Infections epidemiology","Insulin Resistance physiology","Insulin Secreting Cells metabolism","Insulin sensitivity and resistance","Male","Middle Aged","Risk Factors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-STENO"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-STENO","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"elevated risk of infection in individuals with hyperinsulinaemic type 000000000002 diabetes a danish 000000000012 year cohort study","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85212090806","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgem