{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: Research suggests that the prevalence of depressive disorder is increasing globally in the general population, but it is unclear if this holds true for the working population. We analysed e trends in the prevalence of depressive disorder in four nationwide, weighted samples of the Danish working population from 2012 to 2018. METHODS: We used data from the Work Environment and Health in Denmark study, which is a series of nationwide cross-sectional surveys on working conditions and health conducted in 2012, 2014, 2016 and 2018 (N = 76,770). We used a Major Depression Inventory score ≥ 21 to indicate a depressive disorder and further ICD-10 and DSM-IV algorithms in supplementary analyses. We calculated weighted prevalence, change in prevalence, and estimated average Annual Percent Change (APC). RESULTS: The point-prevalence of depressive disorder was 7.2 % (95 % CI: 7.2; 7.3) in the 2012-survey and 11.2 % (11.1; 11.2) in 2018, yielding a total relative increase in prevalence over the six-year period of 54.0 % (53.6; 55.8) and an APC of 6.7 (95 % CI: 2.3; 11.4). We observed the higher prevalence in both men and women, in all age and educational groups, and in different occupational positions. Women, young individuals, and individuals of lower socioeconomic position had a higher prevalence of depressive disorder that persisted in all waves. Analyses using the ICD-10 or DSM-IV definitions of depressive disorder showed lower prevalence but similar trends over time. CONCLUSION: The results suggest that the general population trend towards an increase in depressive disorder also applies to the working population in Denmark.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Dalsager, Louise","Sørensen, Kathrine","Sørensen, Jeppe Karl","Burr, Hermann","Dragano, Nico","Hanson, Linda Magnusson","Nielsen, Maj Britt Dahl","Rod, Naja Hulvej","Wahrendorf, Morten","Madsen, Ida E H","Rugulies, Reiner"],"cuid":["S03-095.53-814109"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Dalsager, Louise"],"country":["Germany","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Federal Institute for Occupational Safety and Health","Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf","Stockholm University","University Hospital of Düsseldorf","University of Düsseldorf, Düsseldorf, Germany."],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Anxiety disorders","Cross Sectional Studies","Denmark epidemiology","Depressive Disorder Major epidemiology","Depressive Disorder epidemiology","Female","Health","Humans","Inventory","Major depression","Male","Middle Aged","Prevalence","Validity","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"time trends in prevalence of depressive disorder in the danish working population from 000000002012 to 000000002018","src":["ku","nfa","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jad.2025.119449","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-095.53-814109","identifier":[{"id":"105005490168","type":"scopus"},{"id":"001502013900003","type":"wos"},{"id":"40398605","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-e55aab30-7d3a-4659-9d1e-7ab967bfa9aa","nfa-dee3b24f-0e02-4a64-aa70-bff527a0a589","sdu-5c1699e6-09ab-47c2-b64b-f927499999c6"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Anxiety disorders","Cross-Sectional Studies","Denmark/epidemiology","Depressive Disorder, Major/epidemiology","Depressive Disorder/epidemiology","Female","Health","Humans","Inventory","Major depression","Male","Middle Aged","Prevalence","Validity","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Sci