rc":["KU University of Copenhagen","NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Time trends in prevalence of depressive disorder in the Danish working population from 2012 to 2018","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-095.53-814109","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: Teaching of dental caries diagnostics is an essential part of dental education. Diagnosing proximal caries is a challenging task, and automated systems applying artificial intelligence (AI) have been introduced to assist in this respect. Thus, the implementation of AI for teaching purposes may be considered. The aim of this study was to assess the impact of an AI software on students’ ability to detect enamel-only proximal caries in bitewing radiographs (BWs) and to assess whether proximal tooth overlap interferes with caries detection. Materials and Methods: The study included 74 dental students randomly allocated to either a test or control group. At two sessions, both groups assessed proximal enamel caries in BWs. At the first session, the test group registered caries in 25 BWs using AI software (AssistDent®) and the control group without using AI. One month later, both groups detected caries in another 25 BWs in a clinical setup without using the software. The student's registrations were compared with a reference standard. Positive agreement (caries) and negative agreement (no caries) were calculated, and t-tests were applied to assess whether the test and control groups performed differently. Moreover, t-tests were applied to test whether proximal overlap interfered with caries registration. Results: At the first and second sessions, 56 and 52 tooth surfaces, respectively, were detected with enamel-only caries according to the reference standard. At session 1, no significant difference between the control (48%) and the test (42%) group was found for positive agreement (p =.08), whereas the negative agreement was higher for the test group (86% vs. 80%; p =.02). At session 2, there was no significant difference between the groups. The test group improved for positive agreement from session 1 to session 2 (p <.001), while the control group improved for negative agreement (p <.001). Thirty-eight per cent of the tooth surfaces overlapped, and the mean positive agreement and negative agreement were significantly lower for overlapping surfaces than non-overlapping surfaces (p <.001) in both groups. Conclusion: Training with the AI software did not impact on dental students’ ability to detect proximal enamel caries in bitewing radiographs although the positive agreement improved over time. It was revealed that proximal tooth overlap interfered with caries detection.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Schropp, Lars","Sørensen, Anders Peter Sejersdal","Devlin, Hugh","Matzen, Louise Hauge"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-699213"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Manchester"],"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Caries","Education","Radiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-OTH","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"use of artificial intelligence software in dental education a study on assisted proximal caries assessment in bitewing radiographs","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/eje.12973","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-699213","identifier":[{"id":"37961027","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85176922779","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0a5c66a5-077e-410b-9b68-cd5c65a0b002"],"keyword":["artificial intelligence","caries","education","radiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A6AE05B36682FDDAADEE93D9A2EB7F76"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/eje.12973","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health - Section for Oral Radiology and Endodontics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other hospitals","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-OTH","l3nl":"Other Hospitals","l3nn":"Other Hospitals","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Hospitals","namevar":["Private Practice - Dental - Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-OTH","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of manchester","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Manchester","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"schropppau","last":"Schropp","locid":"3ae0d2fd-2662-499f-8d5e-5246156d138f","name":"Schropp, Lars","orcid":"0000-0003-4897-1554","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Peter Sejersdal","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Anders Peter Sejersdal","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Hugh","key":"devlinpau","last":"Devlin","name":"Devlin, Hugh","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louise Hauge","key":"matzenpau","last":"Matzen","locid":"6d2056e0-6343-4b7e-94d0-f3c63475edd4","name":"Matzen, Louise Hauge","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37961027"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/eje.12973","issn":["13965883","13995863"],"issn_org":["13965883"],"issue":"2","journal":"European Journal of Dental Education","pages":"490-496","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell Publishing Ltd","status":"p","title":"European Journal of Dental Education","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85176922779"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Hospitals","Universities","private practice - dental - denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-0a5c66a5-077e-410b-9b68-cd5c65a0b002"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Use of artificial intelligence software in dental education: : A study on assisted proximal caries assessment in bitewing radiographs","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-699213","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Pubertal growth patterns correlate with future health outcomes. However, the genetic mechanisms mediating growth trajectories remain largely unknown. Here, we modeled longitudinal height growth with Super-Imposition by Translation And Rotation (SITAR) growth curve analysis on ~ 56,000 trans-ancestry samples with repeated height measurements from age 5 years to adulthood. We performed genetic analysis on six phenotypes representing the magnitude, timing, and intensity of the pubertal growth spurt. To investigate the lifelong impact of genetic variants associated with pubertal growth trajectories, we performed genetic correlation analyses and phenome-wide association studies in the Penn Medicine BioBank and the UK Biobank. RESULTS: Large-scale growth modeling enables an unprecedented view of adolescent growth across contemporary and 20th-century pediatric cohorts. We identify 26 genome-wide significant loci and leverage trans-ancestry data to perform fine-mapping. Our data reveals genetic relationships between pediatric height growth and health across the life course, with different growth trajectories correlated with different outcomes. For instance, a faster tempo of pubertal growth correlates with higher bone mineral density, HOMA-IR, fasting insulin, type 2 diabetes, and lung cancer, whereas being taller at early puberty, taller across puberty, and having quicker pubertal growth were associated with higher risk for atrial fibrillation. CONCLUSION: We report novel genetic associations with the tempo of pubertal growth and find that genetic determinants of growth are correlated with reproductive, glycemic, respiratory, and cardiac traits in adulthood. These results aid in identifying specific growth trajectories impacting lifelong health and show that there may not be a single \"optimal\" pubertal growth pattern.","author_count":52,"contributors":["Bradfield, Jonathan P","Kember, Rachel L","Ulrich, Anna","Balkiyarova, Zhanna","Alyass, Akram","Aris, Izzuddin M","Bell, Joshua A","Broadaway, K Alaine","Chen, Zhanghua","Chai, Jin-Fang","Davies, Neil M","Fernandez-Orth, Dietmar","Bustamante, Mariona","Fore, Ruby","Ganguli, Amitavo","Heiskala, Anni","Hottenga, Jouke-Jan","Íñiguez, Carmen","Kobes, Sayuko","Leinonen, Jaakko","Lowry, Estelle","Lyytikainen, Leo-Pekka","Mahajan, Anubha","Pitkänen, Niina","Schnurr, Theresia Maria","Have, Christian Theil","Strachan, David P","Thiering, Elisabeth","Vogelezang, Suzanne","Wade, Kaitlin H","Wang, Carol A","Wong, Andrew","Holm, Louise Aas","Chesi, Alessandra","Choong, Catherine","Cruz, Miguel","Elliott, Paul","Franks, Steve","Frithioff-Bøjsøe, Christine","Gauderman, W James","Glessner, Joseph T","Gilsanz, Vicente","Griesman, Kendra","Hanson, Robert L","Kaakinen, Marika","Kalkwarf, Heidi","Kelly, Andrea","Hansen, Torben","Holm, Jens-Christian","Sørensen, Thorkild I A","Grant, Struan F A","Cousminer, Diana L"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-719944"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, Central","America, North","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corpau":["Early Growth Genetics Consortium"],"country":["Australia","Canada","Finland","Germany","Mexico","Netherlands","Singapore","Spain","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Children's Hospital Los Angeles","Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia","Erasmus University Rotterdam","Harvard University","Haverford College","Helmholtz Zentrum München - German Research Center for Environmental Health","Helsinki University Hospital","Hospital Clínic de Barcelona","Hospital de Especialidades","Imperial College London","McMaster University","National University of Singapore","Tampere University","University College London","University of Bristol","University of California at Irvine","University of Cincinnati","University of Newcastle","University of North Carolina at Charlotte","University 