year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Kapitlet omhandler sanseintegration og problemer med en adækvat sanseintegration i forbindelse med psykisk sygdom. Kapitlet indledes med en gennemgang af sanserne og vores evne til at integrere sansestimuli. Derpå følger et afsnit om behandlingsmetoder, som kan afhjælpe sansemæssige problemer og fremme potentialet for at bedre den generelle trivsel og opnå forbedret evne til at leve et meningsfuldt hverdagsliv.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Sørensen, Kirsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-792354"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Sygdom sundhedsvidenskab og sygepleje","Uddannelse professioner og erhverv"],"level":"edu","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-UC"],"level3":{"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCL"]},"level3all":["DI-UC-UCL"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sanseintegration i relation til psykisk sygdom","src":["ucl"],"src-code":["DI-UC-UCL"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-792354","ids":["ucl-630579df-510a-4b82-b576-b40a228419cf"],"keyword":["Sygdom, sundhedsvidenskab og sygepleje","Uddannelse, professioner og erhverv"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Educational","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-39515C26126D33CA56EF7D3B353F71FA"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ucl university college","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCL","l3nl":"UCL University College","l3nn":"UCL University College","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCL University College","namevar":["UCL Erhvervsakademi og Professionshøjskole, Direktion, Sundhed, Ergoterapeut og Sygeplejerske i Odense"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCL","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kirsten","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"748f6f8e-bd0c-45bb-a2fc-750b16dff8b1","name":"Sørensen, Kirsten","orcid":"0000-0002-0717-8448","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Frederik Alkier","key":"gildbergpedh","last":"Gildberg","name":"Gildberg, Frederik Alkier","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kristian Alexander Jul","key":"paaskepedh","last":"Paaske","name":"Paaske, Kristian Alexander Jul","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Louise","key":"ahrendtpedh","last":"Ahrendt","name":"Ahrendt, Louise","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ellen Boldrup","key":"tingleffpedh","last":"Tingleff","name":"Tingleff, Ellen Boldrup","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"edition":"3.","eisbn":["9788776250287"],"isbn":["9788776250287"],"pages":"398-406","place":"København","publisher":"FADL's Forlag","status":"p","title":"Grundbog i klinisk psykiatrisk sygepleje"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","UCL University College","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ucl-630579df-510a-4b82-b576-b40a228419cf"],"src":["UCL University College"],"title":"Sanseintegration i relation til psykisk sygdom","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-792354","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Portal hypertension has cerebral consequences via its causes and complications, namely hepatic encephalopathy (HE), a common and devastating brain disturbance caused by liver insufficiency and portosystemic shunting. The pathogenesis involves hyperammonemia and systemic inflammation. Symptoms are disturbed personality and reduced attention. HE is minimal or grades I to IV (coma). Bouts of HE are episodic and often recurrent. Initial treatment is of events that precipitated the episode and exclusion of nonhepatic causes. Specific anti-HE treatment is lactulose. By recurrence, rifaximin is add-on. Anti-HE treatment is efficacious also for prophylaxis, but emergence of HE marks advanced liver disease and a dismal prognosis.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Thomsen, Karen Louise","Sørensen, Michael","Kjærgaard, Kristoffer","Eriksen, Peter Lykke","Lauridsen, Mette Munk","Vilstrup, Hendrik"],"cuid":["S02-095.92-751714"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Thomsen, Karen Louise"],"keyword":["Cirrhosis","Gastrointestinal Agents therapeutic use","Hepatic Encephalopathy","Hepatic Encephalopathy etiology","Humans","Hyperammonemia","Hypertension Portal etiology","Inflammation","Lactulose therapeutic use","Portal hypertension","Rifaximin therapeutic use"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cerebral aspects of portal hypertension hepatic encephalopathy","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/264889059/Manuscript_revised.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-095.92-751714","identifier":[{"id":"85191295411","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38945642","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-5796d4a8-59a7-4802-9c57-e7a85e91d408","sdu-1665a88d-430e-43a3-bd91-c43d5dab7f4b"],"keyword":["Cirrhosis","Gastrointestinal Agents/therapeutic use","Hepatic Encephalopathy/etiology","Hepatic encephalopathy","Humans","Hyperammonemia","Hyperammonemia/etiology","Hypertension, Portal/etiology","Inflammation","Lactulose/therapeutic use","Portal hypertension","Rifaximin/therapeutic use"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1C76F7192B06D3D1EC4E1A32926A3FF8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/264889059/Manuscript_revised.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Hepatology and Gastroenterology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Esbjerg and Grindsted Hospital, Research Unit of Medical gastroenterology-hepatology (Esbjerg)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen Louise","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"32baa31f-0b93-4c6d-af7e-0da0519f574f","name":"Thomsen, Karen Louise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Michael","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"52635821-2c0d-418e-848d-e3e1f1175e12","name":"Sørensen, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-8750-4329","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kristoffer","key":"kjærgaardpau","last":"Kjærgaard","locid":"35ec4dba-1425-425c-9860-460c3989eba7","name":"Kjærgaard, Kristoffer","orcid":"0000-0002-6440-2784","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter Lykke","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","locid":"02b5f3f7-8692-403b-9a17-1dc588d638e7","name":"Eriksen, Peter Lykke","orcid":"0000-0003-1115-8308","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette Munk","key":"lauridsenpau","last":"Lauridsen","locid":"47ea3fdf-66e2-4eb3-ade8-aaf22d015ee9","name":"Lauridsen, Mette Munk","orcid":"0000-0003-0586-8549","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hendrik","key":"vilstruppau","last":"Vilstrup","locid":"9e1905f3-3094-4aea-bbde-7178733bf6cb","name":"Vilstrup, Hendrik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38945642"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.cld.2024.03.008","issn":["10893261","15578224"],"issn_org":["10893261"],"issue":"3","journal":"Clinics in Liver Disease","pages":"541-554","publisher":"WB  Saunders","publisher_org":"W.B. Saunders","status":"p","title":"Clinics in Liver Disease","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85191295411"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-5796d4a8-59a7-4802-9c57-e7a85e91d408","sdu-1665a88d-430e-43a3-bd91-c43d5dab7f4b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Cerebral Aspects of Portal Hypertension : Hepatic Encephalopathy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.92-751714","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Major migration events in Holocene Eurasia have been characterized genetically at broad regional scales1–4. However, insights into the population dynamics in the contact zones are hampered by a lack of ancient genomic data sampled at high spatiotemporal resolution5–7. Here, to address this, we analysed shotgun-sequenced genomes from 100 skeletons spanning 7,300 years of the Mesolithic period, Neolithic period and Early Bronze Age in Denmark and integrated these with proxies for diet (13C and 15N content), mobility (87Sr/86Sr ratio) and vegetation cover (pollen). We observe that Danish Mesolithic individuals of the Maglemose, Kongemose and Ertebølle cultures form a distinct genetic cluster related to other Western European hunter-gatherers. Despite shifts in material culture they displayed genetic homogeneity from around 10,500 to 5,900 calibrated years before present, when Neolithic farmers with Anatolian-derived ancestry arrived. Although the Neolithic transition was delayed by more than a millennium relative to Central Europe, it was very abrupt and resulted in a population turnover with limited genetic contribution from local hunter-gatherers. The succeeding Neolithic population, associated with the Funnel Beaker culture, persisted for only about 1,000 years before immigrants with eastern Steppe-derived ancestry arrived. This second and equally rapid population replacement gave rise to the Single Grave culture with an ancestry profile more similar to present-day Danes. In our multiproxy dataset, these major demographic events are manifested as parallel shifts in genotype, phenotype, diet and land use.","author_count":77,"contributors":["Allentoft, Morten E.","Sikora, Martin","Fischer, Anders","Sjögren, Karl Göran","Ingason, Andrés","Macleod, Ruairidh","Rosengren, Anders","Schulz Paulsson, Bettina","Jørkov, Marie Louise Schjellerup","Novosolov, Maria","Stenderup, Jesper","Price, T. Douglas","Fischer Mortensen, Morten","Nielsen, Anne Birgitte","Ulfeldt Hede, Mikkel","Sørensen, Lasse","Nielsen, Poul Otto","Rasmussen, Peter","Jensen, Theis Zetner Trolle","Refoyo-Martínez, Alba","Irving-Pease, Evan K.","Barrie, William","Pearson, Alice","Sousa da Mota, Bárbara","Demeter, Fabrice","Henriksen, Rasmus A.","Vimala, Tharsika","McColl, Hugh","Vaughn, Andrew","Vinner, Lasse","Renaud, Gabriel","Stern, Aaron","Johannsen, Niels Nørkjær","Ramsøe, Abigail Daisy","Schork, Andrew Joseph","Ruter, Anthony","Gotfredsen, Anne Birgitte","Henning Nielsen, Bjarne","Brinch Petersen, Erik","Kannegaard, Esben","Hansen, Jesper","Buck Pedersen, Kristoffer","Pedersen, Lisbeth","Klassen, Lutz","Meldgaard, Morten","Johansen, Morten","Uldum, Otto Christian","Lotz, Per","Lysdahl, Per","Bangsgaard, Pernille","Petersen, Peter Vang","Maring, Rikke","Iversen, Rune","Wåhlin, Sidsel","Anker Sørensen, Søren","Andersen, Søren H.","Jørgensen, Thomas","Lynnerup, Niels","Lawson, Daniel J.","Rasmussen, Simon","Korneliussen, Thorfinn Sand","Kjær, Kurt H.","Durbin, Richard","Nielsen, Rasmus","Delaneau, Olivier","Werge, Thomas","Kristiansen, Kristian","Willerslev, Eske"],"cuid":["S04-095.15-793206"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corpau":["100 ancient"],"corresponding_author":["Allentoft, Morten E.","Sikora, Martin","Willerslev, Eske"],"country":["Australia","France","Germany","Greenland","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Curtin University","Department of Genetics, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, UK","Department of Historical Studies, University of Gothenburg, SE, 41255 Gothenburg, Sweden","French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS)","GeoGenetics Group, Department of Zoology, University of Cambridge","Kiel University","Lund University","MARUM Center for Marine Environmental Sciences and Faculty of Geosciences, University of Bremen","Musée de l’Homme, Paris","National Natural History Museum","Paris-Cité University","Trace and Environmental DNA (TrEnD) Laboratory, School of Molecular and Life Sciences, Curtin University, Perth, Western Australia, Australia","Translational Genomics Research Institute","University College London","University of Bremen","University of Bristol","University of California at Berkeley","University of Cambridge","University of Gothenburg","University of Greenland","University of Lausanne","University of Wisconsin-Madison","Université de Paris","Wellcome Sanger Institute","Wellcome Sanger Institute, Wellcome Trust Genome Campus"],"keyword":["Culture","Datasets as Topic","Denmark ethnology","Genome Human genetics","Genomics","Genotype","History Ancient","Human Migration history","Humans","Phenotype","Pollen"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-KUM"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"OT":["Lundbeck Foundation GeoGenetics Centre, GLOBE Instute, University of Copenhagen"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-KUM","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-NP-LUND","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"000000000100 ancient genomes show repeated population turnovers in neolithic denmark","src":["au","dtu","ku","kum"],"src-code":["DI-GV-KUM","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06862-3","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The Lundbeck Foundation GeoGenetics Centre is supported by grants from the Lundbeck Foundation (R302-2018-2155, R155-2013-16338), the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF18SA0035006), the Wellcome Trust (WT214300), Carlsberg Foundation (CF18-0024), the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF94, DNRF174), the University of Copenhagen (KU2016 programme) and Ferring Pharmaceuticals A/S, to E.W. This research has been conducted using the UK Biobank Resource and the iPSYCH Initiative, funded by the Lundbeck Foundation (R102-A9118 and R155-2014-1724). This work was further supported by the Swedish Foundation for Humanities and Social Sciences grant (Riksbankens Jubileumsfond M16-0455:1) to K.K. M.E.A. was supported by Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions of the EU (grant no. 300554), The Villum Foundation (grant no. 10120) and Independent Research Fund Denmark (grant no. 7027-00147B). A.F. was funded by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG, German Research Foundation) under Germany´s Excellence Strategy–EXC 2150–390870439. R. Macleod was supported by an SSHRC doctoral studentship grant (G101449: ‘Individual Life Histories in Long-Term Cultural Change’). B.S.P. has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under the ERC-StG grant agreement No 94924. M.N. is funded by the Human Frontier Science Program Postdoctoral Fellowship LT000143/2019-L4. A.R.-M. was supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (grant R302-2018-2155) and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant NNF18SA0035006); E.K.I.-P. was supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (grant R302-2018-2155) and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant NNF18SA0035006). W.B. is supported by the Hanne and Torkel Weis-Fogh Fund (Department of Zoology, University of Cambridge). A.P. is funded by Wellcome grant WT214300. B.S.d.M. and O.D. are supported by the Swiss National Science Foundation (SFNS PP00P3_176977) and European Research Council (ERC 679330). G.R. is supported by a Novo Nordisk Foundation Fellowship (gNNF20OC0062491). N.N.J. is supported by Aarhus University Research Foundation. A.J.S. is supported by a Lundbeckfonden Fellowship (R335-2019-2318) and the National Institute on Aging (NIH award numbers U19AG023122, U24AG051129 and UH2AG064706). R. Maring was funded by the Aarhus University Research Foundation through a grant awarded to M. A. Mannino for the project titled Danish and European Diets in Time (AUFF-E-2015-FLS-8-2). S.R. was funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF14CC0001). T.S.K. is funded by Carlsberg grant CF19-0712. R.D. is funded by the Wellcome Trust (WT214300). R.N. is funded by the National Institute of General Medical Sciences (NIH grant R01GM138634). T.W. is supported by the Lundbeck Foundation iPSYCH initiative (R248-2017-2003). We are indebted to P. Bennike for her contribution during the formative years of the project until shortly before her death in 2017. We acknowledge staff at the National Museum, the Anthropological Laboratory, and the regional museums in Denmark, responsible for collecting, recording and curating prehistoric skeletal remains studied herein. The process of identifying and sampling suitable archaeological remains for the current study was dependent on numerous specialists in Danish archaeology including researchers, museum employees and citizen scientists. We thank the following in this regard (alphabetically ordered): A. H. Andersen, S. Bergerbrant, K. Christensen, K. M. Gregersen, V. Grimes, E. Johansen, O. T. Kastholm, T. Lotz, E. Lundberg, M. Mannino, J. Olsen, K. Rosenlund and H. H. Sørensen. We also acknowledge those who have assisted in the sampling process or the gathering of provenance data on prehistoric human remains which were not analysed here owing to insufficient DNA preservation. These include H. Dahl (Tybrind Vig), I. B. Enghoff (Østenkær), A. B. Gurlev (Vedbæk Havn), L. Holten (Aldersro), O. Lass (Hesselbjerg/Ferle Enge and Nivå), L. Matthes (Knudsgrund/Knudshoved) and K. Randsborg (deceased) (Nivå). We are grateful for contributions from F. Racimo, and express our gratitude to the many researchers who have supported laboratory work, analytical procedures and evaluation of results that are presented here, including M. Mannino, P. Reimer and M. Thompson. E.W. thanks St. John’s College, Cambridge, for providing a stimulating environment of discussion and learning. The Lundbeck Foundation GeoGenetics Centre is supported by grants from the Lundbeck Foundation (R302-2018-2155, R155-2013-16338), the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF18SA0035006), the Wellcome Trust (WT214300), Carlsberg Foundation (CF18-0024), the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF94, DNRF174), the University of Copenhagen (KU2016 programme) and Ferring Pharmaceuticals A/S, to E.W. This research has been conducted using the UK Biobank Resource and the iPSYCH Initiative, funded by the Lundbeck Foundation (R102-A9118 and R155-2014-1724). This work was further supported by the Swedish Foundation for Humanities and Social Sciences grant (Riksbankens Jubileumsfond M16-0455:1) to K.K. M.E.A. was supported by Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions of the EU (grant no. 300554), The Villum Foundation (grant no. 10120) and Independent Research Fund Denmark (grant no. 7027-00147B). A.F. was funded by the Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft (DFG, German Research Foundation) under Germany´s Excellence Strategy–EXC 2150–390870439. R. Macleod was supported by an SSHRC doctoral studentship grant (G101449: ‘Individual Life Histories in Long-Term Cultural Change’). B.S.P. has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under the ERC-StG grant agreement No 94924. M.N. is funded by the Human Frontier Science Program Postdoctoral Fellowship LT000143/2019-L4. A.R.-M. was supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (grant R302-2018-2155) and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant NNF18SA0035006); E.K.I.-P. was supported by the Lundbeck Foundation (grant R302-2018-2155) and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant NNF18SA0035006). W.B. is supported by the Hanne and Torkel Weis-Fogh Fund (Department of Zoology, University of Cambridge). A.P. is funded by Wellcome grant WT214300. B.S.d.M. and O.D. are supported by the Swiss National Science Foundation (SFNS PP00P3_176977) and European Research Council (ERC 679330). G.R. is supported by a Novo Nordisk Foundation Fellowship (gNNF20OC0062491). N.N.J. is supported by Aarhus University Research Foundation. A.J.S. is supported by a Lundbeckfonden Fellowship (R335-2019-2318) and the National Institute on Aging (NIH award numbers U19AG023122, U24AG051129 and UH2AG064706). R. Maring was funded by the Aarhus University Research Foundation through a grant awarded to M. A. Mannino for the project titled Danish and European Diets in Time (AUFF-E-2015-FLS-8-2). S.R. was funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF14CC0001). T.S.K. is funded by Carlsberg grant CF19-0712. R.D. is funded by the Wellcome Trust (WT214300). R.N. is funded by the National Institute of General Medical Sciences (NIH grant R01GM138634). T.W. is supported by the Lundbeck Foundation iPSYCH initiative (R248-2017-2003). We are indebted to P. Bennike for her contribution during the formative years of the project until shortly before her death in 2017. We acknowledge staff at the National Museum, the Anthropological Laboratory, and the regional museums in Denmark, responsible for collecting, recording and curating prehistoric skeletal remains studied herein. The process of identifying and sampling suitable archaeological remains for the current study was dependent on numerous specialists in Danish archaeology including researchers, museum employees and citizen scientists. We thank the following in this regard (alphabetically ordered): A. H. Andersen, S. Bergerbrant, K. Christensen, K. M. Gregersen, V. Grimes, E. Johansen, O. T. Kastholm, T. Lotz, E. Lundberg, M. Mannino, J. Olsen, K. Rosenlund and H. H. Sørensen. We also acknowledge those who have assisted in the sampling process or the gathering of provenance data on prehistoric human remains which were not analysed here owing to insufficient DNA preservation. These include H. Dahl (Tybrind Vig), I. B. Enghoff (Østenkær), A. B. Gurlev (Vedbæk Havn), L. Holten (Aldersro), O. Lass (Hesselbjerg/Ferle Enge and Nivå), L. Matthes (Knudsgrund/Knudshoved) and K. Randsborg (deceased) (Nivå). We are grateful for contributions from F. Racimo, and express our gratitude to the many researchers who have supported laboratory work, analytical procedures and evaluation of results that are presented here, including M. Mannino, P. Reimer and M. Thompson. E.W. thanks St. John’s College, Cambridge, for providing a stimulating environment of discussion and learning."],"id":"S04-095.15-793206","identifier":[{"id":"85181852344","type":"scopus"},{"id":"001143579000013","type":"wos"},{"id":"38200294","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10781617","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-36e20316-953f-4b7e-8bf8-7d9b68312937","dtu-51f0d451-d77c-4b97-a324-cf22ebf8cf7f","ku-a31b4a17-dfc3-4b86-bc27-997244bc73ab","kum-f9506713-be49-427d-b3e5-d6d262522f75"],"keyword":["Culture","Datasets as Topic","Denmark/ethnology","Diet/history","Emigrants and Immigrants/history","Farmers/history","Genome, Human/genetics","Genomics","Genotype","History, Ancient","Human Migration/history","Humans","Hunting/history","Phenotype","Pollen","Scandinavians and Nordic People/genetics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-63403A1CF668CFC27BF06C4A0EE19BAA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/432765731/100_ancient_genomes_show_repeated_population_turnovers_in_Neolithic_Denmark.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06862-3","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/379873563/s41586_023_06862_3.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/351362164/s41586-023-06862-3_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org"