{"abstract":"Background: Individual dietary recommendations change as loss of kidney function progresses. Adopting these recommendations in everyday life poses major challenges for patients. Individualising dietary counselling is crucial to easy accessibility. Aim: To investigate patients’ needs with regard to a dietary app for patients with chronic kidney disease, patients’, and health professionals’ immediate responses to such a dietary app and suggestions for improvement and further development of a prototype. Design: A prototype of the dietary app has been developed and demonstrates how all information it provides can be tailored to the individual patient according to stage of disease, anthropometrics, and phosphate and potassium levels. A qualitative evaluation of the prototype was conducted using the Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Research checklist for reporting. Approach: Seven individual interviews and four focus groups were analysed using interpretive description. Participants: Individual interviews with seven patients who have stage 4 or 5 chronic kidney disease and are not on dialysis, and four focus groups: one with participants from the individual interviews, one with six patients on haemodialysis, one with 13 kidney dieticians and one with seven health professionals. Findings: Both patients and healthcare professionals were positive about the app. Individualisation is necessary for the app to work in practice. The patients reported access to a diet diary and recipes as important elements. Conclusion: There is a need to improve the tools we use today to enhance patient adherence to dietary recommendations. The development of an app for individual dietary counselling could be a useful solution.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Louise","Ivarsen, Per","Førrisdahl, Lisbeth","Christensen, Mette R","Streubel-Kristensen, Lise","Sørensen, Annie","Finderup, Jeanette"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-687585"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Chronic kidney disease","Self management"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dietary app for patients with kidney disease qualitative evaluation of a prototype","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jorc.12473","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-687585","identifier":[{"id":"37337626","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85162167579","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-3664e728-4300-4f99-b244-3027f45c26a5"],"keyword":["chronic kidney disease","dietary app","self-management"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D9DB7FF350EC29A9D9B78C6DDD255520"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jorc.12473","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/411430665/Journal_of_Renal_Care_-_2023_-_Suder_-_Dietary_app_for_patients_with_kidney_disease_Qualitative_evaluation_of_a_prototype.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Medicine and Nephrology","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - SDCA-Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louise Birk","key":"suderpau","last":"Suder","locid":"b5dceb1b-6aa9-4dd7-ab12-c10199dfa916","name":"Rasmussen, Louise","name_org":"Suder, Louise Birk","orcid":"0009-0008-5479-6047","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Per","key":"ivarsenpau","last":"Ivarsen","locid":"8cc2f153-c64c-426b-8d9c-095f6b9d83a8","name":"Ivarsen, Per","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lisbeth","key":"førrisdahlpau","last":"Førrisdahl","name":"Førrisdahl, Lisbeth","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette R","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Mette R","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lise","key":"streubelkristensenpau","last":"Streubel-Kristensen","locid":"f2ec267c-f8e7-4b2d-ac82-7ea3d48f2b58","name":"Streubel-Kristensen, Lise","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Annie","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"9279294d-85a6-43a1-b4b2-fab4a881b724","name":"Sørensen, Annie","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jeanette","key":"finderuppau","last":"Finderup","locid":"706821c0-5dd9-44b0-b07b-a5bacb97a700","name":"Finderup, Jeanette","orcid":"0000-0003-2646-0227","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37337626"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/jorc.12473","issn":["17556678","17556686"],"issn_org":["17556678"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Renal Care","pages":"181-191","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Journal of Renal Care","volume":"50"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85162167579"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-3664e728-4300-4f99-b244-3027f45c26a5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Dietary app for patients with kidney disease : Qualitative evaluation of a prototype","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-687585","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Increasing numbers of elderly patients experience prolonged decreased functional capacity and impaired quality of life after seemingly successful cardiac surgery. After discharge from hospital, these patients experience a substantial gap in care until centre-based cardiac rehabilitation commences. They may benefit from immediate coaching by means of mobile health technology to overcome psychological and physiological barriers to physical activity. The aim of this study was to explore the usability, acceptability, and relevance of a mobile health application designed to support remote exercise-based cardiac rehabilitation of elderly patients early after cardiac surgery from the perspective of patients, their relatives, and physiotherapists. Methods: We adapted a home-based mobile health application for use by elderly patients early after cardiac surgery. Semi-structured dyadic interviews were conducted with a purposive sample of patients (n = 9), their spouses (n = 5), and physiotherapists (n = 2) following two weeks of the intervention. The transcribed interviews were analysed thematically. Results: Three themes were identified: 1) creating an individual fit by tailoring the intervention; 2) prioritizing communication and collaboration; and 3) interacting with the mobile health application. Overall, the findings indicate that the mobile health intervention has the potential to promote engagement, responsibility, and motivation among elderly patients to exercise early after surgery. However, the intervention can also be a burden on patients and their relatives when roles and responsibilities are unclear. Conclusion: The mobile health intervention showed potential to bridge the intervention gap after cardiac surgery, as well as in fostering engagement, responsibility, and motivation for physical activity among elderly individuals. Nevertheless, our findings emphasize the necessity of tailoring the intervention to accommodate individual vulnerabilities and capabilities. The intervention may be improved by addressing a number of organizational and communicational issues. Adaptions should be made according to the barriers and facilitators identified in this study prior to testing the effectiveness of the intervention on a larger scale. Future research should focus on the implementation of a hybrid design that supplements or complements face-to-face and centre-based cardiac rehabilitation. Trial registration: Danish Data Protection Agency, Central Denmark Region (1–16-02–193-22, 11 August 2022).","author_count":6,"contributors":["Toft, Bente Skovsby","Rodkjær, Lotte Ørneborg","Sørensen, Lotte","Rørbæk Saugbjerg, Marianne","Bekker, Hilary Louise","Modrau, Ivy Susanne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-699623"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Toft, Bente Skovsby"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Leeds"],"keyword":["Aged","Cardiac Surgical Procedures","Cardiac rehabilitation","Digital health","Feasibility Studies","Frail Elderly","Humans","Interview","Mobile health","Physical activity","Qualitative Research","Quality of Life","Telemedicine"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"feasibility of early digital health rehabilitation after cardiac surgery in the elderly a qualitative study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-024-10601-3","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Research Centre for Patient Involvement, Aarhus University Hospital"],"id":"S01-000.00-699623","identifier":[{"id":"38254129","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85182721744","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10801932","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-64499cb1-2446-4ce7-b486-e9377fab7f7b"],"keyword":["Aged","Cardiac Surgical Procedures","Cardiac rehabilitation","Digital Health","Feasibility Studies","Frail elderly","Humans","Interview","Mobile health","Physical activity","Qualitative research","Quality of Life","Telemedicine","mHealth, cardiac rehabilitation, elderly, physical activity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DCE61B8DFFBF381B9DEE50C46DA9066C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-024-10601-3","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Cardiothoracic and Vascular Surgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of leeds","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Leeds","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bente Skovsby","key":"toftpau","last":"Toft","locid":"04b50613-719d-4a5f-90bb-e887d97bc14f","name":"Toft, Bente Skovsby","orcid":"0000-0003-3107-1699","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lotte Ørneborg","key":"rodkjærpau","last":"Rodkjær","locid":"fb466eea-3f78-4efd-927f-fb5609358520","name":"Rodkjær, Lotte Ørneborg","orcid":"0000-0003-1570-2120","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lotte","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"cead2f7a-a2b1-4eff-a7cf-b99138f62258","name":"Sørensen, Lotte","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Marianne","key":"rørbæksaugbjergpau","last":"Rørbæk Saugbjerg","name":