{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The gut derived anorexigenic hormone neurotensin (NT) is upregulated after bariatric surgery which may contribute to the sustained weight loss. In contrast, diet-induced weight loss is most often followed by weight regain. We therefore investigated whether diet-induced weight loss impacts levels of circulating NT in mice and humans and whether NT levels predicts body weight change after weight loss in humans. METHODS: In vivo mice study: Obese mice were fed ad-libitum or a restricted diet (40-60 % of average food intake) for 9 days to obtain similar weight loss as observed in the human study. At termination, intestinal segments, the hypothalamus and plasma were collected for histological, real time PCR, and radioimmunoassay (RIA) analysis. CLINICAL TRIAL: Plasma samples from 42 participants with obesity, completing an 8-week low-calorie diet in a randomized controlled trial, were analyzed. Plasma NT was measured by RIA at fasting and during a meal test before and after diet-induced weight loss and after one year of intended weight maintenance. RESULTS: In obese mice, food restriction-induced body weight loss of 14 % was associated with a 64 % reduction in fasting plasma NT (p < 0.0001). In the mouse duodenum (p = 0.07) and jejunum (p < 0.05), NT tissue concentration was decreased without tissue atrophy indicative of a physiological downregulation. In the mouse hypothalamus a downregulation of Pomc (p < 0.01) along with upregulation of Npy (p < 0.001) and Agrp (p < 0.0001) expression was found after restricted feeding in support of increased hunger after diet-induced weight loss. Therefore, we investigated the NT response in humans undergoing weight loss maintenance. In humans, similar to the mice, the low-calorie diet induced weight loss of 13 % body weight was associated with 40 % reduction in fasting plasma NT levels (p < 0.001). Meal-induced NT peak responses were greater in humans who lost additional weight during the 1 year maintenance phase compared to participants who regained weight (p < 0.05). CONCLUSION: Diet-induced weight loss decreased fasting plasma NT levels in both humans and mice with obesity, and regulated hunger-associated hypothalamic gene expression in mice. Meal-induced NT responses were greater in humans who lost additional weight during the 1 year maintenance phase compared to participants who regained weight. This indicates that increased peak secretion of NT after weight loss may contribute to successful maintenance of weight loss. CLINICAL TRIAL REGISTRATION NUMBER: NCT02094183.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Brethvad, Annemette Overgaard","Zakariassen, Hannah Louise","Holt, Joachim","Lundgren, Julie Rehné","Jakobsen, Alexander","Hartmann, Bolette","Iepsen, Eva Pers Winning","Kissow, Hannelouise","Holst, Jens Juul","Madsbad, Sten","Torekov, Signe Sørensen","Holst, Birgitte"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-537800"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"increased meal induced neurotensin response predicts successful maintenance of weight loss data from a randomized controlled trial","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.metabol.2023.155534","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-537800","identifier":[{"id":"85150384778","type":"scopus"},{"id":"001042616000001","type":"wos"},{"id":"36933790","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-f1f12c0e-8c77-473b-81b6-80c79189779d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-926246D3AB6B6A94DAD5C36576F1F801"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/340106239/1_s2.0_S0026049523001373_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.metabol.2023.155534","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Experimental Medicine, Section of Laboratory Animal Facilities"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Annemette Overgaard","key":"brethvadannemetteovergaardpau","last":"Brethvad","locid":"7e7a2d48-2a93-4231-82aa-9a054abd9310","name":"Brethvad, Annemette Overgaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hannah Louise","key":"zakariassenhannahlouisepau","last":"Zakariassen","locid":"d01c4488-99e4-4c7d-a9b5-b5e07368c3c4","name":"Zakariassen, Hannah Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Joachim","key":"holtjoachimpau","last":"Holt","locid":"ae8bb934-cd85-4557-a342-dd5ca6922444","name":"Holt, Joachim","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Julie Rehné","key":"lundgrenjulierehnépau","last":"Lundgren","locid":"88bf4b27-fa5b-4090-a986-cb00acedd3b0","name":"Lundgren, Julie Rehné","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Alexander","key":"jakobsenalexanderpau","last":"Jakobsen","locid":"e0b15003-8dbe-443e-a98a-2c5344cf74a5","name":"Jakobsen, Alexander","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bolette","key":"hartmannbolettepau","last":"Hartmann","locid":"eb04fc6b-068a-4d5b-a106-1b7be3f7e12b","name":"Hartmann, Bolette","orcid":"0000-0001-8509-2036","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eva Winning","key":"iepsenevaperswinningpau","last":"Lehmann","locid":"a491ddc5-3cd9-40bb-9837-b310f14c384c","name":"Iepsen, Eva Pers Winning","name_org":"Lehmann, Eva Winning","orcid":"0000-0002-0880-8798","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hannelouise","key":"kissowhannelouisepau","last":"Kissow","locid":"7acc7923-4bb5-4e35-9bc8-6bf8c5b0b609","name":"Kissow, Hannelouise","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens Juul","key":"holstjensjuulpau","last":"Holst","locid":"864b6401-9024-4773-9bdb-f4d4ca57dcad","name":"Holst, Jens Juul","orcid":"0000-0001-6853-3805","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sten","key":"madsbadstenpau","last":"Madsbad","locid":"1018c708-245a-4057-9263-074578042385","name":"Madsbad, Sten","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Signe Sørensen","key":"torekovsignesørensenpau","last":"Torekov","locid":"c1d71a61-ee09-4c29-ae48-7520707a73be","name":"Torekov, Signe Sørensen","orcid":"0000-0001-6779-0252","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Birgitte","key":"holstbirgittepau","last":"Holst","locid":"be07b398-88db-4db5-9851-89125f68edf4","name":"Holst, Birgitte","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36933790"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.metabol.2023.155534","issn":["00260495","15328600"],"issn_org":["00260495"],"journal":"Metabolism","paperid":"155534","status":"p","title":"Metabolism","title_org":"Metabolism: Clinical and Experimental","volume":"143"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85150384778"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f1f12c0e-8c77-473b-81b6-80c79189779d"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Increased meal-induced neurotensin response predicts successful maintenance of weight loss : Data from a randomized controlled trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-537800","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Aims: We wanted to assess if 15O-H2O myocardial perfusion imaging (MPI) in a clinical setting can predict referral to coronary artery catheterization [coronary angiography (CAG)], execution of percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), and post-PCI angina relief for patients with angina and previous coronary artery bypass graft (CABG). Methods and results: We analysed 172 symptomatic CABG patients referred for 15O-H2O positron emission tomography (PET) MPI at Aarhus University Hospital Department of Nuclear Medicine & PET Centre, of which five did not complete the scan. In total, 145 (87%) enrolled patients had an abnormal MPI. Of these, 86/145 (59%) underwent CAG within 3 months; however, no PET parameters predicted referral to CAG. During the CAG, 25/86 (29%) patients were revascularized by PCI. Relative flow reserve (RFR) (0.49 vs. 0.54 P = 0.03), vessel-specific myocardial blood flow (MBF) (1.53 vs. 1.88 mL/g/min, P < 0.01), and vessel-specific myocardial flow reserve (MFR) (1.73 vs. 2.13, P < 0.01) were significantly lower in patients revascularized by PCI. Receiver operating characteristic analysis of the vessel-specific parameters yielded optimal cutoffs of 1.36 mL/g/ min (MBF) and 1.28 (MFR) to predict PCI. Angina relief was experienced by 18/24 (75%) of the patients who underwent PCI. Myocardial blood flow was an excellent predictor of angina relief on both a global [area under the curve (AUC) = 0.85, P < 0.01] and vessel-specific (AUC = 0.90, P < 0.01) level with optimal cutoff levels of 1.99 mL/g/min and 1.85 mL/ g/min, respectively. Conclusion For CABG patients, RFR, vessel-specific MBF, and vessel-specific MFR measured by 15O-H2O PET MPI predict whether subsequent CAG will result in PCI. Additionally, global and vessel-specific MBF values predict post-PCI angina relief.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Vester, Mazen","Madsen, Simon","Kjærulff, Mette Louise Gram","Tolbod, Lars Poulsen","Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","Kristensen, Steen Dalby","Christiansen, Evald Høj","Nielsen, Per Hostrup","Sørensen, Jens","Gormsen, Lars Christian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-675288"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Gormsen, Lars Christian"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Uppsala University"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"myocardial perfusion imaging by sup 000000000015 sup o h sup 000000000002 sup o positron emission tomography predicts clinical revascularization procedures in symptomatic patients with previous coronary artery bypass graft","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ehjopen/oead044","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-675288","identifier":[{"id":"37206919","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85161080962","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10191278","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-88db432e-a437-43ba-9269-13b1045d2a33"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EC20CC71AF496A6474092D4EAFD1113C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ehjopen/oead044","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Nuclear Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mazen","key":"vestermazenpau","last":"Vester","locid":"5cb4c97e-12ab-4e01-a1c8-4e89a86711ce","name":"Vester, Mazen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simon","key":"madsensimonpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"7178db82-0c1e-4350-8693-29ddb14306f4","name":"Madsen, Simon","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette Louise Gram","key":"kjærulffmettelouisegrampau","last":"Kjærulff","locid":"03a92e42-ba02-4e8c-9662-528d92b0640e","name":"Kjærulff, Mette Louise Gram","orcid":"0000-0003-1215-2207","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Poulsen","key":"tolbodlarspoulsenpau","last":"Tolbod","locid":"a1e56507-eb80-4f94-9470-df90dcecbc62","name":"Tolbod, Lars Poulsen","orcid":"0000-0002-8105-7046","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bent Roni Ranghøj","key":"nielsenbentroniranghøjpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"896f4a21-d29c-4ad9-b0ef-4e7f8616c234","name":"Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","orcid":"0000-0001-9228-3869","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Steen Dalby","key":"kristensensteendalbypau","last":"Kristensen","locid":"6d64271d-8a2c-41ea-891d-e85555bbd47a","name":"Kristensen, Steen Dalby","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Evald Høj","key":"christiansenevaldhøjpau","last":"Christiansen","locid":"06b04feb-3d2f-402d-b211-7ad5fd0387f1","name":"Christiansen, Evald Høj","orcid":"0000-0002-8740-4862","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Per Hostrup","key":"nielsenperhostruppau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f7d1db00-47c2-4afc-b82c-805fa4372e91","name":"Nielsen, Per Hostrup","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens","key":"sørensenjenspau","last":"Sörensen","locid":"cab278f0-6db5-48a8-8764-b7da319d7818","name":"Sørensen, Jens","name_org":"Sörensen, Jens","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Christian","key":"gormsenlarschristianpau","last":"Gormsen","locid":"a360c42f-861a-40d6-9d23-64b560b33190","name":"Gormsen, Lars Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-0144-1305","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37206919"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/ehjopen/oead044","issn":["27524191"],"issue":"3","journal":"European Heart Journal Open","paperid":"oead044","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"European Heart Journal Open","volume":"3"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85161080962"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-88db432e-a437-43ba-9269-13b1045d2a33"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Myocardial perfusion imaging by <sup>15</sup>O-H<sup>2</sup>O positron emission tomography predicts clinical revascularization procedures in symptomatic patients with previous coronary artery bypass graft.","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-675288","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Research goals The current economic crisis and ageing population raise questions of how the entrepreneurial development in Europe can be strengthened, for example through generational change and continued entrepreneurial development. By coining the term entrepreneurial potential (Krueger & Brazeal, 1994), a new scientific field has recently manifested itself. The knowledge developed within this field, may further our understanding of the concept and processes of entrepreneurial potential and its potential for solving imminent societal challenges. With this purpose, we review the literature on entrepreneurial potential and develop a wider theoretical model of entrepreneurial potential. Theoretical background Focusing the review on literature on developmental-competence actualization of human potential, we apply the Aristotelian distinction between potentiality and actuality (Aristotle, 2017) as a guiding, theoretical framework for the review. Methodology As the purpose of the review was to identify and understand all the relevant research traditions and approaches to entrepreneurial potential and to synthesize this into an overall theoretical model, we applied a semi-systematic review methodology (Wong et al, 2013). The literature search was performed in Scopus and Web of Science, resulting in 356 (Scopus) and 303 hits (Web of Science) using the phrase search “entrepreneurial potential”, supported by backward snowballing (Jalali & Wohlin, 2012) Results The literature review reveals that this nascent field studying entrepreneurial potential is not yet clearly outlined and delimitated, neither in terms of theorization, construct definition, nor operationalization. Theorization/ The main theoretical lenses applied in the emerging field are socialisation and/or personality theories. Using social learning theory (Bandura, 1977), social cognitive theory (Bandura, 1986), and theory of planned behavior (Ajzen, 1991), entrepreneurial potential is theorized to develop through role modelling, implicit learning, or other intangibles, often studied through a family business background (Chen et al, 1998; Wang et al, 2018; Hahn et al, 2021). Construct/ Most publications do not define the phenomenon of entrepreneurial potential but frequently use proxy phenomena as indicators of entrepreneurial potential (Chen et al., 1998, Wang et al, 2018). Entrepreneurial potential is most commonly defined as a precursor for and preparedness to engage in entrepreneurial activities (Krueger & Brazeal, 1994; Santos et al, 2013; Veljkovic et al, 2019). Operationalization/ Our review finds a great variety of operationalisations and approaches in the few available empirical studies (Chen et al, 1998; Hahn et al, 2021). Also the operationalisationsapply proxy-measurements, such as entrepreneurial intentions, risk-taking propensities, locus of control, personality traits, etc. Some studies seek to operationalize entrepreneurial potential as a fixed set of specific competencies (Raab et al, 2005; Santos et al, 2013), while others as more general characteristics (Krueger & Brazeal, 1994; Veljkovic et al, 2019) Gender/ The review reveals important gender differences in the development, state, and actualization of entrepreneurial potential. Some empirical studies suggest that women need higher degrees of self-efficacy than men to engage in entrepreneurial acts (Wilson et al, 2007, 2009). Knowledge in gendered entrepreneurial potential is underrepresented. With the purpose of synthesizing the available knowledge on entrepreneurial potential, we develop a theoretical model based on Aristoteles’ broader conception of potentiality. The model will depict entrepreneurial potential as a teleological process, through which entrepreneurial potential develops (becoming/potentiality), is self-efficacious (state of being), and manifests itself (doing/actuality). Limitations The scope of the review is limited by the selection of search words and inclusion/exclusion criteria and is, as a semi-structured review, intended to explore and synthesize, rather than be exhaustive. Conclusions Mapping the literature review onto our theoretical model, we find that research is needed to offer a generally applicable definition of entrepreneurial potential, to develop and interrelate knowledge on both the potentiality and actuality, and to further the understanding of enabling and inhibiting conditions. Furthermore, specific knowledge on the development and application of female entrepreneurial potential is highly needed. Relevance to Congress Theme The paper is relevant to the congress theme, as it highlights important and emerging research on entrepreneurial potential. Entrepreneurial potential is not exclusively relevant for business start-up or take-over, but may also unfold and actualize in a wider world of work, which is defined by complexity and diversity.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Sørensen, Frej","Holten, Ann-Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-683809"],"event":{"end":"2023-05-27","place":"Katowice, pl","start":"2023-05-24","title":"21st congress of the European Association of Work and Organisational Psychology","year":"2023"},"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what do we know about entrepreneurial potential","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://eawop2023.org/static/sites/tcekd_eawop_2022/book-of-abstracts.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-683809","ids":["ku-2cdf1046-ac91-4d91-947b-6c4ae3b792db"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-10BCCC5072724EA199FB53C63EE18F15"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://eawop2023.org/static/sites/tcekd_eawop_2022/book-of-abstracts.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Psychology, Department of Psychology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Frej","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"39974cd4-0464-4216-aeab-c83a6d417a4a","name":"Sørensen, Frej","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ann-Louise","key":"holtenpau","last":"Holten","locid":"a34ba7a1-75cf-4eb7-99a0-603d1e5ecca1","name":"Holten, Ann-Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6031-5843","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-2cdf1046-ac91-4d91-947b-6c4ae3b792db"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"What do we know about entrepreneurial potential?","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-683809","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is a severe pregnancy complication for both the woman and the child. Women who suffer from GDM have a greater risk of developing Type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) later in life. Identification of any potential biomarkers for the early prediction of gestational diabetes can help prevent the disease in women with a high risk. Studies show microRNA (miRNA) as a potential biomarker for the early discovery of GDM, but there is a lack of clarity as to which miRNAs are consistently altered in GDM. This study aimed to perform a systematic review and meta-analysis to investigate miRNAs associated with GDM by comparing GDM cases with normoglycemic controls. The systematic review was performed according to PRISMA guidelines with searches in PubMed, Web of Science, and ScienceDirect. The primary search resulted in a total of 849 articles, which were screened according to the prior established inclusion and exclusion criteria. Following the screening of articles, the review was based on the inclusion of 35 full-text articles, which were evaluated for risk of bias and estimates of quality, after which data were extracted and relative values for miRNAs were calculated. A meta-analysis was performed for the miRNA species investigated in three or more studies: MiR-29a, miR-330, miR-134, miR-132, miR-16, miR-223, miR-155, miR-122, miR-17, miR-103, miR-125, miR-210, and miR-222. While some miRNAs showed considerable between-study variability, miR-29a, miR-330, miR-134, miR-16, miR-223, and miR-17 showed significant overall upregulation in GDM, while circulating levels of miR-132 and miR-155 were decreased among GDM patients, suggesting further studies of these as biomarkers for early GDM discovery.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Dinesen, Sofie","El-Faitarouni, Alisar","Frisk, Nanna Lond Skov","Sørensen, Anja Elaine","Dalgaard, Louise Torp"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-684889"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Diagnostic biomarker","Gestational diabetes","Prognostic biomarker","microRNA"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"circulating microrna as biomarkers for gestational diabetes mellitus a systematic review and meta analysis","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms24076186","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-684889","identifier":[{"id":"37047159","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85152323462","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-90e13c0b-4c7a-4140-b048-55b6195ceedb"],"keyword":["diagnostic biomarker","gestational diabetes","microRNA","prognostic biomarker"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E6F4CE2A24470E7C276C858618456DC2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms24076186","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/92987900/ijms_24_06186.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Science and Environment"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Zealand University Hospital, Roskilde"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Sofie","key":"dinesenpau","last":"Dinesen","name":"Dinesen, Sofie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Alisar","key":"elfaitarounipau","last":"El-Faitarouni","name":"El-Faitarouni, Alisar","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Nanna Lond Skov","key":"friskpau","last":"Frisk","locid":"25f309b3-6a87-40f7-8fe6-7475e7ecce58","name":"Frisk, Nanna Lond Skov","orcid":"0000-0001-6150-2408","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","country":"dk","first":"Anja Elaine","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"7e5d1e2c-5d4b-4436-bb66-acdca4058c08","name":"Sørensen, Anja Elaine","orcid":"0000-0001-7557-5376","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Louise Torp","key":"dalgaardpau","last":"Dalgaard","locid":"a219fdaf-f994-4a7a-beb8-1f353846ed5a","name":"Dalgaard, Louise Torp","orcid":"0000-0002-3598-2775","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37047159"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/ijms24076186","issn":["14220067","16616596"],"issn_org":["16616596"],"issue":"7","journal":"International Journal of Molecular Sciences","paperid":"6186","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Molecular Sciences","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85152323462"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-90e13c0b-4c7a-4140-b048-55b6195ceedb"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"Circulating microRNA as Biomarkers for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus—A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-684889","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Grønlund, Louise","Mathiasen, Nanet","Sørensen, Pernille","Pryds, Claus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-706453"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"poetic daylight poetisk daglys a book","src":["arkc"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://adk.elsevierpure.com/ws/files/83141438/Poetisk_Dagslys_bog.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-706453","ids":["arkc-b42a5dde-c227-40ef-9c07-5faf85c0206e"],"keyword":["Nej"],"lang":"Multiple","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-DBADD84E20A57610E582D70D5DE2278F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://adk.elsevierpure.com/ws/files/83141438/Poetisk_Dagslys_bog.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDKA","l3nn":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","namevar":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Center for Interior Studies","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Light in Architecture and Design","Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation, Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Institute of Architecture and Design"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDKA","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"grønlundpau","last":"Grønlund","locid":"b5bd2523-ca04-4275-9a2f-e81f54f9f017","name":"Grønlund, Louise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nanet","key":"mathiasenpau","last":"Mathiasen","name":"Mathiasen, Nanet","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pernille","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Pernille","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"prydspau","last":"Pryds","name":"Pryds, Claus","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"40","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["arkc-b42a5dde-c227-40ef-9c07-5faf85c0206e"],"src":["Royal Danish Academy - Architecture, Design, Conservation"],"title":"Poetic Daylight / Poetisk Daglys - a book","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-706453","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Volunteer First Responders are used worldwide. In the Region of Southern Denmark, two types of programs have been established. One of these programs consists of voluntary responders without any requirements of education or training who are summoned to prehospital cardiac arrests. The other type of program is established primarily in the rural areas of the region and consists of volunteers with some mandatory education in first aid. These volunteers are summoned to all urgent cases along with the ambulances. Cooperation between professional healthcare workers and nonprofessionals summoned through official channels may be challenging. This study aimed to explore prehospital clinicians’ experiences of ethical challenges in cooperation with volunteer first responders. Methods: We conducted 16 semi-structured interviews at four different ambulance stations in the Region of Southern Denmark. Five emergency physicians and 11 emergency medical technicians/paramedics were interviewed. The interviews were transcribed, and the data were analysed using systematic text condensation. Results: The study's 16 interviews resulted in the identification of some specific categories that challenged the cooperation between the two parties. We identified three main categories: 1. Beneficence, the act of doing good, 2. The risk of harming patients’ autonomy 3. Non-maleficence, which is the obligation not to inflict harm on others. Conclusion: This study provides an in-depth insight into the ethical challenges between prehospital clinicians and voluntary first responders from the perspective of the prehospital clinicians. Both programs are considered to have value but only when treating patients with cardiac arrest. Our study highlights potential areas of improvement in the two Danish voluntary programs in their current form.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Sørensen, Oliver Beierholm","Milling, Louise","Laerkner, Eva","Mikkelsen, Søren","Bruun, Henriette"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-714136"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Mikkelsen, Søren"],"keyword":["Ambulances","Emergency Medical Services methods","Emergency Responders","Emergency medical technicians","Ethical challenges","Health Personnel","Heart Arrest","Humans","Qualitative Research","Volunteer first responders"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"professional prehospital clinicians experiences of ethical challenges associated with the collaboration with organised voluntary first responders a qualitative study","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13049-023-01147-0","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The presence of VFRs could make an already difficult situation even more stressful for prehospital clinicians []. This was supported by our study of prehospital clinicians. Ethical challenges in the prehospital emergency setting are not only related to the VFRs. Previous studies have identified other themes regarding situations where EMS experiences challenges and conflicts concerning ethical considerations such as caring for patients, the professional role and self-identity, and external collaboration [, , ]."],"id":"S01-000.00-714136","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10644536","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"37964364","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85176458084","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-69c5214d-b87c-48cc-8f26-77be9aa53133"],"keyword":["Ambulances","Community first responders","Emergency Medical Services/methods","Emergency Responders","Emergency medical technicians","Ethical challenges","Health Personnel","Heart Arrest","Humans","Prehospital physicians","Qualitative Research","Volunteer first responders"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-16565DD67DAF6FF7D49F33EC1875163E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/245120548/s13049_023_01147_0.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13049-023-01147-0","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Prehospital Area"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["​Nordsjællands Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Oliver Beierholm","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"a078a3ac-92b3-4e68-bcd0-983f1a0a3a9f","name":"Sørensen, Oliver Beierholm","orcid":"0009-0004-2028-065X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"millingpau","last":"Milling","locid":"e190eca7-5826-456c-bb1e-f41f8e431770","name":"Milling, Louise","orcid":"0000-0001-8320-5391","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Eva","key":"laerknerpau","last":"Laerkner","locid":"af7f45ef-090a-41ad-8