Between Research On, With and For Participation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-727553","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: To assess interactions between combinations of quantitative demands, emotional demands, unclear and contradictory demands, and violence/threats of violence in the prospective association with risk of long-term sickness absence (LTSA). METHODS: We included 55 467 employees from the 2012, 2014 and 2016 waves of the Work Environment and Health in Denmark (WEHD) survey. We measured the four independent variables in the WEHD survey and assessed risk of LTSA in a national register during 12 months of follow-up. Using Cox proportional hazards models, adjusted for age, sex, educational attainment and job group, we estimated risk of LTSA and assessed deviation from additivity using relative excess risk due to interaction (RERI). RESULTS: For combinations of high emotional demands and high quantitative demands (HR 1.50; 95% CI 1.33 to 1.70; RERI 0.06; 95% CI -0.15 o 0.26) and high emotional demands and violence/threats of violence (HR 1.76; 95% CI 1.53 to 2.02; RERI 0.12; 95% CI -0.43 to 0.66) we found no indications of deviations from additive effects in predicting LTSA. For combinations of violence/threats of violence and high quantitative demands (HR 1.90; 95% CI 1.64 to 2.20; RERI 0.36; 95% CI 0.01 to 0.71) and unclear and contradictory demands and high quantitative demands (HR 1.46; 95% CI 1.31 to 1.62; RERI 0.23; 95% CI 0.04 to 0.42) the results indicated an excess risk of LTSA above additivity (ie, superadditivity). CONCLUSIONS: Participants reporting high quantitative demands combined with either violence/threats of violence or unclear and contradictory demands showed a higher risk of LTSA than expected, indicating superadditivity. Results have implications for preventing negative health effects related to adverse psychosocial working conditions.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Clausen, Thomas","Sørensen, Jeppe Karl","Dalsager, Louise","Karlsen, IL","Kristiansen, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-780180"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Denmark epidemiology","Emotions","Employment","Follow Up Studies","Humans","Journal Article","Krav i arbejdet","Proportional Hazards Models","Risk Factors","Sick Leave","Sygefravær"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"do different job demands interact as predictors of long term sickness absence a register based follow up on 000000000055 000000000467 danish workers","src":["nfa"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/oemed-2022-108444","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-780180","identifier":[{"id":"85142475244","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36270798","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["nfa-250df580-ec10-487b-bcea-b5e2a6d58659"],"keyword":["Denmark/epidemiology","Emotions","Employment","Follow-Up Studies","Humans","Journal Article","Krav i arbejdet","Proportional Hazards Models","Risk Factors","Sick Leave","Sygefravær"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9E4BD692FB43CAC2C9E5BE97F22DD798"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/oemed-2022-108444","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"nfa national research centre for the working environment","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-WORK","l3nn":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","namevar":["The National Research Centre for the Working Environment","The National Research Centre for the Working Environment, Directors, Psychosocial Working Environment and Working Accidents and Safety Culture"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-WORK","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","locid":"5dcf2388-82f6-45de-910a-538287fd84e0","name":"Clausen, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-8253-4449","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeppe Karl","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"42f8c87d-2eda-4198-9cf5-fe5d426e9eb7","name":"Sørensen, Jeppe Karl","orcid":"0000-0002-0789-8752","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"dalsagerpau","last":"Dalsager","locid":"09c67a97-b524-4868-a287-957d4f04e6d6","name":"Dalsager, Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-6931-9525","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Iben Louise","key":"karlsenpau","last":"Karlsen","locid":"3ef0a37d-85b9-40e5-9a89-9e2f67171ffb","name":"Karlsen, IL","name_org":"Karlsen, Iben Louise","orcid":"0000-0002-7923-5800","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"kristiansenpau","last":"Kristiansen","locid":"2b90a97c-15d6-468c-a8d6-1362e731d2bc","name":"Kristiansen, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-1637-8900","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36270798"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/oemed-2022-108444","issn":["13510711","14707926"],"issn_org":["13510711"],"issue":"1","journal":"Occupational and Environmental Medicine","pages":"7-13","publisher":"BMJ Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Occupational and Environmental Medicine","volume":"80"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85142475244"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","Governmental Institutions"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["nfa-250df580-ec10-487b-bcea-b5e2a6d58659"],"src":["NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment"],"title":"Do different job demands interact as predictors of long-term sickness absence? A register-based follow-up on 55 467 Danish workers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-780180","year":"2023","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Management of spontaneous intra-abdominal abscess (IAA) in patients with Crohn's disease (CD) with radiologically guided percutaneous drainage (PD) was debated. Methods: This is a secondary analysis from a multicenter, retrospective cohort study of all the patients with CD who underwent PD followed by surgery at 19 international tertiary centers. Results: Seventeen patients (4.8%) who did not undergo surgery after PD were compared to those who had PD followed by surgical intervention 335/352 (95.2%). Patients who had PD without surgery were those with longer disease duration, more frequently had previous surgery for CD (laparotomies/laparoscopies), enteric fistula, on steroid treatment before and continue to have it after PD. Patients who had PD without subsequent surgical resection had a higher risk of stoma construction at later stages 8/17 (47.1%) versus 90/326 (27.6%) (P < .01). Patients with PD with no subsequent surgery had numerically higher rates of abscess recurrence 5/17 (29.4%) compared to those who had PD followed by surgery 45/335 (13.4%) the difference was not statistically significant (P = .07). Conclusions: Even with the low number of patients enrolled in this study who had PD of IAA without subsequent surgery, the findings indicate a markedly worse prognosis in terms of recurrence, length of stay, readmission, and stoma construction. Watchful waiting after PD to treat patients with spontaneous IAA might be indicated in selected patients with poor health status or poor prognostic factors.","author_count":34,"contributors":["El-Hussuna, Alaa","Steenholdt, Casper","Merrild Karer, Mette Louise","Nyggard Uldall Nielsen, Natasja","Mujukian, Angela","Fleshner, Phillip R.","Iesalnieks, Igors","Horesh, Nir","Kopylov, Uri","Jacoby, Harel","Al-Qaisi, Haider Mahmoud","Colombo, Francesco","Sampietro, Gianluca M.","Marino, Marco V.","Ellebæk, Mark","Sørensen, Nina","Celentano, Valerio","Ladwa, Nikhil","Warusavitarne, Janindra","Pellino, Gianluca","Zeb, Aurang","Di Candido, Francesca","Hurtado-Pardo, Luis","Frasson, Matteo","Kunovsky, Lumir","Yalcinkaya, Ali","Alonso, Sandra","Pera, Miguel","Rodríguez, Cristina Antón","Bravo, Ana-Minaya","Granero, Alvaro Garcia","Tatar, Ozan Can","Spinelli, Antonino","Qvist, Niels"],"cuid":["S02-082.54-691834"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["El-Hussuna, Alaa"],"country":["Czechia","Germany","Israel","Italy","Spain","Turkey","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["\"Luigi Sacco\" University Hospital","All Global Affiliations","Catholic University of Valencia","Cedars-Sinai Medical Center","Chelsea and Westminster Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","Department of Pediatrics, Kocaeli University Medical Faculty, Kocaeli 41380, Turkey.","Francisco de Vitoria University","Gazi University","Henares University Hospital","Hospital Universitario La Fe","Hospital Universitario Puerta del Mar","Hospital Universitario Son Espases","Hospital Universitario del Henares","Hospital del Mar","Humanitas University","IRCCS Istituto Clinico Humanitas - Rozzano (Milano)","Klinikum Bogenhausen","Klinikum München Bogenhausen","Kocaeli University","London North West University Healthcare NHS Trust","Masaryk Memorial Cancer Institute","Masaryk University","Olomouc University Hospital","Ospedale Luigi Sacco","Ospedali Riuniti Villa Sofia-Cervello","Palacký University Olomouc","Son Espases University Hospital","Tel Aviv University","The University Hospital Brno","United Hospitals Villa Sofia-Cervello","Universidad Francisco de Vitoria","University of Campania Luigi Vanvitelli","University of Milan","University of Valencia","Vall d'Hebron Institute of Oncology","Vall d'Hebron University Hospital"],"keyword":["Abscess","Complications","Crohn s disease","Stoma","Surgery"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR"],"OT":["OpenSourceResearch Collaboration"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"watchful waiting after radiological guided drainage of intra abdominal abscess in patients with crohn s disease might be associated with increased rates of stoma construction","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/crocol/otad038","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-082.54-691834","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10460196","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"37636010","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85171442441","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-bce6370e-0626-4542-809d-9ca6402abc29","sdu-d5c74853-1137-4fa5-9f92-897191f597e9"],"keyword":["Crohn's disease","abscess","complications","stoma","surgery"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D63AA91F6A82A33D77317E9CC2A612E8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/548860503/El-Hussuna_et_al._2023_._Watchful_Waiting_After_Radiological_Guided_Drainage_of_Intra-abdominal_Abscess_in_Patients_With_Crohn_s_Disease_Might_Be_Associated_With_Increased_Rates_of_Stoma_Construction.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/240703138/otad038.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/crocol/otad038","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],