{"abstract":"Nanoparticles and colored substances can inhibit algal growth by light shading and chemical toxicity. This study presents two complementary approaches to account for shading in algal growth inhibition tests of engineered nanomaterials (ENMs) and colored substances. The first approach distinguishes between shading effects and toxicity by varying the light path in parallel algal growth inhibition tests. This Multiple Path-Length (MPL) test was applied to TiO2 ENMs and the colored substances sodium picramate and Rhodamine B. A left shifting of concentration-response curves, with increasing light path lengths, indicated shading for Rhodamine B, sodium picramate and TiO2 ENMs. EC50-values obtained at the shortest light path length were generally found best suited to quantify the toxicity of ENMs and colored substances. The second approach addresses shading at the cellular level, where particles can attach to the cell surface and affect photo-pigment content and composition. Pigments associated with photosystem I and II were determined at varying light intensities and concentrations of TiO2 ENMs. The photo-pigments that increased in response to physical shading, decreased after TiO2 ENMs exposure. This indicates that toxicity rather than cellular shading dominated the response of algae exposed to TiO2 ENMs. Additional tests were conducted with the nanomaterials CeO2 and goethite to evaluate the applicability of this approach to other ENMs. On this basis, we recommend MPL testing for determining EC50-values that are not confounded by shading in the test solution, and the pigment-based approach for investigating shading on the cellular level.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Skjolding, Lars Michael","Sørensen, Sara Nørgaard","Dyhr, Karen Scharling","Hjorth, Rune","Schlüter, Louise","Hedberg, Camilla","Hartmann, Nanna B.","Mayer, Philipp","Baun, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-368779"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Hjorth, Rune"],"keyword":["Ecotoxicology","Exposure","Green algae","Titanium dioxide"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-U"],"level3":{"GTS":["DI-GTS-ALL","DI-GTS-DHYI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-GTS-DHYI","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"separating toxicity and shading in algal growth inhibition tests of nanomaterials and colored substances","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85131671167","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-368779","identifier":[{"id":"35695192","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85131671167","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-d6e4da12-8219-444f-a153-367d2a7aedcc"],"keyword":["Ecotoxicology","Exposure","Green algae","Test artifacts","Titanium dioxide"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D631E24754A3ED6E9459CDB029943B0E"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Environmental and Resource Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Environmental and Resource Engineering, Circularity & Environmental Impact"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"dhi danish hydraulic institute","l2":"DI-GTS","l2n":"GTS Institutes","l3":"DI-GTS-DHYI","l3nn":"DHI Danish Hydraulic Institute","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DHI Danish Hydraulic Institute","namevar":["DHI Water - Environment - Health"],"nora_code":"DI-GTS-DHYI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Michael","key":"skjoldingpau","last":"Skjolding","locid":"0f7dbfa4-64f2-449a-bb3e-f9d14d60b2ca","name":"Skjolding, Lars Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-3280-1836","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sara Nørgaard","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"1c9842d3-875f-49e3-84a0-d9895fa754fd","name":"Sørensen, Sara Nørgaard","orcid":"0000-0003-3673-9483","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Karen Scharling","key":"dyhrpau","last":"Dyhr","locid":"2947cacc-5dcc-4bbe-917c-065f413e5c1c","name":"Dyhr, Karen Scharling","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rune","key":"hjorthpau","last":"Hjorth","locid":"dc53f548-3cfe-47ce-a08b-3ed74cc67a40","name":"Hjorth, Rune","orcid":"0000-0002-2221-6481","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise","key":"schlüterpau","last":"Schlüter","name":"Schlüter, Louise","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Camilla","key":"hedbergpau","last":"Hedberg","locid":"613fc483-307e-4f89-9539-41063c4d4ef8","name":"Hedberg, Camilla","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nanna B.","key":"hartmannpau","last":"Hartmann","locid":"8dccdf12-c16c-4cd8-a41f-a58c6a0b6dfc","name":"Hartmann, Nanna B.","orcid":"0000-0002-0442-245X","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Philipp","key":"mayerpau","last":"Mayer","locid":"f732d693-1e5e-4940-8d29-9e5f59e43ef3","name":"Mayer, Philipp","orcid":"0000-0001-5138-7506","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"baunpau","last":"Baun","locid":"f3cdf4a9-bfc7-4e0e-bf46-df0da8134f0d","name":"Baun, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-1396-408X","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35695192"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/17435390.2022.2080608","issn":["17435390","17435404"],"issn_org":["17435390"],"issue":"3","journal":"Nanotoxicology","pages":"265-275","publisher":"Taylor and Francis Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Nanotoxicology","volume":"16"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131671167"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All GTS Institutes","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-GTS-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","GTS Institutes","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-d6e4da12-8219-444f-a153-367d2a7aedcc"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Separating toxicity and shading in algal growth inhibition tests of nanomaterials and colored substances","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-368779","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"With increasing numbers of vaccine-breakthrough infections worldwide, assessing the immunogenicity of vaccinated health-care workers that are frequently exposed to SARS-CoV-2-infected individuals is important. In this study, neutralization titers against SARS-CoV-2 were assessed one month after completed prime-boost vaccine regimens in health-care workers vaccinated with either mRNA–mRNA (Comirnaty®, BioNTech-Pfzier, Mainz, Germany/New York, NY, USA, n = 98) or vector-based (Vaxzevria®, Oxford-AstraZeneca, Cambridge, UK) followed by mRNA-based (Comirnaty® or Spikevax®, Moderna, Cambridge, MA, USA) vaccines (n = 16). Vaccine-induced neutralization titers were compared to time-matched, unvaccinated individuals that were infected with SARS-CoV-2 and presented with mild symptoms (n = 38). Significantly higher neutralizing titers were found in both the mRNA–mRNA (ID50: 2525, IQR: 1667–4313) and vector–mRNA (ID50: 4978, IQR: 3364–7508) prime-boost vaccine regimens when compared to SARS-CoV-2 infection (ID50: 401, IQR: 271–792) (p < 0.0001). However, infection with SARS-CoV-2 induced higher titers when compared to a single dose of Vaxzevria® (p = 0.0072). Between mRNA–mRNA and vector– mRNA prime-boost regimens, the vector–mRNA vaccine regimen induced higher neutralization titers (p = 0.0054). Demographically, both age and time between vaccination doses were associated with vaccine-induced neutralization titers (p = 0.02 and p = 0.03, respectively). This warrants further investigation into the optimal time to administer booster vaccination for optimized induction of neutralizing responses. Although anecdotal (n = 3), those with exposure to SARS-CoV-2, either before or after vaccination, demonstrated superior neutralizing titers, which is suggestive of further boosting through viral exposure.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Sølund, Christina","Underwood, Alexander P.","Fernandez-Antunez, Carlota","Bollerup, Signe","Mikkelsen, Lotte S.","Villadsen, Signe Lysemose","Fahnøe, Ulrik","Winckelmann, Anni Assing","Feng, Shan","Nørløv Vinten, Caroline A.","Dalegaard, Magnus Illum","Vizgirda, Greta","Sørensen, Anna Louise","Ramirez, Santseharay","Bukh, Jens","Weis, Nina"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-534812"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Covid 19","SARS CoV 2","Vector vaccine","mRNA vaccine"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"analysis of neutralization titers against sars cov 000000000002 in health care workers vaccinated with prime boost mrna mrna or vector mrna covid 000000000019 vaccines","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines10010075","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-534812","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8780959","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85122722452","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35062736","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-ecd110c8-4bf5-4e7e-85cf-1885e4c154c1"],"keyword":["COVID-19","MRNA vaccine","Neutralization/neutralisation","Neutralizing/neutralising antibodies","SARS-CoV-2","Vector vaccine"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5EA73D0353DD51B41F2EB4C302167D02"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/290523899/vaccines_10_00075_v2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/vaccines10010075","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Amager and Hvidovre"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Immunology and Microbiology, Virology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christina","key":"sølundpau","last":"Sølund","name":"Sølund, Christina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Alexander P.","key":"underwoodpau","last":"Underwood","locid":"6bcab5f0-7463-4162-a31c-534dbb306ddb","name":"Underwood, Alexander P.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Carlota","key":"fernandezantunezpau","last":"Fernandez-Antunez","locid":"e181d8b5-a5c0-4346-8817-3c02d8c8a7bd","name":"Fernandez-Antunez, Carlota","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Signe","key":"bolleruppau","last":"Bollerup","locid":"d3485dd3-c29b-42d0-b988-b4dcfb0885eb","name":"Bollerup, Signe","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lotte S.","key":"mikkelsenpau","last":"Mikkelsen","name":"Mikkelsen, Lotte S.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Signe Lysemose","key":"villadsenpau","last":"Villadsen","name":"Villadsen, Signe Lysemose","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Ulrik","key":"fahnøepau","last":"Fahnøe","locid":"af7d26fe-7626-47c3-8851-4c49157a2198","name":"Fahnøe, Ulrik","orcid":"0000-0002-2527-5751","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anni Assing","key":"winckelmannpau","last":"Winckelmann","locid":"7ed79eec-0162-4a30-a993-56388e5e407d","name":"Winckelmann, Anni Assing","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Shan","key":"fengpau","last":"Feng","locid":"87c183e9-1a83-4577-a645-c891d9b47fce","name":"Feng, Shan","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Caroline A.","key":"nørløvvintenpau","last":"Nørløv Vinten","name":"Nørløv Vinten, Caroline A.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Magnus Illum","key":"dalegaardpau","last":"Dalegaard","name":"Dalegaard, Magnus Illum","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Greta","key":"vizgirdapau","last":"Vizgirda","name":"Vizgirda, Greta","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anna Louise","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Anna Louise","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Santseharay","key":"ramirezpau","last":"Ramirez","locid":"ef00eba2-2eb2-46c3-8995-c5809f996734","name":"Ramirez, Santseharay","orcid":"0000-0003-3699-1814","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jens","key":"bukhpau","last":"Bukh","locid":"ed84ce10-678e-4b40-9651-982bf9977abc","name":"Bukh, Jens","orcid":"0000-0002-7815-4806","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Nina","key":"weispau","last":"Weis","locid":"3a88aaad-c729-432b-b725-999664af7a45","name":"Weis, Nina","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35062736"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/vaccines10010075","issn":["2076393X"],"issue":"1","journal":"Vaccines","paperid":"75","status":"p","title":"Vaccines","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85122722452"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-ecd110c8-4bf5-4e7e-85cf-1885e4c154c1"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Analysis of Neutralization Titers against SARS-CoV-2 in Health-Care Workers Vaccinated with Prime-Boost mRNA–mRNA or Vector–mRNA COVID-19 Vaccines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-534812","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The microRNA-29 family members miR-29a-3p, miR-29b-3p, and miR-29c-3p are ubiquitously expressed and consistently increased in various tissues and cell types in conditions of metabolic disease, obesity, insulin resistance, and type 2 diabetes. In pancreatic β cells, miR-29a is required for normal exocytosis, but increased levels are associated with impaired β-cell function. Similarly, in liver, miR-29 species are higher in models of insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, and either knock-out or depletion using a microRNA inhibitor improves hepatic insulin resistance. In skeletal muscle, miR-29 family upregulation is associated with insulin resistance and altered substrate oxidation, and similarly, in adipocytes, overexpression of miR-29a leads to insulin resistance. Blocking miR-29a using nucleic acid antisense therapeutics show promising results in preclinical animal models of obesity and type 2 diabetes, although the widespread expression pattern of miR-29 family members complicates the exploration of single target tissues. However, in fibrotic diseases, such as in late complications of diabetes and metabolic disease (diabetic kidney disease, nonalcoholic steatohepatitis), miR-29 species expression is suppressed by TGF-β allowing increased extracellular matrix collagen to form. In the clinical setting, circulating levels of miR-29a and miR-29b are consistently increased in type 2 diabetes and in gestational diabetes and are also possible prognostic markers for deterioration of glucose tolerance. In conclusion, miR-29 family miRNAs play an essential role in various organs relevant to intermediary metabolism and its upregulation contributes to impaired glucose metabolism, whereas it suppresses fibrosis development. Thus, a correct balance of levels of miR-29 family miRNA seems important for cellular and organ homeostasis in metabolism.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dalgaard, Louise Torp","Sørensen, Anja Elaine","Hardikar, Anandwardhan A.","Joglekar, Mugdha V."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-563720"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Western Sydney University"],"keyword":["Fibrosis","Insulin Resistance","Islet","Obesity","miR 29"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the microrna 000000000029 family role in metabolism and metabolic disease","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-563720","identifier":[{"id":"35704699","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85135596347","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-73652f11-481b-4a97-bf79-6b2b937861d5"],"keyword":["fibrosis","insulin resistance","islet","miR-29","obesity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-59BFBA659BC4820E9DE1F9A41F306D9D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Science and Environment"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"western sydney university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Western Sydney University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Louise T.","key":"dalgaardpau","last":"Dalgaard","locid":"a219fdaf-f994-4a7a-beb8-1f353846ed5a","name":"Dalgaard, Louise Torp","name_org":"Dalgaard, Louise T.","orcid":"0000-0002-3598-2775","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Anja E.","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"7e5d1e2c-5d4b-4436-bb66-acdca4058c08","name":"Sørensen, Anja Elaine","name_org":"Sørensen, Anja E.","orcid":"0000-0001-7557-5376","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","country":"dk","first":"Anandwardhan A.","key":"hardikarpau","last":"Hardikar","locid":"6c535712-be9f-4d60-9fd1-cc6746b35702","name":"Hardikar, Anandwardhan A.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Mugdha V.","key":"joglekarpau","last":"Joglekar","name":"Joglekar, Mugdha V.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35704699"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1152/ajpcell.00051.2022","issn":["15221563"],"issue":"2","journal":"AJP  Cell Physiology","status":"p","title":"AJP  Cell Physiology","title_org":"American journal of physiology. Cell physiology","volume":"323"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85135596347"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-73652f11-481b-4a97-bf79-6b2b937861d5"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"The microRNA-29 family : role in metabolism and metabolic disease","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-563720","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Hypozincemia is a well-known phenomenon in patients with infection caused by the activation of the acute phase response (APR). Zn status is still based upon plasma Zn levels in venous blood samples. Recent trials have questioned the validity of this measurement in infected patients. The aim of this study was to assess plasma levels of Zn, albumin and Zinc-binding capacity in patients during and following infection. Furthermore, to assess if an assay for albumin-corrected Zn could potentially replace or add knowledge to existing tools for assessment of Zinc-status. A prospective clinical observational trial was conducted. Associations between P-Zn, -Albumin, -Albumin-corrected Zn and Zn binding capacity were analyzed. Analyzes were based upon two venous blood samples drawn during and following infection, respectively. Twenty-three patients admitted to a medical ward showing paraclinical signs of infection were included in the study. Significantly lower levels of Zn and albumin were found during infection compared with the levels post-infection. These findings corresponded to the changes found in Zn binding capacity. About 52% of patients were deemed Zn deficient by plasma Zn levels during infection but after applying the correction for P-Albumin, all patients were found to be within normal ranges of Zn levels. Furthermore, we found no statistically significant difference between albumin-corrected Zn during infection and P-Zn post-infection. The new assay was found to accurately estimate the ‘true’ Zn levels in infected patients. Based on our findings, we propose albumin-corrected P-Zn as a promising new tool, which may result in more precise diagnostics and treatment.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hedegaard, Christoffer Viuff","Sørensen, Mia Dahl","Jørgensen, Louise Helskov","Schaffalitzky de Muckadell, Ove B."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-641779"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Schaffalitzky de Muckadell, Ove B."],"keyword":["Albumin","Chelating agents","Humans","Infection","Prospective Studies","Serum Albumin metabolism","Zinc"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"investigating hypozincemia and validity of plasma zinc measurements in infected patients","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85137812738","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-641779","identifier":[{"id":"36062589","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85137812738","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-ef6b8c74-7aeb-4147-9100-75b2ed6e7ca4"],"keyword":["Chelating Agents","Humans","Hypozincemia","Prospective Studies","Serum Albumin/metabolism","albumin","albumin-corrected zinc","infection","zinc","zinc binding capacity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-39DDD7ED6B61A860544E5D1446284E83"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Pathology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoffer Viuff","key":"hedegaardpau","last":"Hedegaard","name":"Hedegaard, Christoffer Viuff","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mia Dahl","key":"soerensenpau","last":"Soerensen","locid":"3524a591-0bd0-4849-b735-4b916b84defb","name":"Sørensen, Mia Dahl","name_org":"Soerensen, Mia Dahl","orcid":"0000-0002-0105-2940","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise Helskov","key":"jorgensenpau","last":"Jorgensen","locid":"9377a3d2-d14c-46cd-9deb-66359f638eea","name":"Jørgensen, Louise Helskov","name_org":"Jorgensen, Louise Helskov","orcid":"0000-0001-9982-1236","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ove B.","key":"schaffalitzkydemuckadellpau","last":"Schaffalitzky de Muckadell","locid":"3bc4ff3b-1f9d-4045-85de-b1d6d16f57f3","name":"Schaffalitzky de Muckadell, Ove B.","orcid":"0000-0003-1969-2329","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36062589"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/00365513.2022.2114935","issn":["00365513","0085591X"],"issn_org":["00365513"],"issue":"5","journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Clinical & Laboratory Investigation","pages":"371-377","publisher":"Informa Healthcare","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Clinical & Laboratory Investigation","volume":"82"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85137812738"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-ef6b8c74-7aeb-4147-9100-75b2ed6e7ca4"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Investigating hypozincemia and validity of plasma zinc measurements in infected patients","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-641779","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection is seen in chronic pulmonary disease and is associated with exacerbations and poor long-term prognosis. However, evidence-based guidelines for the management and treatment of P. aeruginosa infection in chronic, non-cystic fibrosis (CF) pulmonary disease are lacking. The aim of this study is to investigate whether targeted antibiotic treatment against P. aeruginosa can reduce exacerbations and mortality in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), non-CF bronchiectasis, and asthma. METHODS: This study is an ongoing multicenter, randomized, controlled, open-label trial. A total of 150 patients with COPD, non-CF bronchiectasis or asthma, and P. aeruginosa-positive lower respiratory tract samples will be randomly assigned with a 1:1 ratio to either no antibiotic treatment or anti-pseudomonal antibiotic treatment with intravenous beta-lactam and oral ciprofloxacin for 14 days. The primary outcome, analyzed with two co-primary endpoints, is (i) time to prednisolone and/or antibiotic requiring exacerbation or death, in the primary or secondary health sector, within days 20-365 from study allocation and (ii) days alive and without exacerbation within days 20-365 from the study allocation. DISCUSSION: This trial will determine whether targeted antibiotics can benefit future patients with chronic, non-CF pulmonary disease and P. aeruginosa infection in terms of reduced morbidity and mortality, thus optimizing therapeutic approaches in this large group of chronic patients. TRIAL REGISTRATION: ClinicalTrials.gov NCT03262142 . Registered on August 25, 2017.","author_count":45,"contributors":["Eklöf, Josefin","Alispahic, Imane Achir","Sivapalan, Pradeesh","Wilcke, Torgny","Seersholm, Niels","Armbruster, Karin","Kjærgaard, Jakob Lyngby","Saeed, Mohamad Isam","Nielsen, Thyge Lynghøj","Browatzki, Andrea","Overgaard, Rikke Holmen","Fenlev, Camilla Sund","Harboe, Zitta Barella","Andreassen, Helle Frost","Lapperre, Therese Sophie","Pedersen, Lars","Johnsen, Stine","Ulrik, Charlotte Suppli","Janner, Julie","Moberg, Mia","Heidemann, Maria","Weinreich, Ulla Møller","Vijdea, Roxana","Nielsen, Hans Linde","Titlestad, Ingrid","Johansson, Sofie Lock","Rosenvinge, Flemming Schønning","Østergaard, Christian","Ghathian, Khaled Saoud Ali","Gundersen, Lise","Christensen, Christina Wellendorph","Bangsborg, Jette","Jensen, Torben Tranborg","Sørensen, Vibeke Muff","Ellingsgaard, Thilde","Datcu, Raluca","Coia, John Eugenio","Bodtger, Uffe","Jensen, Jens Ulrik Stæhr"],"cuid":["S03-099.97-010803"],"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Alispahic, Imane Achir"],"keyword":["Anti Bacterial Agents adverse effects","Antibiotics","Asthma","Asthma complications","Bronchiectasis diagnosis","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Ciprofloxacin adverse effects","Fibrosis","Humans","Non CF bronchiectasis","Prednisolone therapeutic use","Prognosis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Pulmonary Disease Chronic Obstructive drug therapy","Randomized Controlled Trial","beta Lactams"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["Esbjerg og Grindsted Sygehus"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"targeted antibiotics for chronic pulmonary diseases target abc can targeted antibiotic therapy improve the prognosis of pseudomonas aeruginosa infected patients with chronic pulmonary obstructive disease non cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis and asthma a multicenter randomized controlled open label trial","src":["aau","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access"