{"abstract":"The Arctic responds rapidly to climate change, and the melting of land ice is a major contributor to the observed present-day sea-level rise. The coastal regions of these ice-covered areas are showing the most dramatic changes in the form of widespread thinning. Therefore, it is vital to improve the monitoring of these areas to help us better understand their contribution to present-day sea levels. In this study, we derive ice-surface elevations from the swath processing of CryoSat-2 SARIn data, and evaluate the results in several Arctic regions. In contrast to the conventional retracking of radar data, swath processing greatly enhances spatial coverage as it uses the majority of information in the radar waveform to create a swath of elevation measurements. However, detailed validation procedures for swath-processed data are important to assess the performance of the method. Therefore, a range of validation activities were carried out to evaluate the performance of the swath processor in four different regions in the Arctic. We assessed accuracy by investigating both intramission crossover elevation differences, and comparisons to independent elevation data. The validation data consisted of both air- and spaceborne laser altimetry, and airborne X-band radar data. There were varying elevation biases between CryoSat-2 and the validation datasets. The best agreement was found for CryoSat-2 and ICESat-2 over the Helheim region in June 2019. To test the stability of the swath processor, we applied two different coherence thresholds. The number of data points was increased by approximately 25% when decreasing the coherence threshold in the processor from 0.8 to 0.6. However, depending on the region, this came with the cost of an increase of 33–65% in standard deviation of the intramission differences. Our study highlights the importance of selecting an appropriate coherence threshold for the swath processor. Coherence threshold should be chosen on a case-specific basis depending on the need for enhanced spatial coverage or accuracy.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Andersen, Natalia Havelund","Simonsen, Sebastian Bjerregaard","Winstrup, Mai","Nilsson, Johan","Sørensen, Louise Sandberg"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-309448"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Natalia Havelund"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","California Institute of Technology"],"keyword":["CS2","Swath processing","Validation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"regional assessments of surface ice elevations from swath processed cryosat 000000000002 sarin data","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs13112213","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-309448","identifier":[{"id":"85108459638","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-10166377-bc73-4e02-a412-8d97efb9eff8"],"keyword":["CS2","Ice elevations","Swath processing","Validation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9ED5E26D787FF69BE9283FB6CE7072FE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/247239223/remotesensing_13_02213_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/rs13112213","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Space Institute","Technical University of Denmark, National Space Institute, Geodesy and Earth Observation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"california institute of technology","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"California Institute of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Natalia Havelund","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"c5e34a6f-c973-41a5-8f78-b0ed469d5210","name":"Andersen, Natalia Havelund","orcid":"0000-0003-4257-1242","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian Bjerregaard","key":"simonsenpau","last":"Simonsen","locid":"d088b945-224e-4fd3-a871-022cc71c5cb1","name":"Simonsen, Sebastian Bjerregaard","orcid":"0000-0001-9569-1294","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mai","key":"winstruppau","last":"Winstrup","locid":"5b8bc307-4b30-4668-9e2c-646716b77fcd","name":"Winstrup, Mai","orcid":"0000-0002-4794-4004","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Johan","key":"nilssonpau","last":"Nilsson","name":"Nilsson, Johan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise Sandberg","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"1c6cb90f-da30-48a2-872f-a94b8100749b","name":"Sørensen, Louise Sandberg","orcid":"0000-0002-3771-4061","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/rs13112213","issn":["20724292"],"issue":"11","journal":"Remote Sensing","paperid":"2213","publisher":"MDPI AG","publisher_org":"M D P I AG","status":"p","title":"Remote Sensing","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85108459638"],"sdg":["13. Climate Action","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-10166377-bc73-4e02-a412-8d97efb9eff8"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Regional Assessments of Surface Ice Elevations from Swath-Processed CryoSat-2 SARIn Data","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-309448","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Human milk oligosaccharide supplementation safely modulates fecal bifidobacteria abundance and holds the potential to manage symptoms in irritable bowel syndrome (IBS). Here, we aimed to determine the role of a 4:1 mix of 2′-O-fucosyllactose and lacto-Nneotetraose (2′FL/LNnT) on the modulation of the gut microbiota composition and host mucosal response, as well as the link between the bifidobacteria abundance and metabolite modulation, in IBS patients. Methods: Biological samples were collected from IBS patients (n = 58) at baseline and week 4 post-supplementation with placebo, 5 g or 10 g doses of 2′FL/LNnT. The gut microbiota composition, metabolite profiles and expression of genes related to host mucosal response were determined. Results: Moderate changes in fecal, but not mucosal, microbial composition (βdiversity) was observed during the intervention with higher dissimilarity observed within individuals receiving 10g 2′FL/LNnT compared to placebo. Both fecal and mucosal Bifidobacterium spp. increased after 2′FL/LNnT intake, with increased proportions of Bifidobacterium adolescentis and Bifidobacterium longum. Moreover, the intervention modulated the fecal and plasma metabolite profiles, but not the urine metabolite profile or the host mucosal response. Changes in the metabolite profiles were associated to changes in bifidobacteria abundance. Conclusion: Supplementation with 2′FL/LNnT modulated the gut microbiota, fecal and plasma metabolite profiles, but not the host mucosal response in IBS. Furthermore, the bifidogenic effect was associated with metabolite modulation. Overall, these findings support the assertion that 2′FL/LNnT supplementation modulate the intestinal microenvironment of patients with IBS, potentially related to health.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Iribarren, Cristina","Magnusson, Maria K.","Vigsnaes, Louise Kristine","Aziz, Imran","Amundsen, Ingvild Dybdrodt","Šuligoj, Tanja","Juge, Nathalie","Patel, Piyush","Sapnara, Maria","Johnsen, Lea","Sørensen, Nikolaj","Sundin, Johanna","Törnblom, Hans","Simrén, Magnus","Öhman, Lena"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-389489"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Öhman, Lena"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Norwich Research Park","University of Gothenburg","University of North Carolina","University of Sheffield"],"keyword":["2 O fucosyllactose","Human milk oligosaccharides","Irritable bowel syndrome","Lacto N neotetraose","Metabolomics","Microbiota","clinical assessment methods lab technology and radiography"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-UC"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effects of human milk oligosaccharides on gut microbiota metabolite profiles and host mucosal response in patients with irritable bowel syndrome","src":["kp"],"src-code":["DI-UC-KP"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu13113836","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding: The study was funded by Swedish Medical Research Council (grants no. 2019-01052 (L.Ö.) and 2018-02566 (M.S.)), Sahlgrenska Academy at University of Gothenburg (L.Ö.), Faculty of Medicine at University of Gothenburg (M.S.), grants from the Swedish state under the agreement between the Swedish government and the county councils; the ALF-agreement, ALFGBG-723921 (L.Ö.), (ALFGBG-722331 (M.S.)) and Regional Executive Board, Region Västra Götaland (grant no. VGFOUREG-931919 (L.Ö.)). The study was sponsored by DSM (previously Glycom A/S), Denmark (T.S., N.J., M.S. (Magnus Simrén))."],"id":"S0