{"abstract":"Background: Inflammatory arthritis including rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and spondyloarthritis (SpA) is characterized by inflammation and destruction of the joints. Approximately one third of patients do not respond to first-line treatments. Nitro-fatty acids are bioactive lipids with anti-inflammatory properties and tissue-protective functions. The nitro-fatty acid 10-NO2-oleic acid (10-NO2-OA) is being tested in clinical trials for patients with fibrotic and inflammatory conditions. Here, we tested whether 10-NO2-OA could inhibit immune reactions involved in the inflammatory and joint destructive processes in inflammatory arthritis. Methods: Synovial fluid and blood samples were obtained from 14 patients with active RA or SpA. The in vitro models consisted of synovial fluid mononuclear cells (SFMCs) cultured for 48 h, SFMCs cultured for 21 days, and fibroblast-like synovial cells (FLSs) co-cultured with peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) for 48 h. Cells were treated with or without 10-NO2-OA or the tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNFα) inhibitor etanercept. Supernatants were analyzed for type I interferon, monocyte chemoattractant protein-1 (MCP-1), matrix metalloproteinase 3 (MMP3) and tartrate resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP). Results: In SFMCs cultured for 48 h, 10-NO2-OA dose-dependently decreased the secretion of bioactive type I interferons and MCP-1 but not MMP3 (P = 0.032, P = 0.0001, and P = 0.58, respectively). Both MCP-1 and MMP3 were decreased by etanercept (P = 0.0031 and P = 0.026, respectively). In SFMCs cultured for 21 days, 10-NO2-OA significantly decreased the production of MCP-1 but not TRAP (P = 0.027 and P = 0.1523, respectively). Etanercept decreased the production of TRAP but not MCP-1 (P < 0.001 and P = 0.84, respectively). In co-cultures of FLSs and PBMCs, 10-NO2-OA decreased the production of MCP-1 (P < 0.0001). This decrease in MCP-1 production was not seen with etanercept treatment (P = 0.47). Conclusion: 10-NO2-OA decreased the release of MCP-1 in three models of inflammatory arthritis. Of particular interest, 10-NO2-OA inhibited type I interferon, and 10-NO2-OA was more effective in reducing MCP-1 production in cultures dominated by FLSs compared with etanercept. Our results encourage clinical investigations of 10-NO2-OA in patients with inflammatory arthritis.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Hansen, Anne Louise","Jensen, Lea Skovmand","Sørensen, Anne Sofie","Hundahl, M. P.","Lomholt, Søren","Holm, Christian K","Kragstrup, Tue Wenzel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-250656"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Kragstrup, Tue Wenzel"],"keyword":["Arthritis","Autoimmunity","Inflammation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nitro fatty acids decrease type i interferons and monocyte chemoattractant protein 000000000001 in ex vivo models of inflammatory arthritis","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12865-021-00471-3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-250656","identifier":[{"id":"000731257300002","type":"wos"},{"id":"85121469450","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8684285","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34920714","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-d47c5ee4-c7dd-4cb3-bfa6-d61646df2434"],"keyword":["Antirheumatic drug","Arthritis","Autoimmunity","Immunosuppressive drug","Inflammation","Nitro-fatty acid"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-257DBAE3B7F644548C1A12B3FDC61DBC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12865-021-00471-3","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine, Department of Biomedicine - Forskning og uddannelse, Skou-bygningen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"A. L.","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"8f179233-a9d2-47cf-97ea-f229b3fd76e6","name":"Hansen, Anne Louise","name_org":"Hansen, A. L.","orcid":"0000-0002-3741-8705","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"L. S. J.","key":"rahbekpau","last":"Rahbek","locid":"fa6fc4b5-33c5-4c88-ba19-5ddfc458ed42","name":"Jensen, Lea Skovmand","name_org":"Rahbek, L. S. J.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"A. S.","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"af561ec5-e4d0-48ec-9462-3e7b00c11efc","name":"Sørensen, Anne Sofie","name_org":"Sørensen, A. S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"M. P.","key":"hundahlpau","last":"Hundahl","name":"Hundahl, M. P.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"S.","key":"lomholtpau","last":"Lomholt","locid":"c0c121b3-43bf-4902-9ba6-4e7e5aa0bb50","name":"Lomholt, Søren","name_org":"Lomholt, S.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"C. K.","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"4b713f1e-9aa5-4065-9c2e-9de76ee44fd4","name":"Holm, Christian K","name_org":"Holm, C. K.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tue W.","key":"kragstruppau","last":"Kragstrup","locid":"e1e2fde6-99ce-4d01-b3d4-5c8160d80136","name":"Kragstrup, Tue Wenzel","name_org":"Kragstrup, Tue W.","orcid":"0000-0002-6439-397X","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34920714"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s12865-021-00471-3","issn":["14712172"],"journal":"BMC Immunology","paperid":"77","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"BMC Immunology","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121469450"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-d47c5ee4-c7dd-4cb3-bfa6-d61646df2434"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Nitro-fatty acids decrease type I interferons and monocyte chemoattractant protein 1 in ex vivo models of inflammatory arthritis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-250656","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Background: Given the importance of neutralising antibodies in protection against SARS-CoV-2 infection, it is critical to assess neutralisation persistence long-term following recovery. This study investigated neutralisation titres against SARS-CoV-2 up to 6 months post-symptom onset in individuals with mild COVID-19. Methods: Plasma neutralisation titres in convalescent COVID-19 individuals were determined at baseline and 6 months post-symptom onset using a cell culture infectious SARS-CoV-2 assay. Total SARS-CoV-2 spike-specific IgG and IgA binding was measured using a lectin capture ELISA and compared between timepoints and correlated to neutralising titres. Findings: All 48 convalescent COVID-19 individuals were found to have detectable SARS-CoV-2 50% inhibitory dilution neutralisation titres (ID50) at baseline and 6 months post-symptom onset with mean ID50 of 1/943 and 1/411, respectively. SARS-CoV-2 neutralisation titres peaked within 1-2 months post-symptom onset. However, 50% of individuals showed comparable ID50 at baseline and 6 months post-symptom onset. Both SARS-CoV-2 spike-specific IgG and IgA levels correlated well with neutralising titres. IgG binding was found to be sustained up to 6 months post-symptom onset, whereas IgA levels declined. Interpretation: This study demonstrates durability of SARS-CoV-2 spike-specific IgG and neutralisation responses following recovery from mild COVID-19. Thus, all subjects included in this study might potentially have protective levels of neutralising antibodies 6 months post-symptom onset. This study also demonstrates a relationship between spike-specific IgA and neutralisation decline, with implications for long-term protection against SARS-CoV-2 infection. Funding: Novo Nordisk Foundation, Independent Research Fund Denmark and Danish Agency for Science and Higher Education.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Underwood, Alexander P.","Sølund, Christina","Fernandez-Antunez, Carlota","Villadsen, Signe Lysemose","Winckelmann, Anni Assing","Bollerup, Signe","Mikkelsen, Lotte S.","Sørensen, Anna Louise","Feng, Shan","Fahnøe, Ulrik","Lassauniere, Ria","Fomsgaard, Anders","Ramirez, Santseharay","Weis, Nina","Bukh, Jens"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-429507"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Covid 19","IgA","IgG","Neutralising antibodies","SARS CoV 2","neutralisation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"neutralisation titres against sars cov 000000000002 are sustained 000000000006 months after onset of symptoms in individuals with mild covid 000000000019","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ebiom.2021.103519","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-429507","identifier":[{"id":"85113156129","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34419923","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8375401","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-3c5c1509-7deb-4517-8d3f-ed58d04047d5"],"keyword":["COVID-19","IgA","IgG","SARS-CoV-2","neutralisation","neutralising antibodies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BCCB24E8A4A610416B01BDBAD1A0397B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/278482354/1_s2.0_S2352396421003121_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ebiom.2021.103519","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2"