68bc422-5767-457d-8f5e-936d7ecefa88","name":"Pedersen, Estrid Thougaard","orcid":"0000-0002-4270-3535","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louise Helskov","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"9377a3d2-d14c-46cd-9deb-66359f638eea","name":"Jørgensen, Louise Helskov","orcid":"0000-0001-9982-1236","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sergei","key":"nedospasovpau","last":"Nedospasov","name":"Nedospasov, Sergei","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ditte Caroline","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"884ca799-a3a4-4b7c-8b36-270846e5b8f9","name":"Andersen, Ditte Caroline","orcid":"0000-0003-4940-7912","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jane","key":"stubbepau","last":"Stubbe","locid":"f6c78cb4-b790-4690-b62e-fee659666775","name":"Stubbe, Jane","orcid":"0000-0002-2703-453X","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Roberta","key":"brambillapau","last":"Brambilla","locid":"8ab82da0-55ce-44ab-b8f2-48b67615b9c3","name":"Brambilla, Roberta","orcid":"0000-0001-5792-7497","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Matilda","key":"degnpau","last":"Degn","name":"Degn, Matilda","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kate Lykke","key":"lambertsenpau","last":"Lambertsen","locid":"d867cc02-bda7-455d-8d02-8b602e35e7b3","name":"Lambertsen, Kate Lykke","orcid":"0000-0001-9276-4902","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33153044"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/cells9112407","issn":["20734409"],"issue":"11","journal":"Cells","paperid":"2407","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","status":"p","title":"Cells","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85095739894"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-bd8018e1-835a-4c77-aff4-de86b80805ea"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Conditional Ablation of Myeloid TNF Improves Functional Outcome and Decreases Lesion Size after Spinal Cord Injury in Mice","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-627549","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Background: In Denmark emergency medical technicians (EMTs) transport patients with acute COPD exacerbations to the emergency department (ED). From a clinical and economic perspective, this transport and assessment at the hospital may be inconvenient if the patient is immediately discharged from the ED. We established an EMT point-of-care diagnostics program of patients with COPD with use of ultrasound and blood analysis. Patients’ perspectives on treatment at home and sense of security are important in order to qualify clinical practice.Aim and Objectives: To explore patient’s and relatives’ experience of treatment at home during emergency calls due to COPD in exacerbation and to investigate their attitude towards avoiding hospitalization as well as experience of stress during point-of-care diagnostics in their own home.Method: A qualitative study comprising semi-structured interviews with 19 patients was conducted. Data was analysed inspired by Kvale et al. focusing on the patient’s views on point-of-care diagnostics and treatment of their COPD in acute exacerbation.Results: The interviews revealed that in order to ensure an experience of quality in the assessment and treatment of patients in their own homes, it was important that the ambulance staff showed great safety and experience in the use of the technical equipment and treatment of dyspnea. It was also of importance that the patients felt confident that their general practitioner followed up on the home treatment initiated.Conclusion: Point-of-care diagnostics and treatment of acute COPD exacerbations was considered a qualitative service by the patients and their relatives.FootnotesCite this article as: European Respiratory Journal 2020; 56: Suppl. 64, 5171.This abstract was presented at the 2020 ERS International Congress, in session textquotedblleftRespiratory viruses in the \"pre COVID-19\" eratextquotedblright.This is an ERS International Congress abstract. No full-text version is available. Further material to accompany this abstract may be available at www.ers-education.org (ERS member access only).","author_count":11,"contributors":["Christensen, Helle Marie","Laursen, Christian B","Wittrock, Daniel","Nadim, Giti","Jørgensen, Gitte","Nielsen, Lars B","Sørensen, Michael K","Heerema, Caroline","Titlestad, Ingrid Louise","Lassen, Annmarie Touborg","Mikkelsen, Søren"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-627682"],"event":{"end":"2020-09-09","place":"Virtuel","start":"2020-09-06","title":"ERS International Congress 2020","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"patients perspectives on point of care diagnostics and treatment by emergency medical technicians in acute copd exacerbations a qualitative study","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-627682","ids":["sdu-bdfc4bdb-9a79-4d1a-aeb7-27fd0c33d129"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FD2635D92A02C59C4E728A4E306BA109"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Odense Respiratory Research Unit (ODIN)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Helle Marie","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"dec4ebf0-8630-4645-9129-859c046769e7","name":"Christensen, Helle Marie","orcid":"0000-0001-6783-4112","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian B","key":"laursenpau","last":"Laursen","locid":"333fc524-a408-42f4-885a-0fee79e488b9","name":"Laursen, Christian B","orcid":"0000-0001-6382-9906","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"wittrockpau","last":"Wittrock","locid":"814a1c3f-d86f-459a-93c4-1cb18d4dc813","name":"Wittrock, Daniel","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Giti","key":"nadimpau","last":"Nadim","locid":"3999e047-8c43-4732-a2e6-1a8d4dc40adb","name":"Nadim, Giti","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gitte","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"df782f47-8d2c-4541-b223-8bf7f07a815e","name":"Jørgensen, Gitte","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars B","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Lars B","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael K","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Michael K","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Caroline","key":"heeremapau","last":"Heerema","name":"Heerema, Caroline","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingrid L","key":"titlestadpau","last":"Titlestad","locid":"67905117-c8b6-4567-b11a-11abb0621cc2","name":"Titlestad, Ingrid Louise","name_org":"Titlestad, Ingrid L","orcid":"0000-0001-6418-0088","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Annmarie T","key":"lassenpau","last":"Lassen","locid":"80e651b5-e70f-43e0-baf8-e88335ac10b8","name":"Lassen, Annmarie Touborg","name_org":"Lassen, Annmarie T","orcid":"0000-0003-4942-6152","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"mikkelsenpau","last":"Mikkelsen","locid":"7df86a29-c6dc-4919-a9f9-6f5c23591e1d","name":"Mikkelsen, Søren","orcid":"0000-0002-5187-7027","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1183/13993003.congress-2020.5171","issn":["09031936","13993003"],"issn_org":["09031936"],"issue":"Suppl. 64","journal":"European Respiratory Journal","paperid":"5171","publisher":"European Respiratory Society","status":"p","title":"European Respiratory Journal","volume":"56"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-bdfc4bdb-9a79-4d1a-aeb7-27fd0c33d129"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Patients’ perspectives on point-of-care diagnostics and treatment by emergency medical technicians in acute COPD exacerbations - a Qualitative study","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-627682","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The Greenland Ice Sheet has been a major contributor to global sea-level rise in recent decades1,2, and it is expected to continue to be so3. Although increases in glacier flow4,5,6 and surface melting7,8,9 have been driven by oceanic10,11,12 and atmospheric13,14 warming, the magnitude and trajectory of the ice sheet’s mass imbalance remain uncertain. Here we compare and combine 26 individual satellite measurements of changes in the ice sheet’s volume, flow and gravitational potential to produce a reconciled estimate of its mass balance. The ice sheet was close to a state of balance in the 1990s, but annual losses have risen since then, peaking at 345 ± 66 billion tonnes per year in 2011. In all, Greenland lost 3,902 ± 342 billion tonnes of ice between 1992 and 2018, causing the mean sea level to rise by 10.8 ± 0.9 millimetres. Using three regional climate models, we show that the reduced surface mass balance has driven 1,964 ± 565 billion tonnes (50.3 per cent) of the ice loss owing to increased meltwater runoff. The remaining 1,938 ± 541 billion tonnes (49.7 per cent) of ice loss was due to increased glacier dynamical imbalance, which rose from 46 ± 37 billion tonnes per year in the 1990s to 87 ± 25 billion tonnes per year since then. The total rate of ice loss slowed to 222 ± 30 billion tonnes per year between 2013 and 2017, on average, as atmospheric circulation favoured cooler conditions15 and ocean temperatures fell at the terminus of Jakobshavn Isbræ16. Cumulative ice losses from Greenland as a whole have been close to the rates predicted by the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change for their high-end climate warming scenario17, which forecast an additional 70 to 130 millimetres of global sea-level rise by 2100 compared with their central estimate.","author_count":91,"contributors":["Shepherd, Andrew","Ivins, Erik","Rignot, Eric","Smith, Ben","van den Broeke, Michiel","Velicogna, Isabella","Whitehouse, Pippa","Briggs, Kate","Joughin, Ian","Kr