","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31632721"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41394-019-0210-z","issn":["20586124"],"issue":"1","journal":"Spinal Cord Series and Cases","paperid":"63","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"Spinal Cord Series and Cases","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85073590491"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-cdaf016f-87e0-4b16-9f7d-2419cc747f78","ku-c8d7be50-3a29-4bcb-bd8c-6a0bffb70642"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Version 2.1 of the International Spinal Cord Injury Bowel Function Basic Data Set","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-092.68-027591","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Pore development in natural porous media, as a result of mineral dissolution in flowing fluid, generates complex microstructures. Although the underlying dynamics of fluid flow and the kinetics of the dissolution reactions have been carefully analyzed in many scenarios, it remains interesting to ask if the preferentially developed flow paths share certain general petrophysical properties. Here we combine in situ X‐ray imaging with network modeling to study pore development in chalk driven by acidic fluid flow under ambient condition. We show that the trajectory of a growing pore correlates with the flow path that minimizes cumulative surface—the overall surface area available to fluid within the residence time—calculated along streamlines. This correlation is not a coincidence because cumulative surface determines conversion of reactant and thus defines the position of dissolution front. Model simulations show that, as fluid channelizes, the growth of the leading pore in the flow direction is guided by migration of the most far‐reaching dissolution front, even in an ever‐changing flow field. In addition, we present a complete tomographic time series of microstructure erosion and show a good accord between the in situ observation and the model simulation. Our results suggest that the microscopic pore development is a deterministic process while being sensitive to stochastic perturbations to the migrating dissolution front.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Yang, Yibo","Hakim, S. S.","Bruns, S.","Uesugi, K.","Stipp, S. L. S.","Sørensen, H. O."],"cuid":["S02-095.92-037907"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Yang, Yibo"],"country":["Japan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Japan Synchrotron Radiation Research Institute"],"keyword":["Chalk","Microstructure evolution","Tomography"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effect of cumulative surface on pore development in chalk","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2018WR023756","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.92-037907","identifier":[{"id":"85061204936","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000477616900017","type":"wos"}],"ids":["dtu-eefde55a-a2d3-4a5a-97f0-4225d5ec0a1f","ku-8e932987-60c0-49e1-8a84-1ea32aed63bd"],"keyword":["chalk","cumulative surface","dissolution front","microstructure evolution","tomography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-5B3C3BEA7EF2A7D59D933CB69FD603F7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2018WR023756","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/186060957/Yang_et_al_2019_Water_Resources_Research.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/243151818/2018WR023756.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/183362294/BERAT_Yang_et_al_2019_Water_Resources_Research.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Chemistry, Department of Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"jp","key":"japan synchrotron radiation research institute","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Japan Synchrotron Radiation Research Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Y.","key":"yangpau","last":"Yang","locid":"d5d0afa8-3422-4740-b6ac-17b575460db5","name":"Yang, Yibo","name_org":"Yang, Y.","orcid":"0000-0003-2533-9126","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"S. S.","key":"hakimpau","last":"Hakim","locid":"60c6860c-4c94-4b91-95ff-45e802cdc01a","name":"Hakim, Sepideh Sadat","name_org":"Hakim, S. S.","orcid":"0000-0001-6486-4551","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"S.","key":"brunspau","last":"Bruns","locid":"9da9f30d-3a37-404e-9bf2-69c4db15ebe3","name":"Bruns, Stefan","name_org":"Bruns, S.","orcid":"0000-0003-1945-0549","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"K.","key":"uesugipau","last":"Uesugi","name":"Uesugi, K.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"S. L. S.","key":"stipppau","last":"Stipp","locid":"b907e7ef-d26a-4093-836b-9b7c9238d956","name":"Stipp, Susan Louise Svane","name_org":"Stipp, S. L. S.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"H. O.","key":"sorensenpau","last":"Sorensen","locid":"e1bf7bae-7f0e-445e-99ea-12eb242407fb","name":"Sørensen, Henning Osholm","name_org":"Sorensen, H. O.","orcid":"0000-0002-7004-547X","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1029/2018wr023756","issn":["00431397","19447973"],"issn_org":["00431397"],"issue":"6","journal":"Water Resources Research","pages":"4801-4819","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Water Resources Research","volume":"55"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061204936"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-eefde55a-a2d3-4a5a-97f0-4225d5ec0a1f","ku-8e932987-60c0-49e1-8a84-1ea32aed63bd"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Effect of Cumulative Surface on Pore Development in Chalk","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.92-037907","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Data regarding high-sensitivity troponin concentrations in patients presenting to the emergency department with symptoms suggestive of myocardial infarction may be useful in determining the probability of myocardial infarction and subsequent 30-day outcomes. METHODS: In 15 international cohorts of patients presenting to the emergency department with symptoms suggestive of myocardial infarction, we determined the concentrations of high-sensitivity troponin I or high-sensitivity troponin T at presentation and after early or late serial sampling. The diagnostic and prognostic performance of multiple high-sensitivity troponin cutoff combinations was assessed with the use of a derivation-validation design. A risk-assessment tool that was based on these data was developed to estimate the risk of index myocardial infarction and of subsequent myocardial infarction or death at 30 days. RESULTS: Among 22,651 patients (9604 in the derivation data set and 13,047 in the validation data set), the prevalence of myocardial infarction was 15.3%. Lower high-sensitivity troponin concentrations at presentation and smaller absolute changes during serial sampling were associated with a lower likelihood of myocardial infarction and a lower short-term risk of cardiovascular events. For example, high-sensitivity troponin I concentrations of less than 6 ng per liter and an absolute change of less than 4 ng per liter after 45 to 120 minutes (early serial sampling) resulted in a negative predictive value of 99.5% for myocardial infarction, with an associated 30-day risk of subsequent myocardial infarction or death of 0.2%; a total of 56.5% of the patients would be classified as being at low risk. These findings were confirmed in an external validation data set. CONCLUSIONS: A risk-assessment tool, which we developed to integrate the high-sensitivity troponin I or troponin T concentration at emergency department presentation, its dynamic change during serial sampling, and the time between the obtaining of samples, was used to estimate the probability of myocardial infarction on emergency department presentation and 30-day outcomes. (Funded by the German Center for Cardiovascular Research [DZHK]; ClinicalTrials.gov numbers, NCT00470587, NCT02355457, NCT01852123, NCT01994577, and NCT03227159; and Australian New Zealand Clinical Trials Registry numbers, ACTRN12611001069943, ACTRN12610000766011, ACTRN12613000745741, and ACTRN12611000206921.).","author_count":54,"contributors":["Neumann, Johannes T","Twerenbold, Raphael","Ojeda, Francisco","Sörensen, Nils A","Chapman, Andrew R","Shah, Anoop S V","Anand, Atul","Boeddinghaus, Jasper","Nestelberger, Thomas","Badertscher, Patrick","Mokhtari, Arash","Pickering, John W","Troughton, Richard W","Greenslade, Jaimi","Parsonage, William","Mueller-Hennessen, Matthias","Gori, Tommaso","Jernberg, Tomas","Morris, Niall","Liebetrau, Christoph","Hamm, Christian","Katus, Hugo A","Münzel, Thomas","Landmesser, Ulf","Salomaa, Veikko","Iacoviello, Licia","Ferrario, Marco M","Giampaoli, Simona","Kee, Frank","Thorand, Barbara","Peters, Annette","Borchini, Rossana","Jørgensen, Torben","Söderberg, Stefan","Sans, Susana","Tunstall-Pedoe, Hugh","Kuulasmaa, Kari","Renné, Thomas","Lackner, Karl J","Worster, Andrew","Body, Richard","Ekelund, Ulf","Kavsak, Peter A","Keller, Till","Lindahl, Bertil","Wild, Philipp","Giannitsis, Evangelos","Than, Martin","Cullen, Louise A","Mills, Nicholas L","Mueller, Christian","Zeller, Tanja","Westermann, Dirk","Blankenberg, Stefan"],"cuid":["S02-099.17-006935"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Canada","Finland","Germany","Italy","New Zealand","Spain","Sweden","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","British Heart Foundation","Cardiology I","