{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Khateb, Heba","Sørensen, Rasmus Schøler","Kjems, Jørgen","Sutherland, Duncan S"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-149786"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corpau":["Rikke Louise Meyer"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the role of nanoscale distribution of fibronectin in staphylococcus aureus adhesion to interfaces and the multivalency in fn fbps interaction utilizing the dna paint","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-149786","ids":["au-23a67e25-4fe8-4573-8f4b-5ee9194882b5"],"keyword":["Protein pattern, Fibronectin, FnBPs and S.aureus adhesion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B95875210B0D5B40CF0D36F0608BE1CF"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center, Interdisciplinary Nanoscience Center - INANO-MBG, iNANO-huset"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":2,"key":"rikke louise meyer","name":"Rikke Louise Meyer","role":"oau"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Heba","key":"khatebpau","last":"Khateb","locid":"d98c6ee8-14ca-4dd9-afbb-71f6f71bed40","name":"Khateb, Heba","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Schøler","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"dd13ff30-6bd7-4512-bc60-efa5c5b0ebdd","name":"Sørensen, Rasmus Schøler","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jørgen","key":"kjemspau","last":"Kjems","locid":"f5915d75-aca3-437a-9a9d-f4fdfef7accd","name":"Kjems, Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0003-4128-9317","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Duncan S","key":"sutherlandpau","last":"Sutherland","locid":"5c5c563f-ca05-4219-b60d-8a3d6303ff2f","name":"Sutherland, Duncan S","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"25","status":"u"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Unpublished"]},"sid":["au-23a67e25-4fe8-4573-8f4b-5ee9194882b5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The Role of Nanoscale Distribution of Fibronectin in Staphylococcus aureus Adhesion to Interfaces and the Multivalency in Fn-FBPs interaction utilizing the DNA-PAINT.","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-149786","year":"2019","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The disease severity of non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and the distinction between simple steatosis and non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) are based on the pathohistological presence of steatosis, inflammation, ballooning and fibrosis. However, little is known about the relation between such structural changes and the function of the afflicted liver. AIMS: To investigate in vivo effects of hepatic fat fraction, ballooning and fibrosis on regional and whole liver metabolic function assessed by galactose elimination in NASH and simple steatosis. METHODS: Twenty-five biopsy-proven, nondiabetic patients with NAFLD (13 NASH with low-grade fibrosis, 12 simple steatosis with no fibrosis) underwent 2-[18 F]fluoro-2-deoxy-d-galactose positron emission tomography and magnetic resonance imaging-derived proton density fat fraction of the liver. Nine healthy persons were included as controls. RESULTS: In the NASH patients, the standardised hepatic uptake of 2-[18 F]fluoro-2-deoxy-d-galactose was reduced to 13.5 (95% confidence interval, 12.1-14.9) as compared with both simple steatosis and controls (16.4 (15.6-17.1), P < 0.001). Thus, the NASH patients had reduced regional metabolic liver function. The liver fat fraction diluted the standardised uptake equally in NASH and simple steatosis but the fibrosis and ballooning of NASH were associated with a further decrease. Moreover, the NASH livers exhibited increased variation in their standardised uptake values (coefficient of variation 13.8% vs 11.6% in simple steatosis and 10.2% in controls, P = 0.02), reflecting an increased functional heterogeneity. CONCLUSIONS: In NASH, the regional metabolic liver function was lower and more heterogeneous than in both simple steatosis and healthy controls. Thus, NASH disturbs the normal homogeneous metabolic function of the liver.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Eriksen, Peter Lykke","Thomsen, Karen Louise","Larsen, Lars P","Grønbæk, Henning","Vilstrup, Hendrik","Sørensen, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-194886"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Disease","Epidemiology","Hepatic steatosis","In vivo","Microcirculation","Progression","Validation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"non alcoholic steatohepatitis but not simple steatosis disturbs the functional homogeneity of the liver a human galactose positron emission tomography study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85066046297","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-194886","identifier":[{"id":"31099410","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85066046297","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000474260300008","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-72ccb078-7e4e-415f-b1ff-5c8a908056d0"],"keyword":["DENSITY FAT FRACTION","DISEASE","ELIMINATION CAPACITY","EPIDEMIOLOGY","HEPATIC STEATOSIS","IN-VIVO","MICROCIRCULATION","PROGRESSION","STANDARD UPTAKE","VALIDATION"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0B9BCEF2650EEBC7B13B552F0876D383"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Hepatology and Gastroenterology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter Lykke","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","locid":"02b5f3f7-8692-403b-9a17-1dc588d638e7","name":"Eriksen, Peter Lykke","orcid":"0000-0003-1115-8308","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen Louise","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"32baa31f-0b93-4c6d-af7e-0da0519f574f","name":"Thomsen, Karen Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Peter","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"814320d4-becd-4a70-9f63-97d3285e8f08","name":"Larsen, Lars P","name_org":"Larsen, Lars Peter","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henning","key":"grønbaekpau","last":"Grønbaek","locid":"32aaf9c5-5cf0-4e7b-86d8-a534fa4a9ae9","name":"Grønbæk, Henning","name_org":"Grønbaek, Henning","orcid":"0000-0001-8998-7910","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hendrik","key":"vilstruppau","last":"Vilstrup","locid":"9e1905f3-3094-4aea-bbde-7178733bf6cb","name":"Vilstrup, Hendrik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Michael","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"52635821-2c0d-418e-848d-e3e1f1175e12","name":"Sørensen, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-8750-4329","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31099410"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/apt.15293","issn":["02692813","09530673"],"issn_org":["02692813"],"issue":"1","journal":"Alimentary Pharmacology and Therapeutics","pages":"84-92","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Alimentary Pharmacology and Therapeutics","volume":"50"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85066046297"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-72ccb078-7e4e-415f-b1ff-5c8a908056d0"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis, but not simple steatosis, disturbs the functional homogeneity of the liver - a human galactose positron emission tomography study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-194886","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"We compared 6 frequently used mouse blood-sampling methods (lateral tail incision; tail-tip amputation; sublingual, submandibular, and saphenous vein puncture; and retrobulbar sinus puncture during isoflurane anesthesia) with regard to induction of local and systemic inflammation, stomach contents, weight changes, and corticosterone levels at 6 h to 12 d after sampling. Local inflammation was assessed through histopathology and assessment of the expression of inflammation and tissue damage–related genes (S1008/9A, Cxcl2, Il1b, Nlrp3, Il6, and Il33) in sampled tissue. Systemic inflammation was assessed through quantification of plasma haptoglobin levels, measurement of blood Il1b expression, and evaluation of histopathologic changes in lung, kidney, liver, and spleen. Apart from slight, transient increases in plasma haptoglobin levels after lateral tail incision, retrobulbar sinus puncture, and saphenous vein puncture, no other signs of systemic inflammation were found. Mice subjected to retrobulbar sinus puncture, sublingual puncture, or isoflurane anesthesia only showed the highest plasma corticosterone concentrations. Retrobulbar sinus puncture had the largest effect on body weight loss. Retrobulbar sinus puncture, sublingual puncture, and submandibular puncture only showed minor and in, most cases, fastresolving inflammation. By contrast, blood sampling by lateral tail incision, tail-tip amputation, or saphenous vein puncture caused tissue damage and inflammation locally at the sampling site, which resolved more slowly compared with head-region sampling techniques, according to results from pathologic and gene expression assessments. Expression of S1008/9A, Cxcl2, Il1b, and Nlrp3 increased 10- to 1000-fold and did not return to baseline until day 6 after sampling or later and did not resolve after tail-tip amputation within the 12-d observation period. Increased expression of genes involved in inflammation and tissue repair correlated with histopathologic changes and may thus represent a quantitative supplement to histopathology. In conclusion, none of the tested methods for obtaining blood samples from mice is superior, according to simultaneous immunologic, histopathologic, and animal welfare–related parameters.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Sørensen, Dorte Bratbo","Metzdorff, Stine Broeng","Jensen, Louise Kruse","Andersen, Kamilla H","Teilmann, Anne Charlotte","Jensen, Henrik Elvang","Frøkiær, Hanne"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-421547"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"time dependent pathologic and inflammatory consequences of various blood sampling techniques in mice","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"http://europepmc.org/Copyright","type":"rem","url":"http://europepmc.org/articles/pmc6526490?pdf=render","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-421547","identifier":[{"id":"PMC6526490","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"30947767","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85066163615","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-2f287f4c-838b-4ee4-ad5f-e40b85151092"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A2AD47FD3C4D5E04FCB27ABE56F66C9A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"http://europepmc.org/Copyright","type":"rem","url":"http://europepmc.org/articles/pmc6526490?pdf=render","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Preclinical Disease Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Dorte Bratbo","key":"sørensendortebratbopau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"b4b02b58-e75f-4c0d-a4ce-907b714484f4","name":"Sørensen, Dorte Bratbo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Stine Broeng","key":"metzdorffstinebroengpau","last":"Metzdorff","locid":"b82968ae-8f89-4370-875c-fcb8f227295b","name":"Metzdorff, Stine Broeng","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Louise Kruse","key":"jensenlouisekrusepau","last":"Jensen","locid":"1edba00c-b457-4660-85a3-24c07c4919f6","name":"Jensen, Louise Kruse","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kamilla H","key":"andersenkamillahpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Kamilla H","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anne Charlotte","key":"teilmannannecharlottepau","last":"Teilmann","locid":"972438c3-e561-4e92-8948-7dd24525e9c0","name":"Teilmann, Anne Charlotte","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik Elvang","key":"jensenhenrikelvangpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"20245e41-1eb4-4cfa-a2d6-e6851e101300","name":"Jensen, Henrik Elvang","orcid":"0000-0001-7277-7829","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hanne","key":"frøkiærhannepau","last":"Frøkiær","locid":"67cbee0f-a387-4ed0-865d-a73419550125","name":"Frøkiær, Hanne","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30947767"],"pub":{"doi":"10.30802/aalas-jaalas-18-000064","issn":["15596109"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of the American Association for Laboratory Animal Science","pages":"362-372","status":"p","title":"Journal of the American Association for Laboratory Animal Science","title_org":"JAALAS - Journal of the American Association for Laboratory Animal Science","volume":"58"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85066163615"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-2f287f4c-838b-4ee4-ad5f-e40b85151092"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Time-dependent Pathologic and Inflammatory Consequences of Various Blood Sampling Techniques in Mice","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-421547","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Aim. To describe differences in healing time of diabetic foot ulcers for patients treated at the Copenhagen Wound Healing Center, Bispebjerg Hospital, between the years 1999/2000 and 2011/2012. The Center is highly specialized and receives diabetes patients with hard-to-heal foot ulcers. A further aim is to attempt to find predictors of healing time of diabetic foot ulcers. Methods. A retrospective descriptive study of records from patients with diabetic foot ulcer treated at the Copenhagen Wound Healing Center in 1999, 2000, 2011, or 2012. Follow-up data was collected until the 3rd of August 2018. Results. Median time (range) to healing was 6 (61.3) months in 1999/2000 and 6.6 (67.8) in 2011/2012 (p=0.2). About 33% of ulcers were healed, 17% were minor or major amputated, and 1.5% were dead within one year in 1999/2000, whereas 30% of ulcers were healed (p=0.6), 14% were amputated (p=0.2), and 12.8% were dead within one year in 2011/2012 (p<0.001). The single factor found significantly associated with longer ulcer duration was infection. Related to shorter ulcer duration were toe localization of the ulcer and good glycemic control. Conclusion. The median time to healing of a diabetic foot ulcer was long, around 6 months and with a high recurrence rate in 1999/2000 as well as in 2011/2012. Some factors were found to be significantly related to healing time, and intervention addressing these may improve the time to heal, although such interpretations must be taken with precaution from the present study and should be proven in randomized prospective intervention trials.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Sørensen, Marie Louise Buhl","Jansen, Rasmus Bo","Wilbek Fabricius, Therese","Jørgensen, Bo","Svendsen, Ole Lander"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-503709"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"healing of diabetic foot ulcers in patients treated at the copenhagen wound healing center in 000000001999 000000002000 and in 000000002011 000000002012","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2019/6429575","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-503709","identifier":[{"id":"85072734991","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6754878","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"31583251","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-b8ba47b2-6454-4af8-9d38-9139cbb97752"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-737150DDB5C95FA6481692FDE15D337D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/236322104/6429575.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2019/6429575","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of 