sk score associates with efficacy of migraine-specific drugs","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-090.65-000669","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background: Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is a major health concern affecting 25% of the world's population. It is generally held that a fatty liver does not influence liver function, but quantitative measurements of metabolic liver functions have not been systematically performed. We aimed to study selected hepatocellular metabolic functions in patients with different stages of NAFLD. Methods: Twenty-five non-diabetic, biopsy-proven NAFLD patients [12 with simple steatosis; 13 with non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH)] and ten healthy controls were included in a cross-sectional study. Hepatocyte cytosolic function was assessed by the galactose elimination capacity (GEC), mitochondrial-cytosolic metabolic capacity by the functional hepatic nitrogen clearance (FHNC), microsomal function by the aminopyrine breath test, and excretory liver function by indocyanine green (ICG) elimination. Results: GEC was 20% higher in NAFLD than in controls [3.15 mmol/min (2.9–3.41) vs. 2.62 (2.32–2.93); P = 0.02]. FHNC was 30% lower in NAFLD [23.3 L/h (18.7–28.9) vs. 33.1 (28.9–37.9); P = 0.04], more so in simple steatosis [19.1 L/h (13.9–26.2); P = 0.003] and non-significantly in NASH [27.9 L/h (20.6–37.8); P = 0.19]. Aminopyrine metabolism was 25% lower in simple steatosis [8.9% (7.0–10.7)] and 50% lower in NASH [6.0% (4.5–7.5)] than in controls [11.9% (9.3–12.8)] (P < 0.001). ICG elimination was intact. Conclusions: The hepatocellular metabolic functions were altered in a manner that was dissociated both by different effects on different liver functions and by different effects of different stages of NAFLD. Thus, NAFLD has widespread consequences for metabolic liver function, even in simple steatosis.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Eriksen, Peter Lykke","Sørensen, Michael","Grønbæk, Henning","Hamilton-Dutoit, Stephen Jacques","Vilstrup, Hendrik","Thomsen, Karen Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-267642"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Indocyanine green","Liver Function Tests","Non alcoholic steatohepatitis","Urea cycle"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"non alcoholic fatty liver disease causes dissociated changes in metabolic liver functions","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85061314043","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-267642","identifier":[{"id":"85061314043","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30770336","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-f26dc938-c2f6-4c9c-8d1a-02c10ad2c257"],"keyword":["Aminopyrine","Hepatic elimination","Indocyanine green","Liver function tests","Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis","Urea cycle"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FACFA1E13F2046BA02216FA95D57A807"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Hepatology and Gastroenterology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter Lykke","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","locid":"02b5f3f7-8692-403b-9a17-1dc588d638e7","name":"Eriksen, Peter Lykke","orcid":"0000-0003-1115-8308","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Michael","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"52635821-2c0d-418e-848d-e3e1f1175e12","name":"Sørensen, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-8750-4329","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henning","key":"grønbækpau","last":"Grønbæk","locid":"32aaf9c5-5cf0-4e7b-86d8-a534fa4a9ae9","name":"Grønbæk, Henning","orcid":"0000-0001-8998-7910","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stephen","key":"hamiltondutoitpau","last":"Hamilton-Dutoit","locid":"9b291bda-53d8-40c3-a0f6-564aefbe8300","name":"Hamilton-Dutoit, Stephen Jacques","name_org":"Hamilton-Dutoit, Stephen","orcid":"0000-0003-2158-3885","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hendrik","key":"vilstruppau","last":"Vilstrup","locid":"9e1905f3-3094-4aea-bbde-7178733bf6cb","name":"Vilstrup, Hendrik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen Louise","key":"thomsenpau","last":"Thomsen","locid":"32baa31f-0b93-4c6d-af7e-0da0519f574f","name":"Thomsen, Karen Louise","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30770336"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.clinre.2019.01.001","issn":["22107401"],"issue":"5","journal":"Clinics and Research in Hepatology and Gastroenterology","pages":"551-560","publisher":"Elsevier Masson","status":"p","title":"Clinics and Research in Hepatology and Gastroenterology","volume":"43"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061314043"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-f26dc938-c2f6-4c9c-8d1a-02c10ad2c257"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease causes dissociated changes in metabolic liver functions","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-267642","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Through both atmospheric and oceanic circulation, heat is transferred between the equator and the poles. Possible ways in which the Arctic ecological systems can be affected by warmer temperatures include: changes in amount and duration of snow and ice cover; frequency and extent of spring floods; changes in the ratio of precipitation minus evapotranspiration; amounts of water transport from lakes and rivers from snow and permafrost melting; and a decrease in frozen precipitation. A key component in transferring heat is through freshwater exchange in and out of the Arctic. Hence, accurate mapping freshwater fluxes and potentially its changes with time is vital to describe the heat transfer and its possible temporal changes. Our results demonstrate how ESA’s Earth Observation data together with in-situ measurements can be used to improve the mapping of the major Arctic Ocean freshwater fluxes. In this paper, we outline how four of the five major freshwater fluxes can be determined using present day Earth Observation data exclusively. These are: discharge from rivers; inflow through ice and melt run off; outflow of freshwater in sea ice; and in/outflow of freshwater through ocean currents. We subsequently present key finding and estimates of these four freshwater fluxes and compare our results with estimates based on in-situ data provided through previous studies.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Andersen, Ole Baltazar","Nielsen, Karina","Sørensen, Louise Sandberg","Skourup, Henriette","Andersen, Natalia Havelund","Nagler, Thomas","Wuite, Jan","Kouraev, Alexei","Zakharova, Elena","Fernandez, Diego"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-320688"],"event":{"end":"2018-04-26","place":"Chania, gr","start":"2018-04-23","title":"International Review Workshop on Satellite Altimetry Cal/Val Activities and Applications","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Ole Baltazar"],"country":["Austria","France","Italy","Russia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CNRS","ENVEO IT GmbH","ESRIN - ESA Centre for Earth Observation","Russian Academy of Sciences"],"keyword":["Altimetry","Earth observation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"arctic freshwater fluxes from earth observation data","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85081562475","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The authors are thankful to ESA for funding the ArcFlux study under the STSE ITT ArcticC program. The authors would also like to thank the space agencies and data repositories (RADS and seaice.dk) for providing state-of-the-art data for the investigation. Finally, the authors would like to thank an anonymous reviewer for valuable comments and suggestions to improve the manuscript."],"id":"S01-000.00-320688","identifier":[{"id":"85081562475","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-363477f7-16ba-4742-93be-49da6f5e4639"],"keyword":["Altimetry","Arctic freshwater","Earth observation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A1BD5C866FA365511BE4D9EF32018744"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Space Institute","Technical University of Denmark, National Space I