r","name":"Miller, Bruce","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ruth","key":"talsingerpau","last":"Tal-Singer","name":"Tal-Singer, Ruth","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Uffe","key":"holmskovpau","last":"Holmskov","locid":"c1ce73b6-d49f-466e-9730-fc83076f65cd","name":"Holmskov, Uffe","orcid":"0000-0002-2391-9445","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jørgen","key":"vestbopau","last":"Vestbo","locid":"4e11f0e8-ccbc-4908-8dcc-14b5289701ff","name":"Vestbo, Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0001-6355-6362","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Grith Lykke","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"950cb689-da86-4abe-835b-a1312ddf5cec","name":"Sorensen, Grith Lykke","name_org":"Sørensen, Grith Lykke","orcid":"0000-0002-5273-1097","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31024964"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1183/23120541.00021-2019","issn":["23120541"],"issue":"2","journal":"ERJ Open Research","paperid":"00021-2019","publisher":"European Respiratory Society","status":"p","title":"ERJ Open Research","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85114076873"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-8f43ceb8-a54f-45e9-a484-77753fcb23dd"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Plasma microfibrillar-associated protein 4 is not prognostic of emphysema progression but is associated with cardiovascular disease history and mortality in COPD patients","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-614254","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"The bronze sculpture known as the Isted Lion is an interesting example of a memorial site of continuously changing meaning. Not only its meaning but also its physical location has changed over time—as it has been moved between towns and countries. Each move has been part of a process of politically motivated reinterpretations not just of the war, which the monument marks, but also of the wider context of the conflict and of changing political aspirations. The changing meanings and political interpretations have been closely intertwined with strong emotions and debates in the German-Danish borderland over some 150 years. But why this monument in particular? A concise answer is: because it has been able to act as the receptacle of widely different and contradictory remembrances. It speaks of an important event, possesses an inherent value as a work of art, and embodies a cultural heritage that is uncomfortable for both nations, but in its present location it also stands for reconciliation and a rejection of nationalist sentiments. This chapter aims to trace the history of this monument and its changing interpretations, and it details the debates that have surrounded its successive roles and meanings. In doing so it provides a close reading of how changing meanings are fabricated.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Sørensen, Marie Louise Stig","Adriansen, Inge"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-632883"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Cambridge"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Museum Sønderjylland"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the isted lion from memorial of war to monument of friendship","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-632883","ids":["sdu-d03eedbf-9c08-44d5-857e-fb7c8bb1e613"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F6610F44F1BF1040B057E4C68BE585FF"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of cambridge","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Cambridge","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Museum Sønderjylland"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie Louise Stig","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Marie Louise Stig","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Inge","key":"adriansenpau","last":"Adriansen","locid":"94287761-67ae-486a-beab-c6b03d7ffba0","name":"Adriansen, Inge","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-18091-1_9","eisbn":["9783030180911"],"isbn":["9783030180904"],"pages":"279-303","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","series":"Palgrave Studies in Cultural Heritage and Conflict","status":"p","subtitle":"From History to Heritage","title":"Memorials in the Aftermath of Armed Conflict"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-d03eedbf-9c08-44d5-857e-fb7c8bb1e613"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The Isted Lion : From Memorial of War to Monument of Friendship","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-632883","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To assess the separate and combined associations of maternal pre-pregnancy body mass index (BMI) and gestational weight gain with the risks of pregnancy complications and their population impact. DESIGN: Individual participant data meta-analysis of 39 cohorts. SETTING: Europe, North America, and Oceania. POPULATION: 265 270 births. METHODS: Information on maternal pre-pregnancy BMI, gestational weight gain, and pregnancy complications was obtained. Multilevel binary logistic regression models were used. MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: Gestational hypertension, pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes, preterm birth, small and large for gestational age at birth. RESULTS: Higher maternal pre-pregnancy BMI and gestational weight gain were, across their full ranges, associated with higher risks of gestational hypertensive disorders, gestational diabetes, and large for gestational age at birth. Preterm birth risk was higher at lower and higher BMI and weight gain. Compared with normal weight mothers with medium gestational weight gain, obese mothers with high gestational weight gain had the highest risk of any pregnancy complication (odds ratio 2.51, 95% CI 2.31- 2.74). We estimated that 23.9% of any pregnancy complication was attributable to maternal overweight/obesity and 31.6% of large for gestational age infants was attributable to excessive gestational weight gain. CONCLUSIONS: Maternal pre-pregnancy BMI and gestational weight gain are, across their full ranges, associated with risks of pregnancy complications. Obese mothers with high gestational weight gain are at the highest risk of pregnancy complications. Promoting a healthy pre-pregnancy BMI and gestational weight gain may reduce the burden of pregnancy complications and ultimately the risk of maternal and neonatal morbidity. TWEETABLE ABSTRACT: Promoting a healthy body mass index and gestational weight gain might reduce the population burden of pregnancy complications.","author_count":170,"contributo