,"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85055078478"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","North Denmark Region","Other Research","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-3fa53501-35a0-41b8-bac1-044c6b7b80cf","au-78a874b3-59d1-4f3d-aea9-30bb315af38e","ku-817cdf27-0284-4172-9e41-289a9c5394f4","sdu-482234e6-53fa-48a2-ae06-b143e44c59ea"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Human genetic variation in GLS2 is associated with development of complicated <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> bacteremia","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-096.54-003121","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Background: Chronic non-malignant lung diseases such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and interstitial lung diseases (ILD) result in reduced quality of life (QoL), a high symptom burden and reduced survival. Patients with chronic non-malignant lung disease often have limited access to palliative care. The symptom burden and the QoL of these patients resembles patients with cancer and the general palliative approach is similar. However, the disease trajectory is often slow and unpredictable, and the palliative effort must be built on accessibility, continuity and professional competences. The Danish Health Authority as well as the WHO recommends that there is access to palliative care for all patients with life-threatening diseases regardless of diagnosis. In 2011, the Danish Health Authority requested that the national medical societies would to formulate guidelines for palliation. Methods: In 2015, a group of members of the Danish Respiratory Society (DRS) was appointed for this purpose. It was composed of experienced ILD and COPD researchers as well as clinicians from different parts of Denmark. A literature review was made, a draft was prepared, and all recommendations were agreed upon unanimously. Results: The Danish version of the position paper was finally submitted for review and accepted by all members of DRS. Conclusion: In this position paper we provide recommendations on the terminology of chronic and terminal lung failure, rehabilitation and palliative care, advanced care planning, informal caregivers and bereavement, symptom management, the imminently dying patient, and organization of palliative care for patients with chronic non-malignant lung diseases.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Marsaa, Kristoffer","Gundestrup, Svend","Jensen, Jens-Ulrik","Lange, Peter","Løkke, Anders","Roberts, Nassim Bazeghi","Shaker, Saher Burhan","Sørensen, Anita Rath","Titlestad, Ingrid Louise","Thomsen, Laura Hohwü","Weinreich, Ulla Møller","Bendstrup, Elisabeth","Wilcke, Torgny"],"cuid":["S04-097.72-004243"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"corresponding_author":["Bendstrup, Elisabeth"],"keyword":["End of life","Lung disease","Palliation","chronic lung failure","non malignant","terminal lung failure"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["Palliative Unit, Herlev-Gentofte Hospital, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"danish respiratory society position paper palliative care in patients with chronic progressive non malignant lung diseases","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/20018525.2018.1530029","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-097.72-004243","identifier":[{"id":"30357015","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6197032","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85064966563","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-56f3cb07-cdd3-42da-8b6f-6262787e61fb","au-74ffdef2-3a8e-4d93-bf14-e41f3f75a2c9","ku-0ca097d0-3f1a-4209-b877-73d9b36f173a","sdu-788135a4-7a39-4e43-9413-52bc19a35960"],"keyword":["Palliation","chronic lung failure","end-of-life","lung disease","non-malignant","terminal lung failure"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-2DFD5F095B6CFFD2B53AB3AC50029B9F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/20018525.2018.1530029","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC",