{"abstract":"The structure and the strength of organic compound adsorption on mineral surfaces are of interest for a number of industrial and environmental applications, oil recovery, CO2 storage and contamination remediation. Biomineralised calcite plays an essential role in the function of many organisms that control crystal growth with organic macromolecules. Carbonate rocks, composed almost exclusively of calcite, host drinking water aquifers and oil reservoirs. In this study, we examined the ordering behaviour of several organic compounds and the thickness of the adsorbed layers formed on calcite {10.4} surfaces. We used X-ray reflectivity (XRR) to study calcite {10.4} surfaces that were prepared in three alcohols: methanol, isopropanol and pentanol and one carboxylic acid: octanoic acid. All molecules adsorbed in self-assembled layers, where thickness depended on the density and the length of the molecule. For methanol and isopropanol, molecular dynamic simulations (MD) provided complementary information, which allowed us to develop a surface model. Branching in isopropanol induced slightly less ordering because of the additional degree of freedom. Pentanol and octanoic acid adsorbed as single monolayers. The results of this work indicate that adhered organic compounds from the surrounding environment can affect the surface behaviour, depending on properties of the organic compound.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Hakim, S. S.","Olsson, M. H. M.","Sørensen, H. O.","Bovet, N.","Bohr, Jakob","Feidenhans'l, R.","Stipp, S. L. S."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-034249"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Biogeochemistry","Environmental Chemistry"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"interactions of the calcite 000000000010 000000000004 surface with organic compounds structure and behaviour at mineral organic interfaces","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-06977-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-034249","identifier":[{"id":"85027023302","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC5548931","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"28790315","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-49dfa544-25b4-4c8e-a291-2af7595e6308","ku-e507c431-d581-4d05-816c-f540ad9a641b"],"keyword":["Biogeochemistry","Environmental chemistry"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-B48889929D966735F071F19368B43217"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-06977-4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/182891023/Interactions_of_the_Calcite_10.4_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/134611977/s41598_017_06977_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Chemistry, Department of Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Micro- and Nanotechnology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Micro- and Nanotechnology, Theoretical Biophysics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Sepideh Sadat","key":"hakimpau","last":"Hakim","locid":"60c6860c-4c94-4b91-95ff-45e802cdc01a","name":"Hakim, Sepideh Sadat","orcid":"0000-0001-6486-4551","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mats Henrik Mikael","key":"olssonpau","last":"Olsson","locid":"d33d21bf-d396-4cd4-af26-1e75ad022ef8","name":"Olsson, Mats Henrik Mikael","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henning Osholm","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"e1bf7bae-7f0e-445e-99ea-12eb242407fb","name":"Sørensen, Henning Osholm","orcid":"0000-0002-7004-547X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Nicolas Emile","key":"bovetpau","last":"Bovet","locid":"a001edf5-b545-4614-b905-727171248f80","name":"Bovet, Nicolas Emile","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jakob","key":"bohrpau","last":"Bohr","locid":"d468e94c-7bd7-4c9d-9ea3-b029bfefcdd7","name":"Bohr, Jakob","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Robert Krarup","key":"feidenhanslpau","last":"Feidenhans'l","locid":"0a3fb80e-6206-4f37-8e19-12d109fdab12","name":"Feidenhans'l, Robert Krarup","orcid":"0000-0002-7963-3649","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Susan Louise Svane","key":"stipppau","last":"Stipp","locid":"b907e7ef-d26a-4093-836b-9b7c9238d956","name":"Stipp, Susan Louise Svane","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28790315"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-017-06977-4","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"7592","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85027023302"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-49dfa544-25b4-4c8e-a291-2af7595e6308","ku-e507c431-d581-4d05-816c-f540ad9a641b"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Interactions of the Calcite {10.4} Surface with Organic Compounds: Structure and Behaviour at Mineral – Organic Interfaces","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-034249","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Background: Loss-of-function mutations in hERG (encoding the Kv11.1 voltage-gated potassium channel) cause long-QT syndrome type 2 (LQT2) because of prolonged cardiac repolarization. However, Kv11.1 is also present in pancreatic and β cells and intestinal L and K cells, secreting glucagon, insulin, and the incretins glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide), respectively. These hormones are crucial for glucose regulation, and long-QT syndrome may cause disturbed glucose regulation. We measured secretion of these hormones and cardiac repolarization in response to glucose ingestion in LQT2 patients with functional mutations in hERG and matched healthy participants, testing the hypothesis that LQT2 patients have increased incretin and β-cell function and decreased -cell function, and thus lower glucose levels. Methods: Eleven patients with LQT2 and 22 sex-, age-, and body mass index-matched control participants underwent a 6-hour 75-g oral glucose tolerance test with ECG recording and blood sampling for measurements of glucose, insulin, C-peptide, glucagon, GLP-1, and GIP. Results: In comparison with matched control participants, LQT2 patients had 56% to 78% increased serum insulin, serum C-peptide, plasma GLP-1, and plasma GIP responses (P=0.03-0.001) and decreased plasma glucose levels after glucose ingestion (P=0.02) with more symptoms of hypoglycemia (P=0.04). Sixty-three percent of LQT2 patients developed hypoglycemic plasma glucose levels (<70 mg/dL) versus 36% control participants (P=0.16), and 18% patients developed serious hypoglycemia (<50 mg/dL) versus none of the controls. LQT2 patients had defective glucagon responses to low glucose, P=0.008. β-Cell function (Insulin Secretion Sensitivity Index-2) was 2-fold higher in LQT2 patients than in controls (4398 [95% confidence interval, 2259-8562] versus 2156 [1961-3201], P=0.03). Pharmacological Kv11.1 blockade (dofetilide) in rats had similar effect, and small interfering RNA inhibition of hERG in β and L cells increased insulin and GLP-1 secretion up to 50%. Glucose ingestion caused cardiac repolarization disturbances with increased QTc intervals in both patients and controls, but with a 122% greater increase in QTcF interval in LQT2 patients (P=0.004). Conclusions: Besides a prolonged cardiac repolarization phase, LQT2 patients display increased GLP-1, GIP, and insulin secretion and defective glucagon secretion, causing decreased plasma glucose and thus increased risk of hypoglycemia. Furthermore, glucose ingestion increased QT interval and aggravated the cardiac repolarization disturbances in LQT2 patients.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Hyltén-Cavallius, Louise","Iepsen, Eva Winning","Wewer Albrechtsen, Nicolai J","Svendstrup, Mathilde","Lubberding, Anniek F","Hartmann, Bolette","Jespersen, Thomas","Linneberg, Allan","Christiansen, Michael","Vestergaard, Henrik","Pedersen, Oluf","Holst, Jens J","Kanters, Jørgen K.","Hansen, Torben","Torekov, Signe"],"cuid":["S03-091.01-003819"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Hansen, Torben"],"keyword":["Action Potentials","Adult","Animals","Arrhythmias Cardiac","Biomarkers blood","Blood Glucose drug effects","C Peptide blood","Case Control Studies","Cell Line Tumor","Electrocardiography","Female","Gastric Inhibitory Polypeptide blood","Genetic Predisposition to Disease","Glucagon","Glucagon Like Peptide 1 blood","Glucagon blood","Glucose","Glucose Tolerance Test","Humans","Hyperglycemia","Hypoglycemia","Hypoglycemia blood","Incretins metabolism","Insulin Secreting Cells","Insulin blood","Islets of Langerhans metabolism","Journal Article","Long QT Syndrome","Male","Mice","Middle Aged","Mutation","Phenotype","Potassium Channel Blockers pharmacology","Potassium Channels Voltage Gated","RNA Interference","Rats Wistar","Time Factors","Transfection","glucagon like peptide 1 GLP 1"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Department of Biomedical Sciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences & NNF Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"patients with long qt syndrome due to impaired herg encoded kv000000000011 000000000001 potassium channel have exaggerated endocrine pancreatic and incretin function associated with reactive hypoglycemia","src":["aau","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.116.024279","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-091.01-003819","identifier":[{"id":"000400246900012","type":"wos"},{"id":"85019575649","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC5412733","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"28235848","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-4e476d17-125c-4026-9a86-30478c9cf664","ku-b3415700-4dc0-4294-a7bc-f0de156cbde2","sdu-ee51b124-0e38-4fee-87a7-91445ec02df5"],"keyword":["Action Potentials","Adult","Animals","Biomarkers/blood","Blood Glucose/drug effects","C-Peptide/blood","Case-Control Studies","Cell Line, Tumor","ERG1 Potassium Channel/antagonists & inhibitors","Electrocardiography","Female","Gastric Inhibitory Polypeptide/blood","Genetic Predisposition to Disease","Glucagon-Like Peptide 1/blood","Glucagon/blood","Glucose Tolerance Test","Heart Conduction System/metabolism","Humans","Hypoglycemia/blood","Incretins/metabolism","Insulin/blood","Islets of Langerhans/metabolism","Journal Article","Long QT Syndrome/blood","Male","Mice","Middle Aged","Mutation","Phenotype","Potassium Channel Blockers/pharmacology","RNA In