{"abstract":"Mannose-binding lectin (MBL) is a key molecule in innate immunity. MBL binds to carbohydrates on the surface of pathogens, initiating the complement system via the lectin-dependent pathway or facilitates opsonophagocytosis. In vivo studies using inbred chicken lines differing in MBL serum concentration indicate that chicken MBL affects Salmonella resistance; further studies are imperative in conventional broiler chickens. In this study 104 conventional day-old chickens (offspring from a cross between Cobb 500 male and female parent breeders) were orally infected with Salmonella enterica subsp. enterica serovar Montevideo. The chickens were divided into two groups based on polymorphisms in their MBL promoter region, designated L/L for low serum concentrations of MBL and L/H for medium serum concentrations of MBL. A semi-quantitative real-time PCR method for detection of Salmonella in cloacal swabs was used, the log10 CFU quantification was based on a standard curve from artificially spiked cloacal swab samples pre-incubated for 8 h with known concentrations of Salmonella ranging from 101 to 106 CFU/swabs, with an obtained amplification efficiency of 102% and a linear relationship between the log10 CFU and the threshold cycle Ct values of (R2 = 0.99). The L/L chickens had significantly higher Log10 CFU/swab at week 5 post infection (pi) than the L/H chickens. A repetition of the study with 86 L/L and 18 L/H chickens, also gave significantly higher log10 CFU ± SEM in cloacal swabs, using the semi-quantitative real-time PCR method from L/L chickens than from the L/H chickens at week 5 pi. These results indicate that genetically determined basic levels of MBL may influence S. Montevideo susceptibility.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Ulrich-Lynge, Sofie Louise","Juul-Madsen, Helle Risdahl","Kjærup, Rikke Brødsgaard","Okimoto, Ron","Abrahamsen, Mitchell","Maurischat, Sven","Sørensen, Poul","Dalgaard, Tina Sørensen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-191699"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Broilers","Chicken","Mannose Binding Lectin","Real time PCR"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"broilers with low serum mannose binding lectin show increased faecal shedding of em salmonella enterica em serovar montevideo","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84982851787","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-191699","identifier":[{"id":"84982851787","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26994208","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-6d582479-96fc-4a7d-b76a-9fcf462fd600"],"keyword":["Salmonella enterica subsp. enterica serovar Montevideo","broilers","chicken","mannose-binding lectin","real-time PCR"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6354EC54425A9C6BE82BB798ACD930A9"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Animal and Veterinary Sciences, Department of Animal and Veterinary Sciences - ANIS Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sofie Louise","key":"ulrichlyngepau","last":"Ulrich-Lynge","locid":"9934f350-7a13-499b-8bcb-5877dc7558d9","name":"Ulrich-Lynge, Sofie Louise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Helle Risdahl","key":"juulmadsenpau","last":"Juul-Madsen","locid":"3829e4a9-b29d-474f-9d37-c83a72fc564c","name":"Juul-Madsen, Helle Risdahl","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Brødsgaard","key":"kjæruppau","last":"Kjærup","locid":"0770c615-8e70-4201-aa98-cf459c0a02e7","name":"Kjærup, Rikke Brødsgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7798-5483","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ron","key":"okimotopau","last":"Okimoto","name":"Okimoto, Ron","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mitchell","key":"abrahamsenpau","last":"Abrahamsen","name":"Abrahamsen, Mitchell","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sven","key":"maurischatpau","last":"Maurischat","name":"Maurischat, Sven","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Poul","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"3172d2c3-ef30-4475-9921-e8fd8f8dad8a","name":"Sørensen, Poul","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tina Sørensen","key":"dalgaardpau","last":"Dalgaard","locid":"78918005-837a-4b5c-b5f9-3f71d33f8047","name":"Dalgaard, Tina Sørensen","orcid":"0000-0002-3424-6047","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26994208"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3382/ps/pew101","issn":["00325791","15253171"],"issn_org":["00325791"],"issue":"8","journal":"Poultry Science","pages":"1779-1786","publisher":"Elsevier Inc.","status":"p","title":"Poultry Science","volume":"95"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84982851787"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-6d582479-96fc-4a7d-b76a-9fcf462fd600"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Broilers with low serum Mannose-binding Lectin show increased faecal shedding of <em>Salmonella enterica </em>serovar Montevideo","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-191699","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Physiologically structured population models have become a valuable tool to model the dynamics of populations. In a stationary environment such models can exhibit equilibrium solutions as well as periodic solutions. However, for many organisms the environment is not stationary, but varies more or less regularly. In order to understand the interaction between an external environmental forcing and the internal dynamics in a population, we examine the response of a physiologically structured population model to a periodic variation in the food resource. We explore the addition of forcing in two cases: (A) where the population dynamics is in equilibrium in a stationary environment, and (B) where the population dynamics exhibits a periodic solution in a stationary environment. When forcing is applied in case A, the solutions are mainly periodic. In case B the forcing signal interacts with the oscillations of the unforced system, and both periodic and irregular (quasi-periodic or chaotic) solutions occur. In both cases the periodic solutions include one and multiple period cycles, and each cycle can have several reproduction pulses.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Heilmann, Irene Louise Torpe","Starke, Jens","Andersen, Ken Haste","Thygesen, Uffe Høgsbro","Sørensen, Mads Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-346506"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bifurcation diagram"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dynamics of a physiologically structured population in a time varying environment","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/127860146/Dynamics_of_a_physiologically_structured_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-346506","identifier":[{"id":"84994890810","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-8c82cc67-1684-470f-8e07-63a1dbd6222f"],"keyword":["Bifurcation diagram","Periodic variation","Structured population model"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EC3140831CB69E391FF2247554EEA96A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/127860146/Dynamics_of_a_physiologically_structured_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Dynamical Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Irene Louise Torpe","key":"heilmannpau","last":"Heilmann","locid":"1194dade-cad7-4492-819f-e12576665243","name":"Heilmann, Irene Louise Torpe","orcid":"0000-0003-2185-1165","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens","key":"starkepau","last":"Starke","locid":"769799c6-1a6a-4f3b-9ad2-c0318450485f","name":"Starke, Jens","orcid":"0000-0001-9411-5893","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ken Haste","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"af8fa35c-b159-497e-af0b-5f5efc6f245e","name":"Andersen, Ken Haste","orcid":"0000-0002-8478-3430","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Uffe Høgsbro","key":"thygesenpau","last":"Thygesen","locid":"2e512345-29bf-435a-b673-5858f7028550","name":"Thygesen, Uffe Høgsbro","orcid":"0000-0002-4311-6324","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads Peter","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"651b67f7-1e41-4719-8369-01b34075cd6b","name":"Sørensen, Mads Peter","orcid":"0000-0003-0820-8722","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ecocom.2016.10.004","issn":["1476945X","14769840"],"issn_org":["1476945X"],"journal":"Ecological Complexity: An International Journal on Biocomplexity in the Environment and Theoretical Ecology","pages":"54-61","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Ecological Complexity: An International Journal on Biocomplexity in the Environment and Theoretical Ecology","title_org":"Ecological Complexity","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84994890810"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-8c82cc67-1684-470f-8e07-63a1dbd6222f"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Dynamics of a physiologically structured population in a time-varying environment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-346506","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT) using cell-free fetal DNA from the peripheral blood of the pregnant woman has become a possibility within recent years, but is not yet implemented in Denmark. NIPT has proven to be very efficient in the screening for especially trisomi 21. This article summarizes the basics behinds the most used NIPT techniques and describes which genetic conditions this method may detect. Finally, the future aspects of implementing NIPT in the prenatal screening programme in Denmark are discussed.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hornstrup, Louise Stig","Ambye, Louise","Sørensen, Steen","Jørgensen, Finn Stener"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-400415"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"noninvasiv prænatal test er et gennembrud inden for prænatal screening","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://ugeskriftet.dk/videnskab/noninvasiv-praenatal-test-er-et-gennembrud-inden-praenatal-screening","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-400415","identifier":[{"id":"26239855","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-0ba503e2-3ecd-4755-89a7-62fd1c919e21"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-FC57FCFB9D3488E48247881505E99BA3"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Louise Stig","key":"hornstruppau","last":"Hornstrup","name":"Hornstrup, Louise Stig","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Louise","key":"ambyepau","last":"Ambye","name":"Ambye, Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Steen","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Steen","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Finn Stener","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"af103a51-0399-4d0c-8377-db0260a8c6c8","name":"Jørgensen, Finn Stener","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26239855"],"pub":{"issn":["00415782","13994174"],"issn_org":["00415782"],"issue":"5","journal":"Ugeskrift for Læger","pages":"2-6","paperid":"V09140465","status":"p","title":"Ugeskrift for Læger","title_org":"Ugeskrift for Laeger","volume":"178"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85021847734","2-s2.0-85036632952"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-0ba503e2-3ecd-4755-89a7-62fd1c919e21"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Noninvasiv prænatal test er et gennembrud inden for prænatal screening","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-400415","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Context: Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) has a largely unknown etiology and presents with a clinical heterogeneous patient group. Small noncoding microRNA (miRNA) might prove promising as biomarker candidates for PCOS patient stratification. Altered miRNA expression profiles have been observed in few studies. Objective: The aim was to assess the miRNA expression profile in follicular fluid from PCOS patients and healthy, regularly cycling, matched controls. Design and Setting: Experimental case-control study including 49 PCOS women (19 of which were hyperandrogenic and 30 normo-androgenic) and 21 healthy matched women all undergoing in vitro fertilization treatment. Interventions and Main Outcome: Anthropometric and relevant clinical baseline measurements were obtained. Relative expression of miRNA levels were estimated using miRNA quantitative PCR arrays and validated by quantitative RT-PCR. Correlation between miRNAs and clinical relevant measurements was estimated. Results: PCOS women, both normo-androgenic and hyperandrogenic, had decreased levels of miR-24-3p, -29a, -151-3p, and -574-3p compared with controls. Furthermore, miR-518f-3p was differentially expressed within the PCOS group with high levels observed in the hyperandrogenic group compared with the normo-androgenic PCOS patients. Serum levels of total and free T were positively correlated with miR-518f-3p in PCOS subjects (P = .001). Distinction between PCOS and controls could be made using miR-151-3p alone with an area under the curve of 0.91 or a combination of four selected miRNAs (area under the curve, 0.93). Bioinformatic target analysis points to an involvement of these miRNAs in biological pathways involving regulation of cell proliferation, extracellular matrix, and processes in intermediary metabolism. Conclusion: Our study provides evidence that the miRNA expression profile in follicular fluid is altered in PCOS and indicates that specific follicular fluid miRNAs are associated with phenotypical traits of PCOS. An altered miRNA profile holds potentials for new methods of PCOS patient stratification and may contribute to and in part explain the heterogeneous nature found within PCOS women.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Sørensen, Anja Elaine","Wissing, Marie Louise","Englund, Anne Lis","Dalgaard, Louise Torp"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-561735"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"microrna species in follicular fluid associating with polycystic ovary syndrome and related intermediary phenotypes","src":["ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2015-3588","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-561735","identifier":[{"id":"85011256428","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ruc-5667b9d8-b154-4d67-b0aa-52de86b2854b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9186499379BE6553F64265B639BE3D35"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2015-3588","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University, Department of Science and Environment"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Holbæk Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Anja Elaine","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"7e5d1e2c-5d4b-4436-bb66-acdca4058c08","name":"Sørensen, Anja Elaine","orcid":"0000-0001-7557-5376","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Marie Louise","key":"wissingpau","last":"Wissing","name":"Wissing, Marie Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anne Lis","key":"englundpau","last":"Englund","name":"Englund, Anne Lis","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Louise Torp","key":"dalgaardpau","last":"Dalgaard","locid":"a219fdaf-f994-4a7a-beb8-1f353846ed5a","name":"Dalgaard, Louise Torp","orcid":"0000-0002-3598-2775","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1210/jc.2015-3588","issn":["0021972X","19457197"],"issn_org":["0021972X"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","pages":"1579-1589","status":"p","title":"Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism","volume":"101"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85011256428"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ruc-5667b9d8-b154-4d67-b0aa-52de86b2854b"],"src":["RUC Roskilde University"],"title":"MicroRNA Species in Follicular Fluid Associating With Polycystic Ovary Syndrome and Related Intermediary Phenotypes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-561735","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: Autologous lipotransfer is seen as an ideal filler for soft tissue reconstruction. The main limitation of this procedure is the unpredictable resorption and volume loss of the fat graft. In the recent decade, an increasing amount of research has focused on the use of adipose tissue-derived stromal cells (ASCs) to enrich the fat graft, a procedure termed cell-assisted lipotransfer (CAL). The aim of this review was to systematically review the current preclinical and clinical evidence for the efficacy of CAL compared with conventional lipotransfer. MATERIALS AND METHODS: A systematic search was performed on PubMed and other databases to identify all preclinical and clinical studies where CAL with ASCs was compared with conventional lipotransfer. A total of 20 preclinical studies and seven clinical studies were included in the review. RESULTS: The preclinical studies consisted of 15 studies using immunodeficient animal models and five studies using immunocompetent studies. Seventeen studies examined weight/volume retention of which 15 studies favored CAL over conventional lipotransfer. One clinical study did not find any efficacy of CAL and the remaining six studies favored CAL. CONCLUSIONS: The present evidence suggests that there is a big potential for CAL in reconstructive surgery; however, the present studies are so far still of low quality with inherent weaknesses. Several aspects regarding CAL still remain unknown such as the optimal degree of cell enrichment and also its safety. Further high-quality studies are needed to establish if CAL can live up to its potential. LEVEL OF EVIDENCE V: This journal requires that authors assign a level of evidence to each article. For a full description of these Evidence-Based Medicine ratings, please refer to the Table of Contents or the online Instructions to Authors www.springer.com/00266 .","author_count":3,"contributors":["Toyserkani, Navid Mohamadpour","Quaade, Marlene Louise","Sørensen, Jens Ahm"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-595881"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adipose derived stromal cell","Fat graft","Regenerative medicine","Stromal vascular fraction"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cell assisted lipotransfer a systematic review of its efficacy","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4819466/pdf/266_2016_Article_613.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-595881","identifier":[{"id":"26893280","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC4819466","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84958762236","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-4f1a0d7c-82ad-497c-b020-7a082b6b7370"],"keyword":["Adipose-derived stromal cell","Cell-assisted lipotransfer","Fat graft","Regenerative medicine","Stromal vascular fraction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D02270B73F01DD2B14919EBBB5838A29"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4819466/pdf/266_2016_Article_613.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Plastic Surgery (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Navid Mohamadpour","key":"toyserkanipau","last":"Toyserkani","locid":"1fe54f0a-4c69-467e-8d39-62f685e36625","name":"Toyserkani, Navid Mohamadpour","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marlene Louise","key":"quaadepau","last":"Quaade","locid":"365d389f-829a-4a48-b078-7acdfa2caeb7","name":"Quaade, Marlene Louise","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jens Ahm","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"267cd59a-3918-47db-8d23-54015d770b6c","name":"Sørensen, Jens Ahm","orcid":"0000-0003-4903-0094","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26893280"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00266-016-0613-1","issn":["0364216X","14325241"],"issn_org":["0364216X"],"issue":"2","journal":"Aesthetic Plastic Surgery","pages":"309-318","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Aesthetic Plastic Surgery","volume":"40"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84958762236"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-4f1a0d7c-82ad-497c-b020-7a082b6b7370"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Cell-Assisted Lipotransfer : A Systematic Review of Its Efficacy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-595881","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Bacteria within the brine network of sea ice experience temperature-driven fluctuations in salinity on both short and long temporal scales, yet their means of osmoprotection against such fluctuations is poorly understood. One mechanism used to withstand the ion fluxes caused by salinity shifts, well-known in mesophilic bacteria, is the import and export of low molecular weight organic solutes that are compatible with intracellular functions. Working with the marine psychrophilic gammaproteobacterium, Colwellia psychrerythraea 34H, and with natural microbial assemblages present in sackhole brines drained from sea ice in Kanajorsuit Bay (2013) and Kobbefjord (2014), Greenland, we measured the utilization of 14C-choline (precursor to glycine betaine, a common compatible solute) at -1°C upon salinity shifts to double and to half the starting salinity. In all cases and across a range of starting salinities, when salinity was increased, 14C-solute (choline or derivatives) was preferentially retained as an intracellular osmolyte; when salinity was decreased, 14C-choline was preferentially respired to 14CO 2. Additional experiments with cold-adapted bacteria in culture indicated that an abrupt downshift in salinity prompted rapid (subsecond) expulsion of retained 14C-solute, but that uptake of 14C-choline and solute retention resumed when salinity was returned to starting value. Overall, the results indicate that bacteria in sea-ice brines use compatible solutes for osmoprotection, transporting, storing and cycling these molecules as needed to withstand naturally occurring salinity shifts and persist through the seasons. Because choline and many commonly used compatible solutes contain nitrogen, we suggest that when brines freshen and bacteria respire such compatible solutes, the corresponding regeneration of ammonium may enhance specific biogeochemical processes in the ice, possibly algal productivity but particularly nitrification. Measurements of potential nitrification rates in parallel sea-ice samples are consistent with a link between use of the compatible solute strategy and nitrification.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Firth, E.","Carpenter, S. D.","Sørensen, Heidi Louise","Collins, R. E.","Deming, J. W."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-620135"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Alaska","University of Washington"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"bacterial use of choline to tolerate salinity shifts in sea ice brines","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.12952/journal.elementa.000120","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-620135","identifier":[{"id":"84986252895","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-a3fd3361-d4b0-47fa-881e-08aaa0b47142"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FE46C4A6CF25F79D077541AFFF2A0D50"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/124101725/156_1626_1_SM_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.12952/journal.elementa.000120","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of washington","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Washington","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Science, Department of Biology, Nordcee"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of alaska","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Alaska","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"E.","key":"firthpau","last":"Firth","name":"Firth, E.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"S. D.","key":"carpenterpau","last":"Carpenter","name":"Carpenter, S. D.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"H. L.","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"7a5442bb-7ee3-464b-9b90-eee87aee3b39","name":"Sørensen, Heidi Louise","name_org":"Sørensen, H. L.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"R. E.","key":"collinspau","last":"Collins","name":"Collins, R. E.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"J. W.","key":"demingpau","last":"Deming","name":"Deming, J. W.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.12952/journal.elementa.000120","issn":["23251026"],"journal":"Elementa","paperid":"000120","publisher":"University of California Press","status":"p","title":"Elementa","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84986252895"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-a3fd3361-d4b0-47fa-881e-08aaa0b47142"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Bacterial use of choline to tolerate salinity shifts in sea-ice brines","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-620135","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Sørensen, Christina Louise"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-649501"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-UC"],"level3":{"OT":["Byarkivet i Horsens"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCL"]},"level3all":["DI-UC-UCL"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"jøderne i horsens","src":["ucl"],"src-code":["DI-UC-UCL"],"type":"doo","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://horsensleksikon.dk/tidlige-joediske-historie-danmark/","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-649501","ids":["ucl-d77101bb-ce29-474c-8f55-480fe015001c"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Popular","noi":["Local-1-1-36D38B97FDD2537C041910295C06E7C1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://horsensleksikon.dk/tidlige-joediske-historie-danmark/","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Byarkivet i Horsens"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":2,"key":"ucl university college","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCL","l3nl":"UCL University College","l3nn":"UCL University College","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCL University College","namevar":["UCL Erhvervsakademi og Professionshøjskole, Rektor, Rektor, Videncenter for Historie og Kulturformidling"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCL"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christina Louise","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Christina Louise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Horsens Leksikon","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","UCL University College","University Colleges"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ucl-d77101bb-ce29-474c-8f55-480fe015001c"],"src":["UCL University College"],"title":"Jøderne i Horsens","type":"Other-Unknown","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-649501","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"The Danish National Centre for Social Research has surveyed research on daycare facilities for 0-6 year-olds in Scandinavia in 2014 and subsequently assessed the quality of the studies. The objective is to make relevant research more accessible to political decision-makers, researchers and practitioners, such as pedagogical consultants, managers at daycare facilities and teachers in pedagogical education programmes. In 2014, 132 studies of institution facilities for 0-6 year-olds were registered. The scope of the relevant studies in the 2014 survey is much greater than in previous years. The sharp rise is an indication that the increasing focus on daycare and learning at preschool is also reflected in the number of research studies. The majority of the studies (75%) focus on conditions for personnel, while slightly fewer (57%) focus on conditions for children at daycare facilities. Only very few studies (5%) focus on conditions for the parents. Of the 132 studies, 94 are assessed as having good research quality, and have been included in the research database: Nordic Base of Early Childhood Education and Care. The research survey was prepared by the Danish Evaluation Institute with financial support from the Danish Evaluation Institute, the Swedish National Agency for Education and the Norwegian Directorate for Education and Training.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Højen-Sørensen, Anna-Katharina","Kristiansen, Louise Jean","Jørgensen, Anne-Marie Klint","Wendt, Rikke Eline"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-662945"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"com","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-VIVE"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-VIVE"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"kortlægning kvalitetsvurdering og analyse af udviklingen i skandinavisk dagtilbudsforskning for 000000000000 000000000006 årige i året 000000002014","src":["vive"],"src-code":["DI-GV-VIVE"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.vive.dk/ws/files/498061/1614_Kortl_gning_af_dagtilbudsforskning.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-662945","ids":["vive-3ac40667-0245-437a-ab20-f37350ed3b47"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Commissioned","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-24014F95DFE23DABDB58F338D4C88D59"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.vive.dk/ws/files/498061/1614_Kortl_gning_af_dagtilbudsforskning.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"vive the danish center for social science research","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-VIVE","l3nn":"VIVE The Danish Center for Social Science Research","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"VIVE The Danish Center for Social Science Research","namevar":["The Danish National Institute for Social Research, Children and Family"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-VIVE","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anna-Katharina","key":"højensørensenpau","last":"Højen-Sørensen","locid":"f722f6b0-8a11-4a11-9297-d7e7365b0ddc","name":"Højen-Sørensen, Anna-Katharina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Louise Jean","key":"kristiansenpau","last":"Kristiansen","locid":"3e9e2883-2427-4941-8c27-7d22b51ed2f5","name":"Kristiansen, Louise Jean","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anne-Marie Klint","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"32c6631c-7603-476e-9319-840128cbabaa","name":"Jørgensen, Anne-Marie Klint","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke Eline","key":"wendtpau","last":"Wendt","locid":"e933fa85-7809-4e4a-82f8-9147e851b43c","name":"Wendt, Rikke Eline","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["13961810"],"issue":"16:14","pages":"124","place":"København","publisher":"SFI - Det Nationale Forskningscenter for Velfærd","repno":"SFI-Rapport, 16:14","series":"SFI-Rapport","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","Governmental Institutions"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["vive-3ac40667-0245-437a-ab20-f37350ed3b47"],"src":["VIVE The Danish Center for Social Science Research"],"title":"Kortlægning, kvalitetsvurdering og analyse af udviklingen i skandinavisk dagtilbudsforskning for 0-6-årige i året 2014","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-662945","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"The gut microbiota has been established as an important player influencing many aspects of human physiology. Breast milk, the first diet for an infant, contains human milk oligosaccharides (HMO) that shape the infant's gut microbiota by selectively stimulating the growth of specific bacteria, especially bifidobacteria. In addition to their bifidogenic activity, the ability of HMO to modulate immune function and the gut barrier makes them prime candidates to restore a beneficial microbiota in dysbiotic adults and provide health benefits. We conducted a parallel, double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, HMO-supplementation study in 100 healthy, adult volunteers, consuming chemically produced 2'-O-fucosyllactose (2'FL) and/or lacto-N-neotetraose (LNnT) at various daily doses and mixes or placebo for 2 weeks. All participants completed the study without premature discontinuation. Supplementation of 2'FL and LNnT at daily doses up to 20 g was shown to be safe and well tolerated, as assessed using the gastrointestinal symptoms rating scale. 16S rRNA sequencing analysis showed that HMO supplementation specifically modified the adult gut microbiota with the primary impact being substantial increases in relative abundance of Actinobacteria and Bifidobacterium in particular and a reduction in relative abundance of Firmicutes and Proteobacteria. This study provides the first set of data on safety, tolerance and impact of HMO on the adult gut microbiota. Collectively, the results from this study show that supplementing the diet with HMO is a valuable strategy to shape the human gut microbiota and specifically promote the growth of beneficial bifidobacteria.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Elison, Emma","Vigsnæs, Louise K.","Rindom Krogsgaard, Laura","Rasmussen, Julie","Sørensen, Nikolaj","McConnell, Bruce","Hennet, Thierry","Sommer, Morten Otto Alexander","Bytzer, Peter"],"cuid":["S02-088.09-035673"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Zurich"],"keyword":["2 O fucosyllactose","Clinical study","Lacto N neotetraose","Safety","Tolerance"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-GLY","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-GLY","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"oral supplementation of healthy adults with 000000000002 i o i fucosyllactose and lacto i n i neotetraose is well tolerated and shifts the intestinal microbiota","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0007114516003354","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-088.09-035673","identifier":[{"id":"000386912200005","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC5082288","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84990928857","type":"scopus"},{"id":"27719686","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-85d8923b-5cb3-4065-8d4d-caaa6be0a825","ku-dbb176ac-5252-45b2-980e-0c8875a303c9"],"keyword":["2′-O-Fucosyllactose","Clinical study","Lacto-N-neotetraose","Safety","Tolerance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-9676ADA74E2D433AAF6EFED160DDF86E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/126902561/Oral_supplementation_of_healthy_adults_with_2_O_fucosyllactose_and_lacto_N_neotetraose_is_well_tolerated_and_shifts_the_intestinal_microbiota.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0007114516003354","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"glycom","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-GLY","l3nn":"Glycom","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Glycom","namevar":["Glycom ApS"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-GLY","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Center of Epidemiology and Screening"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Clinical Microbiomics A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"ch","key":"university of zurich","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Zurich","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Biosustainability","Technical University of Denmark, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Biosustainability, Bacterial Synthetic Biology","Technical University of Denmark, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Biosustainability, Research Groups"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Emma","key":"elisonpau","last":"Elison","name":"Elison, Emma","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise K.","key":"vigsnæspau","last":"Vigsnæs","name":"Vigsnæs, Louise K.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Laura Rindom","key":"krogsgaardpau","last":"Krogsgaard","locid":"377a1748-2961-4598-abaf-65c17a111dac","name":"Krogsgaard, Laura Rindom","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Julie","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Julie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nikolaj","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Nikolaj","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bruce","key":"mcconnellpau","last":"McConnell","name":"McConnell, Bruce","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Thierry","key":"hennetpau","last":"Hennet","name":"Hennet, Thierry","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Morten Otto Alexander","key":"sommerpau","last":"Sommer","locid":"923149b6-ae3b-461c-9c11-c65100ec1c82","name":"Sommer, Morten Otto Alexander","orcid":"0000-0003-4005-5674","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Peter","key":"bytzerpau","last":"Bytzer","locid":"5567dcaa-8b77-40ff-ba72-b3879e0beefd","name":"Bytzer, Peter","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27719686"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/s0007114516003354","issn":["00071145","14752662"],"issn_org":["00071145"],"journal":"British Journal of Nutrition","pages":"1356-1368","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","title":"British Journal of Nutrition","title_org":"The British Journal of Nutrition","volume":"116"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84990928857"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","clinical microbiomics a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-85d8923b-5cb3-4065-8d4d-caaa6be0a825","ku-dbb176ac-5252-45b2-980e-0c8875a303c9"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Oral supplementation of healthy adults with 2'-O-fucosyllactose and lacto-N-neotetraose is well tolerated and shifts the intestinal microbiota","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-088.09-035673","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis are frequent pathogens in life-threatening infections. Genetic variation in the immune system may predispose to these infections. Nuclear factor-κB is a key component of the TLR-pathway, controlled by inhibitors, encoded by the genes NFKBIA, NFKBIE and NFKBIZ. We aimed to replicate previous findings of genetic variation associated with invasive pneumococcal disease (IPD), and to assess whether similar associations could be found in invasive meningococcal disease (IMD). METHODS: Cases with IPD and IMD and controls were identified by linking Danish national registries. DNA was obtained from the Danish Neonatal Screening Biobank. The association between SNPs and susceptibility to IPD and IMD, mortality and pneumococcal serotypes was investigated. RESULTS: 372 children with pneumococcal meningitis, 907 with pneumococcal bacteremia and 1273 controls were included. We included 406 cases with meningococcal meningitis, 272 with meningococcal bacteremia, and 672 controls. The NFKBIE SNP was associated with increased risk of pneumococcal meningitis (aOR 1.68; 95% CI: 1.20-2.36), but not bacteremia (aOR 1.08; 95% CI: 0.86-1.35). The remaining SNPs were not associated with susceptibility to invasive disease. None of the SNPs were associated with risk of IMD or mortality. CONCLUSIONS: A NFKBIE polymorphism was associated with increased risk of pneumococcal meningitis.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Lundbo, Lene F","Harboe, Zitta Barrella","Clausen, Louise N","Hollegaard, Mads V","Sørensen, Henrik Toft","Hougaard, David M","Konradsen, Helle B","Nørgaard, Mette","Benfield, Thomas"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-027673"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Invasive meningococcal disease","Invasive pneumococcal disease","Journal Article","Research Support Non U S Gov t","nuclear factor κB"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"genetic variation in i nfkbie i is associated with increased risk of pneumococcal meningitis in children","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ebiom.2015.11.048","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-027673","identifier":[{"id":"26870821","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC4739413","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84958896062","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-cece9ce4-8e7d-4f0b-a92a-28650a9608c4","ku-b550d3b6-03f4-4e34-90e3-1defbfe9040b"],"keyword":["Invasive meningococcal disease","Invasive pneumococcal disease","Journal Article","Nuclear Factor-κB","Pneumoccoccal serotypes","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-2CE9ED09006A745C2320B7FA7AA418EE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4739413/pdf/main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ebiom.2015.11.048","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","c