d","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-021628","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Underweight and severe and morbid obesity are associated with highly elevated risks of adverse health outcomes. We estimated trends in mean body-mass index (BMI), which characterises its population distribution, and in the prevalences of a complete set of BMI categories for adults in all countries. METHODS: We analysed, with use of a consistent protocol, population-based studies that had measured height and weight in adults aged 18 years and older. We applied a Bayesian hierarchical model to these data to estimate trends from 1975 to 2014 in mean BMI and in the prevalences of BMI categories (<18·5 kg/m(2) [underweight], 18·5 kg/m(2) to <20 kg/m(2), 20 kg/m(2) to <25 kg/m(2), 25 kg/m(2) to <30 kg/m(2), 30 kg/m(2) to <35 kg/m(2), 35 kg/m(2) to <40 kg/m(2), ≥40 kg/m(2) [morbid obesity]), by sex in 200 countries and territories, organised in 21 regions. We calculated the posterior probability of meeting the target of halting by 2025 the rise in obesity at its 2010 levels, if post-2000 trends continue. FINDINGS: We used 1698 population-based data sources, with more than 19·2 million adult participants (9·9 million men and 9·3 million women) in 186 of 200 countries for which estimates were made. Global age-standardised mean BMI increased from 21·7 kg/m(2) (95% credible interval 21·3-22·1) in 1975 to 24·2 kg/m(2) (24·0-24·4) in 2014 in men, and from 22·1 kg/m(2) (21·7-22·5) in 1975 to 24·4 kg/m(2) (24·2-24·6) in 2014 in women. Regional mean BMIs in 2014 for men ranged from 21·4 kg/m(2) in central Africa and south Asia to 29·2 kg/m(2) (28·6-29·8) in Polynesia and Micronesia; for women the range was from 21·8 kg/m(2) (21·4-22·3) in south Asia to 32·2 kg/m(2) (31·5-32·8) in Polynesia and Micronesia. Over these four decades, age-standardised global prevalence of underweight decreased from 13·8% (10·5-17·4) to 8·8% (7·4-10·3) in men and from 14·6% (11·6-17·9) to 9·7% (8·3-11·1) in women. South Asia had the highest prevalence of underweight in 2014, 23·4% (17·8-29·2) in men and 24·0% (18·9-29·3) in women. Age-standardised prevalence of obesity increased from 3·2% (2·4-4·1) in 1975 to 10·8% (9·7-12·0) in 2014 in men, and from 6·4% (5·1-7·8) to 14·9% (13·6-16·1) in women. 2·3% (2·0-2·7) of the world's men and 5·0% (4·4-5·6) of women were severely obese (ie, have BMI ≥35 kg/m(2)). Globally, prevalence of morbid obesity was 0·64% (0·46-0·86) in men and 1·6% (1·3-1·9) in women. INTERPRETATION: If post-2000 trends continue, the probability of meeting the global obesity target is virtually zero. Rather, if these trends continue, by 2025, global obesity prevalence will reach 18% in men and surpass 21% in women; severe obesity will surpass 6% in men and 9% in women. Nonetheless, underweight remains prevalent in the world's poorest regions, especially in south Asia. FUNDING: Wellcome Trust, Grand Challenges Canada.","author_count":39,"contributors":["Di Cesare, Mariachiara","Bentham, James","Stevens, Gretchen A","Zhou, Bin","Danaei, Goodarz","Lu, Yuan","Bixby, Honor","Cowan, Melanie J","Riley, Leanne M","Hajifathalian, Kaveh","Fortunato, Léa","Taddei, Cristina","Bennett, James E","Ikeda, Nayu","Khang, Young-Ho","Kyobutungi, Catherine","Laxmaiah, Avula","Li, Yanping","Lin, Hsien-Ho","Miranda, J Jaime","Mostafa, Aya","Turley, Maria L","Paciorek, Christopher J","Gunter, Marc","Ezzati, Majid"],"cuid":["S03-087.74-027597"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corpau":["NCD Risk Factor Collaboration (NCD-RisC)","NCD Risk Factor Collaboration (NCD-RisC) (Kim Overvad; member)"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Bayes Theorem","Body Mass Index","Developed Countries","Developing countries","Female","Forecasting","Global Health","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Meta analysis","Models Statistical","Obesity","Prevalence","Research Support Non U S Gov t","Review","Thinness","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"trends in adult body mass index in 000000000200 countries from 000000001975 to 000000002014 a pooled analysis of 000000001698 population based measurement studies with 000000000019 000000000002 million participants","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16)30054-X","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-087.74-027597","identifier":[{"id":"27115820","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84962949589","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-cdc3194f-25eb-4056-8fa6-e0ba2123bda5","ku-1e4eabcf-c246-46c2-9c4e-8ec6eefa6621","sdu-ce0c7850-99a6-489c-9d70-7f2ca87e623b"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Bayes Theorem","Body Mass Index","Developed Countries","Developing Countries","Female","Forecasting","Global Health","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Meta-Analysis","Models, Statistical","Obesity","Prevalence","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Review","Thinness","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-6BB86EC86EB171AA7C717BCEF0B7C37E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(16)30054-X","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/162603016/NCD_Risk_Factor_Collaboration_Lancet_2016_Vol_387_1377_1396.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/124498092/Trends_in_adult_body_mass_index_in_200_countries_from_1975_to_2014.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"o