{"abstract":"Background.  Neisseria meningitidis is the cause of meningococcal bacteremia and meningitis, and nasopharyngeal colonization with this pathogen is common. The incidence of invasive disease is highest in infants, whereas adolescents more often are carriers. Altered regulation or dysfunction of the innate immune system may predispose to invasive meningococcal disease (IMD). In this study, we investigated the effect of genetic variation in the mannose-binding lectin gene, MBL2, and its promoter on susceptibility to IMD and IMD-associated mortality among children. Methods.  Children (<5 years) diagnosed during 1982-2007 with IMD and controls were identified through Danish national registries. DNA was obtained from the Danish Neonatal Screening Biobank. The associations between MBL2 diplotypes and IMD susceptibility and 30- and 90-day mortality were investigated using logistic regression analysis. Results.  We included 1351 children: 406 with meningitis, 272 with bacteremia, and 673 age- and sex-matched controls. Of the children studied, 1292 (96%) were successfully genotyped and assigned MBL2 diplotypes. The median age in IMD cases was 19.1 months (interquartile range [IQR], 8.8-32.2 months). Children with defective MBL2 diplotypes were not at higher risk for meningococcal meningitis than children with intermediate and normal diplotypes (odds ratio [OR] = 0.69; 95% confidence interval [CI], .47-1.02). Similar results were found for children with bacteremia and defective diplotypes (OR = 0.84; 95% CI, .53-1.32) as well as for all cases (OR = 0.75; 95% CI, .56-1.01). There was no association between MBL2 diplotypes and mortality. Conclusions.  Defective MBL2 diplotypes did not predict either an increased IMD susceptibility or mortality in a Danish population of children.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Lundbo, Lene F","Sørensen, Henrik Toft","Clausen, Louise N","Hollegaard, Mads V","Hougaard, David M","Konradsen, Helle B","Harboe, Zitta Barrella","Nørgaard, Mette","Benfield, Thomas"],"cuid":["S02-099.55-016890"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mannose binding lectin gene mbl000000000002 polymorphisms do not increase susceptibility to invasive meningococcal disease in a population of danish children","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofv127","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.55-016890","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4602025","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"26464842","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85014269105","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-39d42397-f4d5-42f6-966a-0364cb113347","ku-fdb4c288-52d0-4d61-962e-f2f5a5e5b070"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-DC463BD4226F99E6131F955978CA513C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ofid/ofv127","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lene F","key":"lundbopau","last":"Lundbo","locid":"68502507-00d3-4e13-ace6-6dd9e90d5e78","name":"Lundbo, Lene Fogt","name_org":"Lundbo, Lene F","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Toft","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"4d49d93a-4913-4223-ba59-272e717a845d","name":"Sørensen, Henrik Toft","orcid":"0000-0003-4299-7040","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Louise N","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","name":"Clausen, Louise N","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mads V","key":"hollegaardpau","last":"Hollegaard","locid":"a00937e5-e2ab-4374-871f-e86673c0eb1e","name":"Hollegaard, Mads V","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"David M","key":"hougaardpau","last":"Hougaard","name":"Hougaard, David M","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Helle B","key":"konradsenpau","last":"Konradsen","name":"Konradsen, Helle B","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Zitta Barrella","key":"harboepau","last":"Harboe","name":"Harboe, Zitta Barrella","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"nørgaardpau","last":"Nørgaard","locid":"3cb2ae19-c1b9-4dad-9928-adbd1c51b4b7","name":"Nørgaard, Mette","orcid":"0000-0001-6110-5891","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"benfieldpau","last":"Benfield","locid":"03fdbbba-4bc0-41aa-845b-81ba0bded9ba","name":"Benfield, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-0698-9385","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26464842"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/ofid/ofv127","issn":["23288957"],"issue":"4","journal":"Open Forum Infectious Diseases","pages":"1-6","paperid":"ofv127","status":"p","title":"Open Forum Infectious Diseases","title_org":"Open forum in Infectious Diseases","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85014269105"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-39d42397-f4d5-42f6-966a-0364cb113347","ku-fdb4c288-52d0-4d61-962e-f2f5a5e5b070"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Mannose-Binding Lectin Gene, MBL2, Polymorphisms Do Not Increase Susceptibility to Invasive Meningococcal Disease in a Population of Danish Children","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.55-016890","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Vitamin D status has been associated with cardiometabolic markers even in children, but the associations may be confounded by fat mass and physical activity behaviour. This study investigated associations between vitamin D status and cardiometabolic risk profile, as well as the impact of fat mass and physical activity in Danish 8-11-year-old children, using baseline data from 782 children participating in the Optimal well-being, development and health for Danish children through a healthy New Nordic Diet (OPUS) School Meal Study. We assessed vitamin D status as serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D (25(OH)D) and measured blood pressure, fasting plasma glucose, homoeostasis model of assessment-insulin resistance, plasma lipids, inflammatory markers, anthropometry and fat mass by dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry, and physical activity by 7 d accelerometry during August-November. Mean serum 25(OH)D was 60·8 (sd 18·7) nmol/l. Each 10 mmol/l 25(OH)D increase was associated with lower diastolic blood pressure (-0·3 mmHg, 95 % CI -0·6, -0·0) (P=0·02), total cholesterol (-0·07 mmol/l, 95 % CI -0·10, -0·05), LDL-cholesterol (-0·05 mmol/l, 95 % CI -0·08, -0·03), TAG (-0·02 mmol/l, 95 % CI -0·03, -0·01) (P≤0·001 for all lipids) and lower metabolic syndrome (MetS) score (P=0·01). Adjustment for fat mass index did not change the associations, but the association with blood pressure became borderline significant after adjustment for physical activity (P=0·06). In conclusion, vitamin D status was negatively associated with blood pressure, plasma lipids and a MetS score in Danish school children with low prevalence of vitamin D deficiency, and apart from blood pressure the associations were independent of body fat and physical activity. The potential underlying cause-effect relationship and possible long-term implications should be investigated in randomised controlled trials.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Petersen, Rikke A","Dalskov, Stine-Mathilde","Sørensen, Louise B","Hjorth, Mads F","Andersen, Rikke","Tetens, Inge","Krarup, Henrik","Ritz, Christian","Astrup, Arne","Michaelsen, Kim F","Mølgaard, Christian","Damsgaard, Camilla T"],"cuid":["S04-096.43-010842"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"keyword":["25 OH D 25 hydroxyvitamin D","Cardiometabolic health","Children","Nutrition","PTH parathyroid hormone","Vitamin D"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-NDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCL"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-NDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-UC-UCL"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"vitamin d status is associated with cardiometabolic markers in 000000000008 000000000011 year old children independently of body fat and physical activity","src":["aau","dtu","ku","ucl"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-UC-UCL"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0007114515003372","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-096.43-010842","identifier":[{"id":"WOS:000364957900012","type":"wos"},{"id":"84945467496","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26382732","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-e30da5bd-2b6a-4e58-afe9-96a31e668340","dtu-2c6f3212-c629-4dda-b418-399eba457ba9","ku-3c4b4422-f7b6-41d4-ae47-133beac704f0","ucl-bde49b74-8dd6-4812-b4f7-b55bd5e1d652"],"keyword":["25(OH)D 25-hydroxyvitamin D","Cardiometabolic health","Children","FMI fat mass index","HOMA-IR homoeostasis model of assessment of insulin resistance","MVPA moderate-to-vigorous physical activity","MetS metabolic syndrome","PTH parathyroid hormone","Vitamin D","nutrition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-0CEC107359ED38F6200CEA4C09795549"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","typ