{"abstract":"This study aims to identify key miRNAs in circulation, which predict ongoing beta-cell destruction and regeneration in children with newly diagnosed Type 1 Diabetes (T1D). We compared expression level of sera miRNAs from new onset T1D children and age-matched healthy controls and related the miRNAs expression levels to beta-cell function and glycaemic control. Global miRNA sequencing analyses were performed on sera pools from two T1D cohorts (n = 275 and 129, resp.) and one control group (n = 151). We identified twelve upregulated human miRNAs in T1D patients (miR-152, miR-30a-5p, miR-181a, miR-24, miR-148a, miR-210, miR-27a, miR-29a, miR-26a, miR-27b, miR-25, miR-200a); several of these miRNAs were linked to apoptosis and beta-cell networks. Furthermore, we identified miR-25 as negatively associated with residual beta-cell function (est.: -0.12, P = 0.0037), and positively associated with glycaemic control (HbA1c) (est.: 0.11, P = 0.0035) 3 months after onset. In conclusion this study demonstrates that miR-25 might be a \"tissue-specific\" miRNA for glycaemic control 3 months after diagnosis in new onset T1D children and therefore supports the role of circulating miRNAs as predictive biomarkers for tissue physiopathology and potential intervention targets.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Nielsen, Lotte B.","Wang, Cheng","Sorensen, Kaspar","Bang-Berthelsen, Claus Heiner","Hansen, Lars","Andersen, Marie-Louise M.","Hougaard, Philip","Juul, Anders Christian","Zhang, Chen-Yu","Pociot, Flemming","Mortensen, Henrik B."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798183"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adolescent","Age","Apoptosis","Article","Biological Markers","Biological marker","Biomarkers","Blood","Blood Glucose","Cancer detection","Case Control Studies","Case control study","Child","Cohort Studies","Cohort analysis","Controlled study","Cytology","DNA sequence","Denmark","Diabetes Mellitus Type 1","Disease Progression","Disease course","Endocrinology","Endocrinology Diabetes and Metabolism","Expression","Female","Glycemic control","Hemoglobin A1c","Human","Humans","Hyperglycemia","Insulin Secreting Cells","Insulin dependent diabetes mellitus","Internal Medicine","Male","Medicine","Medicine all","Metabolism","MicroRNA 29a","MicroRNAs","Music","Pathophysiology","Protein expression","Protein function","R","RNA sequence","Remission","Remission Induction","Sequence Analysis DNA","Serum","Unclassified drug","Up Regulation","major clinical study","microRNA","microRNA 148a","priority journal","β cell Function"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"circulating levels of microrna from children with newly diagnosed type 000000000001 diabetes and healthy controls evidence that mir 000000000025 associates to residual beta cell function and glycaemic control during disease progression evidence that mir 000000000025 associates to residual beta cell function and glycaemic control during disease progression","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/127347119/Circulating_levels_of_microRNA_from_children_with_newly_diagnosed_type_1_diabetes_and_healthy_controls_evidence_that_miR_25_associates_to_residual_beta_cell_function_and_glycaemic_control_during_disease_progression.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798183","identifier":[{"id":"84864981566","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-8d03c0d0-bbf7-4c83-9773-a02da6d4145e"],"keyword":["AGE","BIOMARKERS","Biological Markers","Blood Glucose","CANCER-DETECTION","Case-Control Studies","Cohort Studies","DNA sequence","DOAJ:Health Sciences","DOAJ:Internal medicine","DOAJ:Medicine (General)","Denmark","Diab